      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WHICH DOCTOR?</head>
        <p>WHICH DOCTOR?</p>
        <p>Board Of Guardians Rescind A</p>
        <p>Resolution.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER ADVERTISEMENTI Spirit Photography. This, which is to be the Abject of a lecture in the Druids' Hall on Tuesday, came under my notice as a young reporter. I had to call upon a Spiritualist for material for a local news paragraph respecting a meeting at which he had figured. Comparatively inexperienced as I was, he struck me as a most unusual sort of mortal. He had been in America, and, in the hearing of his wife, who remained quite unmoved, he confessed that be had returned to 'the old home a convinced Free Lover. Be- sides there and then expounding to me the doctrines of that strange sect, he thrust into my hand a sheaf of American Spiritualist papers, and—to get back to the point=a weird picture which he assured me was the portrait of a spirit friend photographed at a seance.</p>
        <p>Discussion as to the appointment of a district medical officer for No. 3 District, and public vaccinator for the Rixton district, following the death of Dr. Sephton, -is not yet ended by the Board of Guardians. On three successive occasions the Board have talked over the matter. Until Wednesday it had been decided that any medical practitioner outside of the Union area should not be allowed to compete for the post. But on Wednesday, Mr. Furness moved the rescinding of this rule, as otherwise it would ,mean the exclusion of the son of the late Dr. Sephton from participating in the competition. When he (Mr. Furness) consented to the rule,at the last meeting he was under the impression that Dr. Sephton would have been in the running, but he found soon afterwards that it mould prevent that doctor applying, as he was just over the border. Mr. Furness recalled that the Finance Committee, a few weeks ago, nominated Dr. Sephton for the vacancy, but this tad ,been defeated by the Board. Ile could not recollect an instance where a resolution which had only required formal sanction as that had done, had been de- feated before. If cases like that occurred it only meant that there was no finality about what was done, and it was foreign to business principles. On the basis of convenience, he thought that Dr. Sephton was the gentleman who ought to be appointed. Mr. E. Dennett seconded. The Doctors' Qualifications.</p>
        <p>Ship Canal "Spec." The declaration of a Manchester Ship Canal " divi.," as set forth in last week's sketch in these columns, sent me back in thought to tho incept.on of the *treat scheme. At that Tw 6,4 I was in a busy industrial district in which Co- operative Societies 'abounded, and the working men directing these supported the project with remarkable enthusiasm. Although they quite recognised that there was no early prospect of the particular kind of return on their invest- nients now happily achieved, they freely placed surplus capital at the disposal of the enter- prising promoters, and arranged forthwith to write off a certain percentage. This process they continued until gradually the whole came to be treated as lost. Now that the tide has turned in the shareholders' favour, the co- operators in question—who have meanwhile benefited in other ways as they anticipated— will complacently pat each other on the back, amid the congratulations of all concerned.</p>
        <p>Miss Constance Broadbent said she thought that the Board ought only to consider the quali- fications of the gentlemen. She thought also that the business of the appointments of the medical officer ought to have been on the agenda of the Finance Committee's meetings, as many Guardians did not understand that it was coming up when it did. Supporting the motion, Mr. W. J. Shaw re- marked that if they were going to have the whole of the appointments confined to men living within their particular area, they were acting unwisely. They went elsewhere for their clerk, and they had got a good one, too. (Hear, hear.) Mr. A. Brooks said that Mr. Furness must have heard Mr. Mather remark at the last meeting that the passing of the resolution he wanted rescinded would mean that Dr. Sephton would be out of the running. Mr. Furness remarked that he did not hear Mr. Mather say anything of the kind. - - -</p>
        <p>Intelligent Cheese. Last week's "Home Notes" contained several cheese recipes, and in reading them I was re- minded of a story told me many years ago of a Halifax tradesman—and, probably enough of others elsewhere. It Was to the effect that an elderly man from a neighbouring village, noted ,for driving a hard bargain, called at a shop in the town mentioned, and bought at 4d. per pound a lump of %heese which had become so " lively " that the dealer had almost despaired of being able to get rid of it at any price. The purchaser, explaining that he had a little other business, asked if he might leave the cheese until his return, upon which the vendor made the facetious suggestion, "Clap it daan aghtside; it'll be hooam.az sooin's thee." Unfortunate Initials.</p>
        <p>Neither could Mr. Mather recollect whether he made that remark before or after the resolution had been passed. Mr. J. Ball also considered that Dr. Sephton</p>
        <p>should be able to compete. His appointment would be the most pleasant to the poor, and there would be easier access to him than to any other doctor. For years the poor had been going to the same surgery. "Why not leave a man in his own area? I am told every doctor has quite as much work as he can do in his own district," Mr. Ball concluded.</p>
        <p>The "Farm and Garden" item respecting the intentions of the Cheshire " A.S." brought irresistibly to mind the case of one whom I will call Aminadab Samson Sweetbread—not be- cause that vies his name, but because it serves to indicate his initials. He was a member of a Board of Guardians whose meetings I attended in the days :of auld lang sync. The name of each township in the Union was shown on a brass plate on the upper inner arch of the respective representatives' chairs. Once, while contemplating this little work of art, the well- to-do Mr. Aminadab had a brilliant inspiration, and he forthwith wrote out an order for his dining-room chairs at home to be ornamented in like manner with his initials. It was not until the completion of the job and the delivery of the "goods" that the owner awakened to the fact that the,intended adornment read "A.S.S." Of course, it was a case of "As you were" so far as those chairs were concerned. That Reminds Me.</p>
        <p>We have all heard many amusing tales of Wilberforce, Bishop of Oxford, and, as a "Son of the Manse" whom I know has .noted in his weekly "Ecclesiastical Table-talk," Canon Adderly, of Birmingham, reproduces several in an interesting volume of reminiscences lately issued. The foregoing in turn reminds me of one of thesp in which "Soapy Sam" unmistak- ably scored at the expense of a would-be wit. While addressing a meeting, the Bishop coughed or cleared his throat. "Try Thorley's food for cattle," broke in the inevitable "voice." "Thank you," said the Bishop, "it may be good for asses, but it does not suit Samuel Oxon." " Ashton's Tonic."</p>
        <p>Would I might have enjoyed the advantage of• hearing, and not only of reading a su4mmary of, ' last week's Literary and Philosophical lecture. As to the wisdom of laughter, does not Solomon himself allow that "there is a time to laugh" as well as a time to mourn? To be sure, there are different kinds of laughter. Everybody is familiar with Goldsmith's line, "The loud, laugh that spoke the vacant mind"; and Steele, who wrote much on this subject, treated of it under the heads: the dimplers, the smilers, the laughers, the grinners, the horse-laughers. Another writer shows still more precisely that there is "more variety in laughing than in almost any other human operation," enlarging on giggles, cantious cachinnations, simpers, guffaws, and the silver-ringing music-laugh. Carlyle has something on similar lines, beginning by describing laughter as "the cipher key where- with wo decipher the whole man "—really a paraphrase of Steel, which I should never have suspected in the Chelsea sage if Ihad not read both. However, of what Mr. Ashton discoursed so ably and I now echo faintly this is the sum: there is indeed wisdom in a laugh, and, if one may so far presume, Martial was :undoubtedly right in saying "Laugh if you are wise.",</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>"A Breach of Faith."</head>
        <p>"A Breach of Faith."</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said he had been very much surprised at Mr. Furness's motion, be- cause the resolution was most clearly: under stood at the last meeting. He thought Mr. Furness was committing a breach of faith; afrl hoped he would withdraw. Mr. J. Billing also thought Mr. Furness might withdraw. Mr. Thomas Dale said that members asking the Board to rescind resolutions it had only just come to was like playing at business and, to say the least, was very undignified. In desiring the appointment of Dr. Bennett he betieved he was acting in the best interests of the Oistriet. He did not think they had any right to give the public the idea that they were Ma s.;ng Li-pcint- ments family heirlooms. Throughout the meeting there scemel to be a feeling that the whole trouble was cause.] through the Finance Committee's rer.:ontraett fa- tion of Dr. Sephton not having been statcd the Board's agenda, with the resat that mem- bers did not know the matter was to be co.:. sidered. Several members declared that had they known it was going to be discussed they would have been present. The Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley), therefore, ex- plained that it was the custom of the Board that unless they were particularly requested by a member, no details of the business of the com- mittees' should be placed on the Board's, agenda. Mr. Furness's resolution was carried by 15 votes to three, and the Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) observed that they now started with a clean slate, but could not make the appoint- ment immediately. It was then decided to advertise the vacancy without any territorial restriction being made. The letters of the doctors who had replied to the last advertisement were, therefore, allowed to lie on the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Tomnly Indignant.</head>
        <p>Tomnly Indignant.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant; Nobody hurt? Then what the deuce are you kicking up such a row for?" Tommy: "Well,, sir, look at the mess they bloomin"uns 'ave made in the trench just after I've swep; it up !"—" Punch."</p>
        <p>F' 0 Ft</p>
        <p>Best House Coal</p>
