      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>!.=</p>
        <p>JOT:TINS BY AY OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Clinching a Bargain.</p>
        <p>I suppose that piece of ham that a dirty old woman last week convicted here of stealing it had messed about would be of no use to any- body. Still, I don't know. When I was a youngster I had pointed out to me a chimney- sweep who had gone into a cookshop for a pennyworth of pudding, and, on a good "square of Yorkshire" being passed over the counter to him, slapped it with his sooty hand, exclaiming "Call that a penn'oth? Aw'll yo a haweny for't !" Needless to add, he got the coveted "tit-bit" at the substantial reduction in price proposed by himself. Stone Deaf Indeed.</p>
        <p>The reference in the account of the Winwick Hospital concert to the extremely deaf old lady whom the motor's hum reminded of a bee sent me to a volume in my bookcase I had not opened for awhile. - For 30 years I have possessed. not only the inimitable "Tom's 1' complete poems in two books (comic and serious), but also four volumes of "Hood's Own," in one of which is represented a malicious person dis- charging a big blundcrbuss close behind an aged woman who is most decidedly "hard of hear- ing;" lier only response. being a mild "Come in." Sometimes Serviceable.</p>
        <p>A certain reverend canon ivho was somewhat similarly afflicted, was once made chairman of one of the old School Boards whosemeetings I at- tended. I had known him as a young curate in another parish, and was on sufficiently familiar terms to chaff him about his election. "Why did you accept?" I urged, "you're so very deaf, you know." " Ah, my dear fellow," replied the genial cleric, "that is sometimes an advantage in a chairman. Whenever any- thing disagreeable is said, you may be sure I shall not hear that." The ensuing lessons in tact were fine. When a noisy and troublesome member vip:orously interposed some frivolous objection, and in demonstrative fashion pro- ceeded to hammer it home, the benign chair- man, whose childlike and bland smile never came off, patiently awaited the end of the storm, and then, pretending to have entirely misunder- stood its purport, quietly proceeded, with the business in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 26, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 26, 1916.</p>
        <p>FLAR P</p>
        <p>First Local Police Lighting</p>
        <p>Prosecution.</p>
        <p>SIXTY-THREE GLOBES</p>
        <p>BURNING.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable's warnings to those in- habitants. of Warrington who have disregarded their obligations under the new Lighting Order have been carried into effect. The first prosecution was heard at the Police Court on Thursday, before Mr. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), Mr. A. J. Jolley and Dr. Naden, when the promotors of a Saturday night fair on the market ground, Queen-street, were sum- moned for failing to reduce the lights to a minimum intensity, consistent with safety, as required by the new Lighting Order. In view of the fact that this was the first case heard at the Court, the magistrates took a lenient view and a "light " penalty was im- posed of 60s. each on the four defendants. The Chairman pointed out that tie magistrates were determined to support the authorities in enforcing the regulations, and offenders would not get off so lightly in the future. The defendants 'fere James Croydon, " round- about " proprietor, 6, Market-place; Arthur Duck, Richard Chadwick and Gilbert Chadwick, "Aunt Sally" and " Hoop-la " stall- holders. They were represented by Mr. T. S.</p>
        <p>Steel. The facts as related by the Chief Constable were that on Saturday night Sergeant Ken- wright and P.C. Cameron visited the fair ground in Queen-street. They noticed that the "round- abouts," owned by the defendant Croydon, were very brilliantly lighted. Tht officers drew the attention of the proprietor to this fact, and pointed out that he was committing an offence under the lighting regulations. One large arc ,lamp had been extinguished, but no fewer than sixty-three small electric globes Were kept alight. Defendant was warned that it was im- possible for the lights to remain in such a con- dition without being a source of danger, but he declined to put them out. In regard to the offence committed by Duck, the officers observed that he had a large in- candeScent light on each of his two stalls, which was emitting brilliant rays. In no sense conk! the lights be termed as complying with t'le regu lations. On the stalls of Richard Chadwu,k there were two large oil lamps partially shaded, but the covering over them was not sufficient ic. prevent the rays of light from being seen through the canvas at the top of the still. The four " hoop-la " stalls of Gilbert Chadwick were lit up by incandescent lamps, they limre pqr- tially covered, but not sufficiently to nrw..-cr.t a, brilliant light from shining outwards on the ground. Police Pestered.</p>
        <p>It would be a serious matter if the police were to be pestered in that way by people who de- clined to comply with the Order, declared the Chief Constable. In other areas summonses had been issued and heavy penalties inflicted. • He hoped after hearing the evidence the magis- trates would make an example of these men, so that no repetition would take place in the town. Infringements had taken place and other pro- ceedings were being considered under the Order. Sergeant Kenwright gave evidence in support of the Chief Constable's statement. Mr. Steel: Did you know that the lights on the stalls had been reduced from 12 to 4?—Yes. The lights were shaded with a piece of tin?— Yes, but the shades were not low enough. With regard to the " roundabouts " owned by Mr. Croydon, had he got a green curtain round to prevent, lights from showing F—Yes. Were there Some coloured globes amongst the sixty-three lights ?—Yes, but twenty-seven were not coloured globes, and gave a bright light. Mr. Steel: The sort of thing you see on a tramcar? The Chief Constable: They don't have 63 on the tramcars. For the defence, Mr. Steel contended , that the men had no desire to evade the regulations, but, on the contrary, they were desirous of assisting the police as far as possible in carry- ing out these most difficult regulations. The men had got to earn a livelihoo4, and during, the winter they were only able to make a few shillings once a week towards their expenges. On Saturday night, in order to assist the authorities, as far as possible, eight of the lights were extinguished and the remainder were shaded. If there was anything the police could suggest for the further reduction of the lighting, and still enable the defendants to carry on their business, they were quite willing to adopt it. The regulations were quite a new thing, and, as one walked around the town, it was notiCe- able that many people had not yet appreciated what their duty was under the Order. The Chief Constable: The only way to make it a warning to others is to inflict a heavy penalty. The defendants having set the regula- tions at defiance, ought to be severely punished. They were warned a week previously, and when their attention was drawn to the lights on the night in question they elected to go on. Mr. Steel: Although there have been prosecu- tions in other parts of the country, the lighting regulations had been in operation for a consider- able time. The Chief Constable: No, no! Under the same Order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>Showmen's Livelihood.</head>
        <p>Showmen's Livelihood.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: The law did not contemplate that such men should be deprived of their means of livelihood. The Chief Constable: The law thinks of the safety of the realm. Mr. Steel: The defendants did all that was possible. The coloured lamps were used to pre- vent showing a big light. The Chief Constable: Only for ornamental purposes. The Chairman pointed out that the prosecu- tions were not taken with the object of making money out of the defendants, but to enforce the regulations. Some attempt had been made to meet the requirements, but they were not sufficient. It must be understood that the leniency extended in this, the first case, was not to be taken that offenders would be treated so lightly in the future. The magistrates were determined to support the authorities in enforc- ing the regulations. He had some misgivings whether they were not being too lenient in fining each defendant 60s. The next time.it would be pounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>EXPERIMENTS WITH</head>
        <p>EXPERIMENTS WITH</p>
        <p>COUGII CURE</p>
        <p>By a London Scientist.</p>
        <p>Chas. Hyatt-Woolf, Esq., F.R.P.S., F.R.S.L., Editor of • Popular Science Siftings, writes as follows in his book, Truths about things we live on and daily use:—" With a view to discovering a form of medieine needed to diminish conges- tion, aid expectoration, and soothe the respira- tory track, I experimented in the laboratory with Veno's Lightning Cough Cure and applied it in practice. I found this remedy Contained a variety of matters capable of affording relief in all those eases where coughing is a symptom. Not only this, but it is a distinct nervis tonic, ' and is not only applicable with advantage in cases of bronchial coughs, but also for stomach coughs. It would likewise have a certain value in consumption, it showed distinct ability to abate feverish symptoms, and, .in all bases to which I applied it, the influence' of Veno's Lightning Cough Cure was most marked. This Cough Cure is very nicely com- pounded, so much so that it is even pleasant to take, its delightful flavour commending it to the most. fastidious patient. It contains no opiate or anything that could 'effect harm!' Take 'Veno's Lightning Cough. Cure for all .diseases of. chest, throat, and lungs, in young or old. Prices: 111 d., 113, and 31-, of-all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Leather Trade Active.</head>
        <p>Leather Trade Active.</p>
        <p>The output in this district is easily main- tained at very full figures, says the Warrington correspondent of the "Leather Trades Review,-7 the extent of which is bounded only by the quantity of leather offered, and, whilst for the present the latter is on the small stde, there are some indications that beforc :on; we may be getting tanning material it ore freely, and this should induce an expansion in the produc- tion of leather. Prices are hardening all round, advances are being made gradually first on one line and then on another, so that whilst' one cannot speak of a definite general increase of price, yet in the, course of a week practically , every class stands at a higher figure, and in some cases the increase is quite considerable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>EC,OI,4O)Pn. PA EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>EC,OI,4O)Pn. PA EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley Enumerates</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Regrettable Savings Effected.</head>
        <p>Regrettable Savings Effected.</p>
        <p>2s. I N THE L RATE TO BE ASKED FOR.</p>
        <p>That a well-directed effort has been made to economise in expenditure on education was insisted upon by Mr. A. J. Jolley when he presented the estimates to the monthly meeting of the Education Committee on Monday even- ing, although the amount the Town- Council is asked to provide totals £29,000, tile same as</p>
        <p>last year. Mr. Jolley said that during the financial year which was now drawing to a close the strictest attention had been given by Mr. J. M. Murray (director of education) and the various com- mittees in trying, as far as possible, to economise in expenditure. We heard a great deal about economy now, and, in his opinion,' in one or two directions the desire to economise had been carried a little too far, but there had been several savings effected. In regard to the savings on schools the num- ber of men who had enlisted had been replaced mostly by women, but not all of them had been replaced. As far as possible a transference of scholars had been made from one school to another in order to save a teacher. Supposing that by removing twenty scholars from one school to another a teacher could be saved,</p>
        <p>that had been done. Similarly with regard to books, apparatus, furniture and repairs, scrupulous attention had been given to every detail in a manner which ought to satisfy the hardiest economist. One of the fruits of that had been that they would close the financial year with a consider- ably larger balance than the amount estimated</p>
        <p>for last year. A Larger Balance Than Expected. However, we had lost grants in several direc- tions, amounting approximately to £6OO, which, under ndrmal conditions, we should have had. Yet, in spite of that, the balance was larger than anticipated, due mainly to the fact that the Oakwood-avenue School had not been opened. No teachers' or caretaker's salaries had had to be paid there, and, roughly, £OOO had been saved by its non-opening. Whereas they had estimated to provide £4OO for the Grammar School, the deficiency there had been small, and, by that circumstance, £313 had been saved. Through the entry into military service of some elementary teachers and the rearrange- ment of the staffs, .C 272 12s. had been saved. There had been a saving of £l7O under the • heading of Bursars- and Scholarships. - That, in one sense, was to be regretted, of course. They would have rejoiced had they had the students to receive the money and make use of the bursars and scholarships Which the ratepayers had so generously provided. • £lOO had been saved on furniture, £lBO on income tax on loans, and £2O on travelling expenses. Regarding the last-named, certain visits had been put off this year, but it was not desirable that these should be discontinued altogether. The lamented death of Mr. Robson, .of the Secondary School, had preceded the appoint- ment of a successor at a salary at the bottom of the scale, and thus, with the saving of one teacher, there had been a saving of .2150. On the Art School £26 had .been saved owing to the non-replacement of a pupil teacher. A Regrettable Silver Lining.</p>
        <p>On the technical and evening classes there' had been a saving of DIN. "That is regret- table,' commented Mr. Jolley, "but there it is! I suppose there is a silver lining to every cloud, and fh.ough there is a £420 lining to this cloud it is not altogether satisfactory." (Hear, hear.) In the estimates in all there would be a deficiency of £31,137, put the Finance Com- mittee had decided that the Town Council should be asked for £29,000. Some might regard this as bad finance. In normal times he him- self would have hesitated to agree to the sugges- tion, but he thought that in the prevailing abnormal condition of things they might safely take this line. He and .11irocolleagues on the committee approved and tecommended it. It meant that the Council was being asked to pro- vide a 2s. rate for the purpose of educationo If they had not had a satisfactory balance it would have meant approximately a 2s. 2d. rate. In the introductory remarks of his speech Mr. Jolley explained that "generally the estimates had been almost accurate. It always gave him great pleasure to pay his tribute to the Director of Education and the Borough Treasurer ane..• their staffs, who had done their work very well. The expenditure at the end of last year was £6O less than the estimated ex- penditure, and the actual Government grants were £l7O more than had been estimated. :When they thought of the large figures they were deal- ing with they would see what was involved in that: the expenditure was estimated so closely that it was within one-tenth of one per cent. of the amount expected. That was very credit- able to all who had had anything to do with it. (Hear, hear.). Salaries and War Bonus.</p>
        <p>The salaries of teachers in elementary schools showed an increase of £378 10s. (the total amount being £36,645), but those of teachers in Secondary .ind evening schools showed a de- crease of £3lO (the total amount being £4,850). There was, therefore, a net increase under that head of £69. The actual net increase of salaries as such was considerably less than that, but through the operation of resolutions passed by , the Totorn Council a war bonus was paid to some of thp lower paid teachers, as it was paid to all persons employed by the Corporation whose salaries were below a certain figure. There had been an increase of £475 in the estimated ex- penditure on fuel, light and cleaning. Half of that was due to the increased cost of labour, in. eluding a war bonus, and provision was also made for the opening of the Oakwood-avenue Council School. It was anticipated that the school would be ready for occupation after the summer holidays, and there would be payment in respect of caretaker's wages, fuel, &amp;c. The cost of fuel had, of course, increased. An advance of £196 on the amount estimated for rents, rates and taxes was also partly due to the erection of the Oakwood-avenue School. An increase in sinking fund was accounted for by the foct that payments with respect to the loan for the Oakwood-avenue School would come into the account in the coming financial year. So far as the total expenditure was con- cerned there were increases amounting to £l,lll, but against them had to be set decreases of £377, leaving a net increase of £734.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Government Grants Disappointing.</head>
        <p>Government Grants Disappointing.</p>
        <p>They were losing a little in grants. Twelve months ago they anticipated that the proposed Government grant to necessitous authorities would yield them £5OO. In normal times they would have received that sum, but when the Year began they were informed that no higher i grants would be given under that head than had been paid in the previous year, which meant that instead of receiving £5OO they would get ,only .428.. They had lost £142 on the annual grant and £95 on the fee grant, so that approxi- mately the loss in grants was £6OO. They had gained a little on the medical inspection grant, and this was a very satisfactory feature. There was one form of gain which some of them looked upon as being a loss, Secondary School and bursars' grants being down to the extent of £63. Evening and art school grants were £l2O down. Evening school expenses were also lower, and they would all regret that it was so. Their even- • ing school work had been one. of the most encouraging branches of educational: activity in Warrington; they had stood very 'high, both in quantity and -quality. Since the .outbreak of war, however, many of the senior 'students had gone to. serve their country loyally. Many who were not attached to the military forces had been working on munitions, and consequently a number of the senior classes had been dropped. On the receipts as a whole there was a. net decrease of °UN:. Mr. E. .Plinston seconded the 'passing of the ininittes, which were then approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Gratitude.</head>
        <p>Gratitude.</p>
        <p>The following letter has been reA%-od IT the matron of an Indian hospital:— "Dear and Fair Madam,--l• hi.‘,3 much pleaiiure to inform you that my dcurly un- fortunate wife will be no longer un.l,-r y. ur care, she having left this world for the next on the 27th ult. For your help in this matter I shall over remain grateful.—Yours reverently ." " Punch.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SUCCESS t THE POULTRY</head>
        <p>SUCCESS t THE POULTRY</p>
        <p>YARD.</p>
        <p>When the area available for the fowls is extremely limited, it is an excellent plan to divide the plot of ground into two equal parts, and place the house in the middle. One year the bird d are allowed the run of one half, while in the other some quickly-growing vegetables are planted. The following year the order is changed, and the half that was cultivated the previous year is used for the fowls, and the other half is planted. It is advisable to plant grass seeds rather than cabbage or such like garden produce, as this affords the fowls a grass run instead of an earthen one for the first month or two. In such a manner quits a small piece of land can accommodate a fairly large number of fowls without the least danger of the soil becoming tainted. It is a good plan, too, to plant a few fruit trees or bushes in the run, as not only do they absorb some manure, but after a few years they afford excellent shelter from wind and sun.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>COOLING EGGS WHILE HATCHING.</head>
        <p>COOLING EGGS WHILE HATCHING.</p>
        <p>A very common mistake in artificial incuba- tion is made with regard to the daily cooling of the eggs. It is seldom that operators allow sufficient time for this, which can only be accounted for by the fact that it is usually be- lieved to be harmful. It must be remembered that an egg takes a long time to lose its heat, and therefore if only allowed to cool for a few minutes it has very little effect. Again, the length of time' given must depend on the tem- perature of the room, for the eggs lose their heat more rapidly in a cold room than in a warm one. Taking the average temperature of GO degs., the eggs should be cooled for twenty minutes in the morning and fifteen minutes in j the evening. The difference in length is sug- gested, for usually a longer time elapses be- tween the night and morning cooling than be- tween the morning J and evening. If the tem- perature in the room is only 50 degs., then fifteen minutes in the morning and twelve minutes in the evening is sufficient; if the room is only 40 degs., then twelve minutes and ten minutes is ample. If, on the contrary, the room is 70 degs., twenty-five minutes should be allowed in the morning and twenty minutes at night, while if it be 80 degs., then thirty-five minutes and thirty minutes respectively should be allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SYSTEM REQUIRED.</head>
        <p>SYSTEM REQUIRED.</p>
        <p>In the present time of strife and trouble, when an increased yield in eggs is so important, it behoves everyone interested in 'the poultry industry, and the farmer especially, to improve his stock with this end in view. We find that the majority of farmers, even those who pay but small attention to poultry, secure new male birds each year, but they invariably choose a different breed, with the only possible result that the fowls retain their mongrel appearance. When new males of the same variety are in- troduced every year the members of the poultry yard rapidly assume the distinct characteristics, both in outward type and in economic quali- ties. Excellent as this method is it may be very considerably enhanced, and the improve- ment more pronounced in a much shorter time, if greater care is exercised at the commence- ment in selecting the hens or pullets that are to be mated with the first newly-imported male bird. The hens are frequently chosen irre- spective of breed, and they are different in every characteristic from the cock. The result is that a much longer period is needed before improvement can be brought about. Especially does this. apply to the newer varieties of poul- try, since in many of the later established varieties the type is, not sufficiently fixed to have much influence over the' progeny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CHOICE OF THE HENS.</head>
        <p>THE CHOICE OF THE HENS.</p>
        <p>This trouble may easily be obviated, however,' by selecting hens as nearly alike in build and character to the male as possible, and if this is done, even in the first year distinct improva- ment is noticed. The foregoing plan is very simply carried out, the only extra labour en- tailed being the erection of a small pen in some convenient place where the breeding stock can be kept., When all the eggs that are intenled for sitting have been obtained, the cccuexats of the pen can be restored to frediem, and allowed to return to their usual quarer4 It will thus be seen that improvement in the existing stock may easily be eecurel and min- tamed at a very small outlay. Reliable birds can be readily obtained at a moderate cost from breeders who are conducting their opera- tions on purely • utility lines. Careful attention to the selection of the best individual layers from the flock year by year undoubtedly helps to establish a good laying strain, but all the advantage that accrues from breeding from hens kurpvl to possess remarkable laying powers is neutralised if the male is not also from a good ,laying source. The male possesses a much greater influence than is usually supposed over the egg producing powers of his progeny. It will thus be seen how important it is to secure a male bird from a good laying strain. • A SMALLHOLDERS' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>In Denmark, where so many sraallholdings are to be found, and the tendency is to increase the number, it is natural that there should have been established some special schools for small tenants and their wives. One of the schools is situated by Koerehave, one and a</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>BROWN LEGHORN EGGS, Lord-Frost's 280-egg strain, 5/- sitting; unfertiles replaced. —Hawkins, Ninewells, Coleford, Gloucester.</p>
        <p>ING"*—White Leghorn, White Wyandotte, Norman aunt's competition laying strain, 4/- sitting. Buff Orpington, Golding's direct, 5/- sit- ting. Beet Pen all winners, 10/- sitting.. Guaran- teed fertile or replaced.—Bennett, Pelham-road, Retford.</p>
        <p>QYIVING •EGGS from world's champion 1,7 record layers; White Wyandotte, Rie's direct 28d erng strain, 5/- dozen. carriage paid.— Rose, The Manor Maplestead, Itaistead, Essex,</p>
        <p>- WHITE LEGHORN§, White Wyandottes (Barron's 245/272 s"g strains). direct. Splendid winter layers. Eggs. 3/6 per dozen.—D. Alty, jun., Westhead, Ormskirk. _ .</p>
        <p>wHEYS can now supply 'Ga. corn— • Eggs from a " Barron ' Cock and Hen at 4/- per sitting, two sittings 7/- : unfertile replaced once.—Leftwich Greer!, Northwich. 234 Egg Strain Black Leghorns.ainglecomb; also rosecomb: 4/- dozen.—Wiloox (Ancona specialist), Toll Bar, Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>"- • r flail EGGS from White Wyandotte!' 1/11 during first 5 months last year. Mated this season to Tom Barron's,Metealfs,Pierson Web- ber's best Birds. Also Cam's and Barron's White Leghorns, and Peirson Webber's Rhode Island Reds. Eggs, 5/- sitting; Chicks, c.ay old, February, March or April delivery from 10/- dozen.—Alton Poultry Farm, 40, Gravelly Hill North, Birming- ham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>half miles from the town of Ringsted. Beside the school buildings is to be found a model farmyard, and there is, naturally, a poultry farm, where the scholars can learn the practi- cal work. Poultry-breeding, as well as 'book- keeping and horticulture. is included in the course of instruction. The place is capitally equippeti, and the appliances are quite up to , date. The main poultry-house is situated in a small forest, where the fowls in hot weather can get plenty of shade. The house is built in two floors. On the first floor is the sleeping room, and by means of a ladder the fowls can very easily reach the second floor, where the scratching shed is to be found, and where there is plenty of straw irr autumn and winter time. Formerly different breeds of poultry were kept, but now they only breed White Leghorns. This is to suggest that every smallholder should keep one of the most common economical breeds. Every .year sixty Pullets* are trap- nested.—" Middlesex," in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SENTRY'S " CHALLENGE.</head>
        <p>A SENTRY'S " CHALLENGE.</p>
        <p>Druiiken Soldier Stopped Near</p>
        <p>Canal Bridge.</p>
        <p>AFTERWARDS FOUND DROWNED."</p>
        <p>"Why should we mourn departed friends, Or shake at death's alarm? Death is but the servant Jesus sends To call them to His arms."</p>
        <p>The above lines were found written in the water-eoaked pocket-book of Private Henry Tipping, of the 2/4th Loyal North Lancashire Regiment, stationed at Northwich-road Bridge on the Ship Canal, whose body was recovered from the Ship Canal, imar the Stag Bridge, Walton, on Monday afternoon. At the inquest held by Mr. 11. C. Yates on Wednesday, at the Stockton Heath Police Station, a verdict of "Found drdwned" was returned, there being no evidence to show how Tipping got into the water. Margaret Amelia Tipping, the wife of the deceased, residing at 34, East Cliffe, Lytham, Blackpool, said her husband was 53 years of age. In December, 1914, he left home to rejoin the Arniy. Previously he had 'been employed as a labourer at the Lythain Council Gas Works. She last saw him alive on January 24th, when he came home on a few days' leave. He was then in good health, which he usually enjoyed, except ler slight attacks of bronchitis. Ile had previously served eight years in the Army, and four on the Reserve. He was a fairly steady man and usually took drink in moderation. Sergeant-Major Hill, of No. 2 Company, Loyal North Lancashire Regiment, quartered at Warrington, identified the body as that of Private Tipping, who belonged to the same company as witness. Deceased was stationed at Northwich-road Bridge and was granted leave of absence from January 21st to January 121.1 h. &amp;t the expiration of his leave Tipping had orders. to transfer to the Moore- lane Bridge. Private Richards, of the same company, who is stationed at the Action Grange Railway Bridge (Walton side), said that on January 25th, at 6.30 p.m., he heard someone call out "Sentry." In reply to his challenge the voice replied "Friend." Witness went up to and after proceeding about twenty-five yards found a soldier lying on his Aide on 'the bank of the canal. He was fully dressed, with the exception of his cap, but was in a drunken condition. The soldier informed witness he was going to the Moore-lane Bridge. After hemo- ing him to his. feet he saw he walked with difficulty and witness assisted him for about 150 yards. Witness left the soldier and re- turned to his post. He could not identify the body as that of the soldier referred to, as it was a very dark night when he met him,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Sentry's Duty.</head>
        <p>A Sentry's Duty.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Was it not your duty to call out the guard and put the man in charge? Witness replied that the guard-room was threequarters of a mile away and he dared not leave his post. The Coroner: Suppose this man had been a German spy, dressed in military uniform, what would you have done? Witness: We have no regulations to leave our post of duty. Sergeant Knowles, of the 2,4 th Loyal North Lancashire Regiment, stationed at Lower Wal- ton, who recovered the body, said he last saw Tipping alive on January 25th, about 4.30, at the Stag Inn. They. had a drink together. , Witness had a glass of beer and Tipping a small rum. They were only in a short time and adjourned to. the Ship Inn, where they had the same quantity of drink. When they parted at 4.55 Tipping was perfectly sober and • informed witness that he was going to his new billet at Moore Bridge. Tipping was quite cheerful, but witness could not say what he had had to drink that day. The place where he recovered the body was not far away from where Private Richards found him. The Coroner: Was it not the duty of Rich- ards to have called out the guard?. Witness: Re should have warned someone. P.C. Worthington proved that the deceased's watch had stopped at 7.55. . If He Had Been A German Spy!</p>
        <p>The Coroner, in reviewing the evidence, said he thought it was a dangerous procedure for Richards to have allowed Tippin,z h) walk along the canal bank in such a drunken con- dition. It was his duty to see that the man was lodged in the guard-room or to have taken other precautions. If that had been done the. man's life would have been saved. He did not blame Richards, as in evidence he had stated he dare not leave his post and there were no regulations to give instructions as to how he should act under such an eventuality. It was quite possible that the man might have been a spy and dressed in British military uniform. The men who wee protecting .our. bridges and canals ought, not to allow any 'strangers 60 near a bridge as Tipping was. It was quite possible that he had had other drink, because two small glasses of rum would not make him drunk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</head>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>Headquarters, Enville-street. Orders for 'week ending March 3rd. 1916:</p>
        <p>Parades. Tuesday.—Fall in at Headquarters 7.30 prompt. Company and Physical Drill. Semaphore Class, 8 p.m. to 9 p.m.</p>
        <p>- Friday.—Fall in at Bridge-street Rooms 7.30 prompt. Dress: Marching Order. Band Practice at Headquarters, 8 p.m. to 9 • p.m. Orderlies of the Week. Corporal W. Rigby and Lance-Corporal H. Smith. Note.—The officers have noticed that ninny Cadets have not yet provided themselves wi:ch water-bottles and haversacks." N.C.O.'s and Cadets are reqnested to do so as early as possible By Order, J. B. STARKEY, C.O. The Quartermaster will be pleased to inter- view any boy desirous of joining the Corps, any evening after 7 p.m., at 1, St. Mary-street; age limit, 14 to 18.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>FARM AND 6ANDA.</p>
        <p>At Chester ; last . week fanners brought to, market 22 tons of Cheshire cheese. The demand, was very brisk, and one fine .lot sold for 120 s.</p>
        <p>per cwt.</p>
        <p>BULLOCK'S THREE-MILE SWIM.</p>
        <p>At Bull last week Mr. Hewson, of Imming- ham, on the Lincolnshire side of the Humber, sold a bullock, which later in, the day was placed on board a ferryboat for Lincolnshire. It jumped overboard, but later was found graz- ing at Barrow-on-Humber. It had swum the- Humber, which is three miles wide at this point.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>COME BACK FROM ERIN!</head>
        <p>COME BACK FROM ERIN!</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cheshire Chamber of Agriculture at Crewe, it was decided to suggest to the Board of Agriculture that as Ireland hag been exempted from compulsory- military ser- vice, Irish labourer's should be starred for 'agri- cultural work and encouraged to return to- districts where their services are most needed and most valuable to the nation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>GOVERNMENT AND FARMERS.</head>
        <p>GOVERNMENT AND FARMERS.</p>
        <p>There was a discnßsion at the annual meeting of the Liverpool and District Farmers' Club on the "action of the Army Council in seizing all the baling presses in the district, and their re- fusal to allow the use of the same, except under coercive conditions." Complaint was made that instead of a transaction between a willing seller and a willing buysr, as the Act stipulated, it was pure and simple coercion. It was pointed out that owners had signed agreements and hired the presses to Government officers. If they had not the Government would have com- mandeered them. They had to make the best of a bad job.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WHEAT RESERVE.</head>
        <p>A WHEAT RESERVE.</p>
        <p>The "Times," discussing the., question of ,a wheat. reserve, recalls Mr. Yeihurgh's scherne advanced some 18 years ago, and says that if the Government have in bulk acquired a stock of about half a million quarters of wheat which, being held otherwise than in port granaries, do' not appear in the port returns, the nation would feel nothing but satisfaction. If the intention of the Government ,were -to start the formation bOf a wheat reserve with.. this, half , million of grain, the decision would be welcomed as the establishment of a policy long overdue. The quantity could be gradually increased, and the opening of the Australian now crop export season would supply an admirable occasion for the reserve being month by, month :raised .Juntil a really satislaebary aggregate was reached. There is plenty of granary spice. Mr. Yerburgh's scheme favoured the acceptance of three months' wheat supply-7,54d,000 to 8,000,000 quarters—as the quantity that should be held. "The scheme," we are told, "was favourably received by the public, but neither Liberal nor Unionist Governments would -find the money for the maintenance of the granAries."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>MILD AND OPEN JANUARY.</head>
        <p>A MILD AND OPEN JANUARY.</p>
        <p>, The crop rePorter9 of the Board of, Agricul- ture, in reporting on the ,agricultural position on Ist February. state that January generally proved mild and open, and enabled good pro- greBs to be made in cultivating the ground. much of the arrears caused by the wet Decem- ber being thus overtaken, while some more wheat was put in. In Wales and the north- west, however, • 'the lveathgr was more stormy, and work was greatly hindered. The early sown wheat is looking very well everywhere, while ' the late sown, although still weaker and more .backward than the first sowings, has greatly benefited by the mild weather, and is generally much more satisfactory,,than a month. ago. 'Other antunin:sown crops. are also healthy and satisfactory generally, though some are still backward.</p>
        <p>Lire stock generally have done quite Well dur- ing the month, many having been turned out into the open, which has enabled farmers to husband their supplieS of roots and hay. These are in many districts somewhat short, but the mild weather has been of great benefit, and un- less a prolonged cold spell sets in there should be about sufficient for requirements 'during the remainder of the winter.</p>
        <p>Labour is everywhere decidedly deficient, and much apprehension i•A felt as to the prospects of getting through tho heavy work of the spring.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WAR OFFICE AID FOR RUINED FARMERS'</head>
        <p>WAR OFFICE AID FOR RUINED FARMERS'</p>
        <p>FUND.</p>
        <p>The Wax Office has undertaken to provide free transport facilities between England' and Franca for the relief in kind which the Agricultural 'Relief of Allies committee (16; Bedford-square. London, W.C.) sends from ,tithe to time to the ruined farmers in the war-ridden areas of our Ally. Thus the national character of the work which the committee is doing on behalf of British .farmera,-who have pledged themselves to help the stricken agriculturists in Allied countries—receives still further official recogni- tion and Support. As the French government already under- takes the carriage oi the relief on its arrival in ,France and superviSes. its distribution, quick transit and equitable ,apportionment are en- sured. At the pref,ent time the committee is dispatching 2,000 sacks of seed oats, 704 sacks of seed potatoes, and LON head of poultry. Later it is hoped to accomplish similar measures of relief for farmers in other Allied countries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>SMARTING PILES CURED.</head>
        <p>SMARTING PILES CURED.</p>
        <p>Farmer's Wile And Zam-Buk's Unique</p>
        <p>Power.</p>
        <p>Ruiner convincing proof of Zam-Bules great soothing and healing power is furnished by Mn. A. L. Burbagc, of Taylor's Farm, Burton Green, near Christchurch, Bournemouth. "-I suffered terribly from itching, burning piles," said Mrs. Burbago to a "Bournemouth Guardian " reporter. "They were so had that I was away from work at the dairy about two months, and for a fortnight I had to stay in,bed. Sleep was impossible, and the constant irritation fairly wore me, out. , •"' - . •</p>
        <p>--"-Ilaving found Zan-,-Buk very useful for pain- ful cuts, and bruises, I now tried the balm for my pileB. A few applications of soothing Zarn-Buk convinced me that I had found the right remedy at last, for this herbal balm allayed. the torturing pain. ,?Vs I kept up the. treatment ,the .smiating piles went. itWay. I have been .quite free from this distressing com- plaint for some. time. " This is not the only time I have used Zam-But. Living opposite the village school, ;often have children brought to rue when they ;hurt themselves. One little boy, when playing on the village men., was cut on the head by a slate. Zam-Puk,quickly healed the Place. A nurse who striv the boy reinarked'how splendidly the broken skin had knitted together.</p>
        <p>"A young woman •iraetured her leg in a cycle accident: Although treated by a doctor the limb, after being set, would not heal properly. I. advised .herto ufze Zara-Buk. Whilst treating' her leg with Za.m-13uk, some splinters of loose bone that had prevented the wound healing cume away on the bandages. The place was then quickly healed up by Zam-Buk. "In many other eases I have recommended Zani-Buk„ and alwaya with the best of results.''' Be sure You get Zaza-Buk. Beware of dog' ',imitations and useless substitutes,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>eburrij grius.</head>
        <p>eburrij grius.</p>
        <p>FAREWELL SERMON.</p>
        <p>Ilev. W. T. Jaye Ends Ills Ministry At Emmanuel Church. .</p>
        <p>After six years' ministry as incumbent of the Emmanuel Reformed Episcopal Church, Bold-street, the Rev. W. T. Jaye relinquished that position on Sunday, when he preached his farewell sermon to a large congregation. The reverend gentleman said. he was very -anxious that the last sermon he would deliver from that pulpit—at any rate as their pastor— :re, 1:11d be something which .would have the tal° gospel message in it His aim always as to Preach the gospel faithfully. His ser- °!l6 Would still be of Christ and Him and whatever they thought of the which it had been his privilege to n old for six years he trusted that no one would Or be able to say that, as a minister of the g°sPel, he preached any sermon without bring- ing in the glorious atonement of the Christ. ne hoped that the Lord would bless them and be with them, and that in good time he would send a man to preach the glorious gospel to them. For the mistakes that he had made he</p>
        <p>asked God's pardon and also theirs. TO God alone belonged all the honour and praise. The reverend gentleman has accepted the living of St. Saviour's Church, Egremont, Liver- Pool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN.</p>
        <p>X Prosperous And licailhy Local Church.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, after devotional exer- cises, the annual congregational 'meeting was constituted with prayer, and presided over by the Moderator of Session (Rev. Wm. Reid). The Printed reports and financial statements of the eongregation, Sunday school, guild, choir, Women's Missionary Association and soldiers' comforts working party were taken as read and sustained. The current account of the congre- gation showed a net increase of over £6, while other offerings have been equally good.- £lB has been given to missions, &amp;c., while over £.39 has gone to aid the Local War Relief, Red Cross Infirmary, Y.M.C.A. Huts, Salvation Army, and other religious and philanthropic funds, in addition to just upon Eti aid sent at the end of last year .to the Protestant Church (Vresby- terian) of Belgium. The church debt has dur- ing the year been reduced from £=s to £275. It carries no 'interest, and is reduced annually at the rate of £5O. This year has also witnessed the completion of the purchase of the freehold of the church site, and of an additional piece of land at the rear, at a total cost of slightly Over £660, while the treasurer (Mr. J. Snail - ham) reports as the ',nucleus of an extension fund the amount in hand of £76 10s. 4.d. The Chairman to the Financial Authority (Mr. Wm. Russell) made a strong appeal to those com- raunicants who up to now had not recognised the sustentation fund—which is the, clergy fund of the church—to do so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>MISSIONS AT HOME.</head>
        <p>MISSIONS AT HOME.</p>
        <p>£40,000 Spent In One Year By Wesleyans.</p>
        <p>It was mentioned by the Rev. W. T. Derry, at the annual meeting of the Warrington Branch of the Wesleyan Home Missionary Society, held in the Lecture Hall, adjoining the 13ewseY-road Wesleyan Church, on Tuesday evening, that the contributions from the War- rington Circuit was £3B Ss. 5d., of which the Biewsey-road Wesleyans contributed E. 6 10,s. Bd. The total income of the Home Mission Fund for the year 191.1-15 was £37,475, the expendi- ture being £39,109. The reverend gentleman added that the care- fully prepared plan for promoting the revival .of the work of God which the conference of 1914 Adopted could not, under the new conditions created by the war, be carried out with any- thing like completeness. Nevertheless, special inissions conducted had been successful, and had led to deeper seriousness on the part of the Young people of the congregations and schools, and at every mission great numbers of them were influenced for good. In no part of the country had the war so deeply affected chui-ch life as in the rural areas. There wore cases in which practically the whole of the officials of the church had enlisted; nevertheless the work still went on. In many churches there had been great blessing experienced through the estab- lishment of a military camp in the neighbour- hood. Methodist, soldiers had attended the ser- vices, taken part in the church life, prayed in the prayer meetings, given their testimony in he classes, and even occupied the pulpits. (Applause.) The reverend gentleman alluded to the work being done at the Soldiers' Home in Bold-street, Warrington. He mentioned that it was Paying its way, but if it -Was found to fail to do so the Mayor had expressed his,willingness to open a Public fund. He (Mr. Derry), however, did not think that would be necessary. It was not a Methodist soldiers' home, except in the sense that Methodists were looking after it and work- ing it. If any outsiders wanted to lend a help-</p>
        <p>C</p>
        <p>'er Oakes, Paetrl,,Puddings a elm</p>
        <p>ing hand the committee would be willing to accept it.</p>
        <p>The Rey. T. A. Speight delivered an interest- ing addrel3s replete with reminiscences of • his six years' work as a missioner in East London.</p>
        <p>w 1 i</p>
        <p>IC</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>•'</p>
        <p>• ~.• V . '</p>
        <p>BAKING POWDER.</p>
        <p>HONOUR TO WHOM HONOUR.</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church Welcome To Corporal Chadwick.</p>
        <p>A most interesting-and unique social gather- ing took place in the Sankey Bridges School- room on Friday 'evening last week in honour of, and to welcome our hero, Corporal Charles Chadwick, of the Royal Garrison Artillery who, as was announced in these columns a short while ago, was awarded the Medialle Militaire, the French Government's highest and consequently, most coveted military distinction. Some time ago it was decided that we should have a social evening to welcome him, and a committee was appointed to act immediately he arrived home on furlough. Seeing he only came on the Tues- day evening prior to the meeting and that very unexpectedly, the committee are to be congratu- lated upon their efforts in making the social so great a success, there being well over one hundred friends present. An excellent pro- gramme had been arranged. A special invitation was extended to the whole of the Chadwick family, and also to Private Albert Newton, one of our members who was home on furlough after having been confined to hospital for some months with a shattered ankle, and to our friend, Mr. John Knowles, who has been severely wounded, and is now dis- charged from the Army. The Chairman, our respected superintendent, Mr. James Gorst, opened the meeting with a short speech, during which he made very touch- ing reference to the Corporal's father, whose , death occurred some months ago. Several other officials also made short speeches of congratula- tion and welcome. Musical items were inter- mixed, and Mr. Knowles made the meeting all the more lively with his humorous songs. After substantial refreshments had been ' served, competitions were arranged, and it was rather pleasing to notice that one of Corporal Chadwick's nieces took off a prize. More musi- cal items followed, and then Corporal Chadwick was called upon to deliver his "maiden speech; but hardly had he risen from his seat when "He's a jolly good soldier" was struck up with enthusiasm, and our distinguished guest could not do otherwise than submit to the voiced feelings of the gathering, who admire him all the more for his very unassuming and genteel manner. Corporal Chadwick spoke very 'briefly indeed, never mentioning his honoor, but thanking all concerned for the hearty invitation he and .his relatives had received to the social which they had all enjoyed so immensely, and for their good wishes and kind expressions. Mr. E. I. Rickers very ably oerformed the duties of accompanist.—w. A. B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>OUR BRAVE FALLEN.</head>
        <p>OUR BRAVE FALLEN.</p>
        <p>Have Peaceful Time In Spiritual World.</p>
        <p>The New Church lectures were continued on Tuesday evening in the Masonic Hall, Bold- street, where the Eva. A. E. Beilby, of Man- chester, spoke on "Where are the millions of slain, and what are they doing?" All who passed through the state of death, said the reverend gentleman, would awaken again in a spiritual worl&amp; a world in which we were all living now, and in which we had lived since birth, but in which we had lived in a sub- conscious state. All those men who had been slain had gone into a world where fighting would be no more, but yet where they would dwell to- gether with Men, women and children. To many of them it would be a change for the better, for their friends would be of the best. The warriors who had laid down their lives on the battlefield would dwell together. Comrades whom they had seen buried a short while ago would be there, and everything would be peace- ful and bright. In reality they had not died. but had exchanged one state of consciousness for another. They had closed their eyes on one world and opened them on another.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WYOLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>WYOLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was the usual meeting on Sunday, pre- sided over by the Secretary, who welcomed Private Bamford, and also moved a vote of sympathy with Mr. Beddard, an old member of the Brotherhood, who had met with a serious accident during the week. Mr. Wm. Street gave an address on "What must I do with Jesus?" and applied the words to all present. We must all answer the ques- tion—liarabbas or Christ, or the world or Christ, as our eternal destiny hangs upon it. Mr. McEachern and the Secretary took part in the meeting. Mr. Turton sang "The Voice of the Father" and "The Watchman" very effectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>BOVRIL (LIMITED).</head>
        <p>BOVRIL (LIMITED).</p>
        <p>Presiding at the annual meeting.," of Bovril (Limited), the Earl of Erroll, K.T., said that notwithstanding the higher, cost of materials, transport and labour, the net profits, with the exception of the Beer War year 1900, constituted a .record in the company's history. The in- creased trade was largely accounted for by the growing popularity of Bovril throughout the British Empire, and to some extent by the large gupplies purchased privately and sent out to soldier friends and relations at the Front- The net profit standing at £168,796 showed an in- crease of £31,211 ever last year irol (Lunged) had for the third year, in 5ac,34,9:.4i0n laid a eividend of 121 per cent., and increased the, reserve fond to £27;500.</p>
        <p>Sir James Crichton-Browne said that judging from the testimonials regarding its use both in field and base hospitals at the different fronts and in hospitals, camps, canteens', and munition works at home, Bovril might be regarded as a national asset. He was .satisfied that when the war was over and all manufactures or prepara- tions of German origin bad been banished from our shores, the unique body-building power of Bovril would be no less helpful in restoring to health Our broken Men and building up their shaft&amp;ed nerves. The repat was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Clear Direction.</head>
        <p>A Clear Direction.</p>
