        <p>Sold Everywhere in Stamped Boxes, Is. 3d. and 35., etith Full Direetions, ~ • ,el.l „ 1,. Safe 4.l.'tt: kst ', -`; .'"i'. l''t trt: 4; .4).11 ,4" , i Easy ...,„. 1...,f ?,',..'e., 43 AI tii .....- 40 -..--... u• ' el•' ' 1 T eathin g ! ''' 4; lt° r''''• ':1 r 4". *4l ' * ? .-, ''.' !1: ~.*.,'.',6 j; 1","' *,; ',.! . ~:.% ' ; .4. . ' it Tenth i lig ! 1 , (dlone hare the I'7-Me Mark: 'GS' EVERY MOTHER'S BOCK. "A 13 kur IN A CRADLN ." ) It., 7 -Me Hints on Feeding, j 0.,,,, 4.410, 1.1. i. 1 • 114'4, 2". , i t. vi • . i ~...^ iv t et*, " v. E. _copy. or .0,. ,„'.'r ...4 V.'ll ... 1.1.;5i (~,..;: ..` 4. '5 • ' . , '," .; ',Kn. .1•46 ' amdbadirrb durti. -</p>
        <p>READ FENNINGS' EVERY MOTHER'S BOOK. It contains Valuable flints on Feeding, o Teething, Weaning, Bleeping, etc.</p>
        <p>-• Ask your Chemist or Grocer for a NOM copy, or ono Intl be sent, POST FREE, on application to 41/41.F120 FIUININGS, Cowes. Ial• of Mat,.</p>
        <p>,A Londoner was travelling on Tyneside, and he asked a little boy which was the way to May- don. The boy thought a while, and then said: "Go straight lzp there, and you'll pass where the turnip geld was two years ago; then go straight on, and you'll pass where the hoppings were three years ago. Next turn to your left, and then to your right, next turn to your right again, and you'll pass where a fellow got killed ton months ago. Next', go straight up, and you'll pass where Jones lived before they shifted here, and then turn to your left, and you'll see Bladyon."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>A!„ I A • • ,Nl, * r *,` 1;•., ; •</head>
        <p>A!„ I A • • ,Nl, * r *,` 1;•., ; •</p>
        <p>by the civilian population, although women are occupying many official positions in asylums and infirmaries. The staff.of Bolton Infirmary is entirely composed of women. At present there is a lack of facilities for women students of medicine, only one general hospital in Eng- land being open to them, and in that institu- tion there are more students than beds. In the hospitals admitting men only, it is a common occurrence for one student to be put in charge of as many as seventy cases. The fighting line has absorbed a number of students, who in normal times would have qualified as doctors during the next five years. In the Royal In- firmary at Sheffield there are three women resident doctors, one woman assistant house physician, and two women assistant house surgeons. The University of Sheffield admits women on exactly the same footing as men; they are eligible also for staff appointments.</p>
        <p>Anti-Waste Campaign. The members of the Institute of Hygiene have inaugurated an anti-waste campaign, and arranged for a series of lectures dealing with the avoidance of waste of food, It was stated at one of these lectures that a sum amounting to six hundred million pounds—taking the retail price of food—is spent annually 'in this country, and that more than ten per cent. of this sum is wasted. It appears that at the present moment the upper classes are econo- mising to a greater extent than the lower, who are spending a large part of their ear4ings in unnecessary and expensive foodstuff.s. Much waste arises from the mishandling of eatables,</p>
        <p>as well as in wasteful ways of cooking. Ac- cording to one expert an adult whose occupa- tion is of a sedentary nature would enjoy improved health if he eats less, but those working with their muscles cannot afford to reduce the quantity of food they have been accustomed to consume.</p>
        <p>The Vogue of the Coat-Dress. When the coat-frock arrived last year, a great success was predicted for it, and for once prophecy has been fulfilled. This spring it will take the form of an outdoor dress and will be largely made of velveteen—that most popular of fabrics—which is this season more attractive in appearance than ever before. The most delightful characteristic of the new coats and frocks meet on and adorn the graceful and becoming coat-dress. It has the semi-tight- fitting bodice, is, trimmed With velvet, or satin</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Women and Peerages.</p>
        <p>The war, unhappily, seems likely to extin- guish more historic titles than create new honours. So many will lapse in consequence of -the death of the holder or his heir, in the war, that it has been suggested that daughters, or, failing them, sisters of peers, should be made eligible to succeed to the title. This would only be returning to the custom in feudal times, when peerages descended to daughters when there was no son, and a heiress conferred her title on her husband. But war is a creator as well as a destroyer of titles, and we could wish for nothing better than that a new duke- dom should emerge from the struggle of the nations. At least four or five of our dukes owe their titles to the prowess of their ancestors, in war, and it is to be hoped that the Great War will produce a British successor to Marl- borough and Wellington.</p>
        <p>Women and Agriculture. There is to be a general appeal to women to work on the land. Government machinery is to be put in operation, and every village is to be canvassed with a view to raising an army of women workers to take the place of the men who have joined the colours. No fewer than</p>
        <p>400,000 are wanted, but, as an occupation, agri- cultural work appeals to few women, and least of all to those best fitted to do it. In the country, and particularly in the South; there is a feeling among women that there is some- thing very menial and degrading about work in the fields. There is nothing attractive about milking cows at four in the morning, spread- ing manure, or attending to pigstyes, and with her allowance the wife of an agricultural labourer who has enlisted does not feel com- pelled to do it, especially as there is not so ratich money in it as in munition and other war work. Therefore the appeal is to be mainly directed to her sense of patriotism, in view of the urgent need there is for women to replace the men who have been taken from agricul- tural pursuits.</p>
        <p>Women Doctors. The shortage of doctors is already being felt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>HOME AND HEALTH NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME AND HEALTH NOTES.</p>
        <p>Golden Syrup Pudding.</p>
        <p>Chop 30z. of suet, mix it with 6oz. of flour; add a dessertspoonful each of carbonate of soda and of ground ginger, and a pinch of salt. Beat up an egg, mix it with Pa. of golden syrup and about a gill of milk, sufficient to moisten the pudding; stir to the dry ingred- ients. Put the pudding in a greased basin and steam it for two and a half hours. 'Serve with golden syrup sauce. Italian Macaroni. Macaroni served in the Italian style means simply boiled macaroni, over which a highly seasoned brown gravy is poured, grated cheese being sent round with the dish. The gravy is nicest made by cutting up in 2-inch pieces some lean, juicy beef, and simmering in a little water, into which a fried onion is put. Strain and thicken, adding half a dozen chopped mushrooms.</p>
        <p>Corns, Soft and Hard. You can easily cure the soft corn, which is often perfect agony. I learnt that only in re-</p>
        <p>cent years from a lady friend. None of the doctors' books said anything about it. All you have to do with a soft corn is to get a little powdered chalk, and rub it on with cotton wool every night. Very soon your trouble vanishes. The sensitiveness is apparently due to the moisture, and disappears when the place is kept thoroughly dry. Yoti can also cure a hard corn if you have patience and perseverance enough to endure a good deal of pain and perhaps lameness. Cut off the hard top and apply strong nitric acid with a bit of stick—the butt- end of tt. lucifer match will do. Cut off again and repeat in about ten days, and so on till all is gone. But most people don't care to do this, and keep themselves easy merely by cutting the corn until all the white core is out. Then you Wm comfort for a fortnight or so, When tire process has to be repeated.—" Preston Guar- dian."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>• children's Corner. 9)</p>
        <p>Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 4.</p>
        <p>Children, Join This Corner! Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best! Always Be Glad! Uncle Jack, " Examiner," Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SLATES IN SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>SLATES IN SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,— Last week I asked you to state your opinion of the proposal to re-introduce the slate into schools in order to economise with paper, which is scarce and dear', and will be more scarce and dear before the war is ended. The response has been most gratifying and shows the amount of general knowledge chil- dren, even very young children, possess now- a-days. Some have stated their facts not quite accu- rately. For example, some say that the ships cannot bring paper or paper pulp from Norway and Sweden, because we are blockaded. Really our Navy has kept the seas of the world open to us and we can get all the paper or pulp we want, so far as the enemy are concerned. The real reason is that all our ships are wanted for war purposes and only a limited numbez'. can be spared to carry paper.. Still the essays are very good and show a high standard of excellence. I have awarded the prize to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Niece Dorothy Wright (730),</head>
        <p>Niece Dorothy Wright (730),</p>
        <p>who gave. as I asked, her opinion on the sug- gestion, besides stating the facts. I like to hear the opinions of my little nephews and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>nieces.</head>
        <p>nieces.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>What shall we do for next week? The spring is approaching and Nature will be coming into active life. The prize next week shall be for the best essay on</p>
        <p>The Flowers I Would Like To Grow. Some of you have gardens at Ochool or at home, and I know most of you will have opinions upon such an interesting question. Your loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL ESSAY</head>
        <p>THE SUCCESSFUL ESSAY</p>
        <p>By Niece Dorothy Wright (No. 730), 68, Win- wick-street, who is 13 years of age and attends Wycliffe School. She writes :-- " Dear Uncle Jack,—l suggest that slateS should not come in use again, whether paper is dear or not, because of the dirty habits that children hare of spitting on a rag and rubbing, which is allowed by teachers in many infant schools.</p>
        <p>Little children cannot write on paper with a pencil as well as they can with chalk on slates, so if a child has to use a slate, he or she should have a sponge or a damp rag to clean the slate with. . -</p>
        <p>" The little children are given slate be- cause when they write on them the writing can be rubbed off easily, but on paper it cannot, and therefore it would mean a lot of paper being wasted by the little ones." Her younger sister, Niece Mabel Wright, aged 10, who also attend l Wycliffe School, .sends a somewhat similar essay and adds : " The school teachers say they hate to see the children spitting on slates."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>The next essay in order of merit comes from Niece Margaret Gleave, 64, Priestley-street, who won the drawing prize last week and now proves that she can draw as well as write. Niece Margaret concludes "In order to help my King and country / would rather have slates than books, if it is in other ways very inconvenient for myself. Those who wish to carry out the above will always think of the proverb 'Love yourself last,' and to remember their Kinj and country."</p>
        <p>Niece Mildred Brown, 35, Forshaw-street, writes in favour of a partial return to the, slate. "But," she says, "it is hoped the oldl mode of education will be adopted under cleaner conditions. One of these conditions should be that each scholar should be pro-. vided with a sufficiently damped sponge or , cloth to, take the place of the former habit.l The rule, however, should not be so strict as ' to make slates a monopoly. Paper ought to be used in moderation for such lessons as drawing, &amp;c., where the original copy cannot I be exactly reproduced." Niece Mabel Langley, 178, Padgate-lane.—"We have not yet got slates at Beamont School; we still have paper. If we have to have' slates I shall try my best to write as well as I do on paper. Slates are not very ,nice to write on, but things are getting so scarce and dear that we will have to put up with them." Niece Edith Pugh, 44, Glazebrook-street.— " Slates will be very useful, because when they are cleaned you can do something else on them; once we have filled a book a new one will have to be bought. My one objection to slates is they are unhealthy and some children will insist upon cleaning them in such a defiled way." Niece Annie Massey, Sandy-lane, Lymm.—</p>
        <p>"My opinion is that slates -will be as good as books and if kept clean and free from dirt it will be better. I think that if each scholar had her own slate there would not be as many diseases, as some are apt to use all kinds of old pieces of stuff which contracts the dis- ease."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Whittle, 123, Cartwright- street, St. Barnabas' School.—“ I think the books are more sanitary than slates, and ..zve are likely to take more pains to keep our books neat and well written than on a slate. When once we have done our lesson in a book we cannot very well alter it as on a slate, and it makes us more particular to do our work right the first time, but if it is any help to our country to save paper then we i,must do our bit in that way, but I prefer the books." Nephew Harold Crltchley, 355, Wellfield- ' street.—" Slates are not so nice and clean as paper and pen, beca.iise some children clean their slates by spitting on them; but -I would rather use a slate than paper, because a slate will not cost as much as buying ever so much paper, and so running ours Aves into a great expense." Niece Nellie Roberts, 43, Dudley-street.— "Miss Taylor (Bearaont School) says at some schools they are using slates. I would not be a bit surprised if we had elates next week but one."</p>
        <p>Nephew Joseph Jeavons, 9, Elizabeth-street.---- "The inspectors stopped the use of slates be- cause the children spit on them to clean them and they became very filthy and smelled a lot: this was the reason for it. They savor a lot of paper. The use of slates would save many a man's life."</p>
        <p>Niece F iorrie Farber, 61, Bewsey-street, Wycliffe School.—" What Uncle lack says about slates being unhealthy is quite right, because it is so. We would rather use slates than have those wicked germ-huns ruling over us. They are not worth giving a capital letter to their name."</p>
        <p>Niece Edith Moriand, 33, Fitzlierbert-street, Beamont Council School.—" I think slates should be used, because nowadays paper is very scarce, and using slates saves buying writing paper, and one sheet of paper can only be written on once, while the slate can be cleaned for another lesson, but when we clean the slate the lesson beforehand has gone, but it still remains on the paper, so there are advantages and disadvantages of slates coming into schools."</p>
        <p>Niece Laura Clayton, 13, Guardian,street.— "Tip+ oF,o of a slate would be all right if the ?hildren would use a small damposponge to Ripe it with." • Niece May Williams, 26, Garibaldi-street.—</p>
        <p>We know the times are very bad, but I think we should look very big babies to go and use slates again. We should think we were little children again, just beginning to go to chaol. We have used books so long and have got so used to it that it would look so silly to rith on slates. That is my opinion, so I will Ftoo now."</p>
        <p>Niece- Hilda Hassall, 36, Factory-lane.—"lf had to use a slate I would feel very babyish after using boob so very long. I am sure I</p>
        <p>Iwould not like anyone to see it. I think I , would almost feel that I had begun school • ~. , again.</p>
        <p>-Niece Annie Eaton, 22, Marson-street.—" Re- turning to use the slate makes us think of the time when we were little ones when we had just started school, .and it makes us feel younger than what we really are." Niece Florence Wycherley, 49, Leonard-street. —"The only advantage that can be claimed in changing from books to slates is that it is likely to help the country. A book is far better to use than a slate, because it is nicer to. write in and is. cleaner. If the books are carefully put away a record of school work' may be kept. It is pleasant to look through these books occasionally." "P.S.—Please enrol 'my brother, Philip, aged 12 years, Beamont Council School." Niece Lilian Himsworth, 15, Fitzherbert- street.—" I think it will be a rather good plan to bring back the slates into the schools. If we are doing a speed test we need not be quite so careful about being so neat and our marks will depend solely on our working. If the slates are brought back again, how funny it will seem! Why, we will be able to imagine our- selves an infant again." " P.S.—Jessie Patin wants to know if she may join your band of nieces and nephews. Her address is: 3, Fitzherbert-street, age 9 years, Beamont Council School." Niece Ethel Critchley, 357, Wellfield-street.— "I don't see why they do not let us write on slates in school. I would rather write on a slate; if we make a slight mistake we can easily rub it out." Niece Bessie Kelsall, 38, Queen-street.—"lt will be a lot of trouble to clean your slates.. I think I should have a wet sponge at the end of my slate. You won't be able to write as neatly as you can on paper, because it is very awkward to write on slates." Niece Ada Leech, 55, Dudley-street, Beamont Council School.—" If anything is written down wrong on the slates it could easily be rubbed out, but not without leaving an untidy mark if the slates are spit on. When slates are used it is necessary to be careful in using them, because they are so easily broken." Several other good letters were received, but they dealt with scarcity of paper and the cause, not the opinion of the writers on the use of slates in schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Waste at Table.</head>
        <p>Waste at Table.</p>
        <p>Speaking on "Waste at the Table," Mr. Grant Ramsey, principal of the Institute of Hygiene, Devonshire-street, said a greater number of people died every year from over- feeding than from starvation, and an eminent specialist had stated that 95 per cent. of all</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>THE EXAMINERS SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 26, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>+4•++.4.++++4":":•+++4.+4•••:•+•:•+4.+4.4.4.4.+4..X.4.4.4.4.+4,••:••:•++4•4.4.++++••4•4•4•4•++ • 4. 4. • • • • • • • • 4. • • • • • • • • 4. 4. • • • • 4. • • • • • • • • • • 4. • • • • 4. • 4. 4. • • 4. • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • •4. _ •:. +• • • • 4. •:. •:• • 4. • • • 4. • • • 4. • •</head>
        <p>+4•++.4.++++4":":•+++4.+4•••:•+•:•+4.+4.4.4.4.+4..X.4.4.4.4.+4,••:••:•++4•4.4.++++••4•4•4•4•++ • 4. 4. • • • • • • • • 4. • • • • • • • • 4. 4. • • • • 4. • • • • • • • • • • 4. • • • • 4. • 4. 4. • • 4. • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • •4. _ •:. +• • • • 4. •:. •:• • 4. • • • 4. • • • 4. • •</p>
        <p>.:..t, .:.</p>
        <p>bands, and is finished with a little stiffened cape that gives a pleasing touch of quaintness to the whole. A charming model in silk, but- toned straight down the front, was trimmed with black satin. A novel waist-belt, aLso of satin, seemed to start from the back of the neck and finished at the waist in broad folds. The bodice had a flaring basque, which was not very deep. In taffetas or cashmere-de-soie the coat-frock is as charming as in velvet The Art of the Veil.</p>
        <p>That the veil has lately assumed a position of importance in the scheme of feminine attire becomes more evident every day. It has now to be seriously considered when deciding on a costume, as well as on a hat. Its negligent adjustment, too, may mar the effect of even a very smart toilette. Some women have the knack of arranging a veil becomingly, and smartly; others lack the gift, and it must be confessed it is not as simple as it seems. So various, however, are veils this season, in shape and material, that every type of face should be suited. To judge from the variety, milliners must have been exercising their ingenuity in devising a special veil for every hat,' or, rather, every shape. These experi- ments have resulted in not a few extrava- gances, as well as in veils of all sizes and lengths, from kiss-veils to floaters, and each is enjoYing.raoro or less poi?ularity. • Hat Pins and Hat Brims.</p>
        <p>The ornamental hat pin is again to the fore, and in some cases is the only decoration on the</p>
        <p>new hats. They are large in size, and as various in design as in the materials of which they are made. Meagre trimmings are the rule this spring, and often consist nxerely of a tiny posy of flowers in silk, satin, velvet, or kid, or a narrow band of ribbon that is finished with a flat but diminutive bow. A curious effect is produced when the sole trimming consists of a jetted or other rose fixed on the top of the crown. A wreath of small but bright green leaves placed at the base of a high bat, with scarcely any brim, is another rem'arkable de- coration. The brims of the larger hats are often ornamented with bands of ruched velvet or silk, just at the edge, or the sole trimming may consist of a narrow flat edging of feathers or leaves. An upstanding frill of ribbon or tulle is placed round the top of the crown of some small straw hats, forming quite an.inex- pensive, if not a very graceful adornment.</p>
        <p>diseases were due to errors of digestion. Two good meals a day were enough, but it was the quantity eaten, rather than the number of meals, which mattered, and three might be taken with equal benefit if they were not too large. The custom of having cut bread and butter was ,very wasteful, as often only half of what was tut was used, or people were induced to eat more than they needed, because it was on the table. Bread was tremendously wasted. It should be kept in stone jars, and when cut turned down on the side that had been cut. Butter, too, was very often wasted by making it into pats, which depreciated its keeping quality by giving it a larger surface to the air. Pancakes in War-time?</p>
        <p>Yes, why not? when by using McDougall's Self-raising Flour you can get the lightest, most delicious and easily digested, at much less cost, because fewer eggs are required. You have no baking powder to buy and mix, McDougall's Flour containing the very best ingredients reads for you, and in just the .exact propor- i tions. Shrove Tuesday this year s on the 7th March. For Yorkshire puddings too, Mc- Dougall's Flour is a speciality. All grocers stock it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>738 Joseph Jeavons, 9, Elizabeth-street; age 11 ; A rpley-street School. 739 Annie Eaton, 22, Marson-street, off Tanners-lane. •</p>
        <p>740 Philip Wycherley, 49, Lequard-street; 12;</p>
        <p>741 Jessie Palin, 3, Fitzherbert-street ; 9; Beamont.</p>
        <p>742 Annio Jackson, 14, Market-street; 10; St, Alban's.</p>
        <p>743 Alice Ashurst, 11, Foundry-street; 12; Ileathside.</p>
        <p>744 Lucy Lees, 51, Forshaw-strect; 11; Bea- mont. •</p>
        <p>745 Phyllis Lightfoot (no address given); 9; Beamont.</p>
        <p>746 Doris Beswick, 72, Priestley-street; 11; Ileathside.</p>
        <p>747, Lizzie Roughley, Howard-street; 11; Heathside.</p>
        <p>748 Horace Williams, 49, Priestley-street; Arpley-streot School. 749 Annie Jackson, 14, Market-street.</p>
        <p>MEMBER'S NUMBER. IsiSiece Erica Bolton asks for her number. It is 529. .</p>
        <p>1 BEAUTIFUL PRIZE.</p>
        <p>Niece Margaret Gloave, 64, Priestley-street, writes : •</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Jack,—l cannot thank you too much for the beautiful prize, which I appre- ciate very much.. It appears to me to be a long, interesting story, and, as I have men- tioned before, will be the superior of my other books."</p>
        <p>"I think it was a lovely iris," writes Niece Annie Massey, of Lymna, who not long ago was herself a prize-winner. She was, of course, referring to last week's prize drawing, which she praises, although she herself was an un- successful competitor. Her praise shows she has a lovely disposition; don't you -think so?</p>
        <p>"THE CHEEK Of THEM I"</p>
        <p>Nomadic Family Lives Rent. Free In Tennis Pavilion.</p>
        <p>SIMPLE-LIFE MARAUDERS IN BEWSEY.</p>
        <p>"The cheek of them!"</p>
        <p>She of the indignant exclamation was one of a little band of Bewsey tennis players who, on visiting their grounds on Sunday, saw leisurely ensconsced in the deck chairs in the pavilion .1 strange man and woman, with a dusty-faced baby lying on the floor in front of them. Wodd was blazing in the fire casket; cups, I saucers, plates and saucepans were arranged untidily about the tables; the man was sitting in his shirt sleeves smoking and having a Sun- day afternoon's meditation; the woman had slipped into oblivion via a Sunday afternoon's nap; and the child was soiling a cushion with its sticky fingers. It looked a pity to disturb them: everything was so romantic—trees all round, like Walden Pond,* and happiness and contentment, and never-care, as of any Romany Rye of yore: the most pleasant accretion of Nature and human nature on the Bewsey side of the borough. But the scene was startling, and who but the dumb could restrain vocal expression? "The cheek of them !" sounded like a cock-crow, an alarm clock, a bugler's "Reveille," a Sergeant's "Show a leg!" or anything which prompts I wakefulness abruptly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Speedy Removal.</head>
        <p>A Speedy Removal.</p>
        <p>The man scooped up his jacket and a basket, the woman scooped up the child and, within ten seconds, they were over the fence like Sunny Jims. Moving off immediately obviated the use of force. There has never been a finer example of "Orders promptly executed" in the history of any shop, local or national. The little party of disturbers hadn't time to ask *them how they had enjoyed themselves; whether the man would like to pay rent monthly or quarterly, whether the woman found the crockery to her liking, whether, when the child grew up, it would be a gipsv.</p>
        <p>After all, these thin-zs- did not matter. It was obvious that not a few days and nights had been spent peacefully amid the scenes of happy summer evenings, but that all the injury done was the breaking of a pane of glass in order to slip the door-catch back.</p>
        <p>At the next meeting of the committee it may be suggested that a winter visiting patrol shall be established. It is too late to talk of distrain- ing on the nomads for rent due, for they have gone, leaving no trace, but good impressions in the chairs behind them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>NATIONAL CARE OF MOTHERS AND CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL CARE OF MOTHERS AND CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Ante-Natal Clinics.</p>
        <p>THE CATHOLIC WOMEN'S LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>n the first year of the present war this country lost more babies under the age of twelve months than soldiers killed upon the battle- field.</p>
        <p>This appalling fact is true, notwithstanding</p>
        <p>the vast strides which have been made in the reduction of infant mortality during recent years. A closer grappling with the problem- serves to open up still further ways of saving child-life.</p>
        <p>Never in our national history has there been a more urgent need for preserving the race. The decline in the birth-rate has recently been accelerated owing to war conditions. That makes it all the more important-for those who are born to be welllborn and well-bred. In- quiries nowadays are being pushed further back and ante-natal conditions are beginning to re- ceive fuller attention.,</p>
        <p>The Women's Co-operative Guild has placed the whole community under a debt by bringing before the public some of the grave difficulties connected with maternity. The book which they recently published, entitled "Maternity," is an ugly revelation of conditions made more bitter by financial stringency. Certain figures were obtained from a number of women be- longing to the wage-earning classes. It was discovered that the proportion of still-births and miscarriages together amounted to 21 per cent. in relation to live births. It is becoming more clear than ever that the care of the mother, both before and after the birth of a child, will help enormously to bring about a reduction of infantile mortality. Maternity benefit under the Insurance Act has, of course, been a step in the right direction, but there is an urgent need for maternity centres, where babies can be born under the most favourable conditions, especially in cases where there is likely to be a risk of losing life. Under present conditions many general volun- tary hospitals refuse to accept maternity cases, and sometimes the Workhouse hospital is the only place available apart from the home. There is a natural objection on the part of many to accept the aid of the Poor J..4aw, and cases have been known where life has been sacrificed owing to a refusal to accept such help. Public opin- ion needs educating on this subject, for it is very difficult for a local Health Authorlty to go far in advance of intelligent public opinion. So far as Warrington is concerned, there will be. an opportunity of hearing something qn this topic at the annual meeting of the Guild of Help and Mothers' Welcomes next Tuesday evening. Mrs. Lawton, one of the speakers, has had special experience in the Potteries, for she is the seeretary of the Women's Maternity Committee for Stoke-on-Trent. She has also organised a scheme for the feeding of vpectant and nursing mothers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Dangerous Ignorance.</head>
        <p>Dangerous Ignorance.</p>
        <p>Already there has been a marked advance in Warrington, but much remains to be done in spreading knowledge as to the best methods of. caring for babies. An example may be given of the kind of ignorance which still prevails in certain quarters. A mother with a very young baby, whose digestive powers were very weak, was advised by a medical man to mix a pro- portion of water with cow's milk. The mother did 90, but when the child was eleven months old it was still getting the same mixture, and the baby weighed no more than many a healthy new-born child. When the mistake was recti- fied the child made rapid progress on undiluted milk.</p>
        <p>Many possible. reforms have been. suggested</p>
        <p>apart from maternity centres, mothers' wel- comes and ante-natal clinics. These include a large increase in maternity benefit, the placing of the midwifery profession on a municipal basis, and the provision of household helps. A new evidence of the public interest in in- fant welfare was given at the conference of the Catholic Women's League, at Brighton last week. Cardinal Bourne then said : "They knew that the amount of infantile mortality from 'preventable causes was enormous and the League was desirous of co-operating with those engaged in this work to stem the evil and remove the causes." On the Cardinal's proposal a resolution was passed urging members of the League to in-</p>
        <p>crease their interest in the great national cam- paign against infantile mortality, and for that purpose to establish more mothers' and babies' welcomes and similar organisations to preserve</p>
