      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ALWAYS ASK FOR A</head>
        <p>ALWAYS ASK FOR A</p>
        <p>"DR. COLUS CHOWNE.:</p>
        <p>‘ftritswar</p>
        <p>WOMEN CREATE PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Railway Clerks Concerned At</p>
        <p>Their Introduction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>GARDEN OF EDEN ANALOGY.</head>
        <p>GARDEN OF EDEN ANALOGY.</p>
        <p>"The introduction of woman into the Garden of Eden led to trouble, and the introduction of women into the railway world has had a similar effect," a mixed audience of Warrington railway clerks was told on Saturday night at the Win- marleigh Cafe. The speaker was Mr. Townend, a member of the executive of the Railway Clerks' Association.</p>
        <p>In Warrington, it was explained by the Secre- tary, Mr. W. Pearce, there are 250 railway clerks, 50 of whom are ladies. During the past year there had been an increase of 73 in the membership of the union, and only about 50 are "without the pale." Up„to Saturday half a dozen ladies were members, but as a result of the meeting more were enlisted. Mr. Townend declared that the most serious problem railway clerks had to face was the in- troduction of the woman worker. He was afraid that railway directors were going to try to use women to crush the demand of the men for better conditions and .higher wages. They were determined, if possible, to use the girls to squash the Railway Clerks' Association, and were doing all they could to prevent the girls joining it. Managers generally declared that the girls were being employed just for the duration of the war„ but the executive of the association were per- suaded that they were here for all time; in fact, one company had drawn up a superannuation scheme whereby they could draw a pension at the age of 65 years. (Laughter.) The directors were determined to have the women because they thought their labour was cheap and because tlley would oust the men. The only way in which male clerks could turn that menace to their advantage was to get the girls inside the association and by fighting for the same wage for the same work, no matter of what sex the clerk was. (Applause.) Al Lady's Warning. Miss Thompson (of Ashton), who was the first lady delegate to the annual conference of the R.C.A., said that at present lady clerks were inexperienced and might not be so good as the men, but in time they would be, and if they were not careful to organise, they would be doing the same work for much less pay. Girls had not been taken into the service of the com- panies out of charity, but because they were cheap and were necessary to the railway service, especially now that the men had joined the Forces. If girls used their opportunities as they ought to do, there was a chance of their earn- ing an independent living. But they must take advantage of their opportunities. Now that the railway companies wanted lady clerks badly was the time to say, "Well, if you want us, you have got to pay for us!" If the companies would not pay them competencies those ladies already in the service should advise their friends not to join, and by that means the companies would have to pay more in order to get labour. "When I joined the service four years ago," she continued, "it was not long before I dis- covered that if I kept on working at 10s. a week and became proficent at my work the railway company would not want to pay anyone else more than that amount. There was discontent amongst us girls because we could not keep ourselves on what we were earning, and there was discontent amongst the men because we were working for so low a wage." (Applause.) She was of opinion that women should get the same wage as men, and for that reason she ardently supported the R.C.A. Until women's wages were increased it was hardly likely that the men would be able to get advances. She appealed to all lady clerks to join the Society, for, as the JtC.A. flourished, so would their con- ditions improve. (Applause.) Individually, they could do little with directors, but, united, they could obtain what was fair and just. (Applause.) On the L. and N.-W. Railway the scale of salaries for women stopped at 235. per week. Poor sum for a week of a woman's life! The scale certainly needed highering. (Ap- plause.) R.C.A. Not a Suffragette Union.</p>
        <p>"The trouble with many lady clerks is that</p>
        <p>they are' afraid of a strike," concluded Miss Thompson. "Don't think the R.C.A. is going to strike yet awhile. It hasn't got a strike policy. We shall not do anything silly or any- thing indiscreet, and a strike is not very likely</p>
        <p>_ to come for some time; and ladies must not think they are going to be given a hammer and told to smash windows as soon as they join." (Laughter, and applause.) Another point touched on by Mr. Townend was the war bonus of ss. a week, which was paid clerks by the Government. When the rail! ways were handed back to the directors, he remarked, an attempt would be made to rob the , clerks of the increase, but they should be deter- mined that so long as the higher price of com- modities reigned the bonus should be paid. To get ready for the fight it was essential that the R.C.A.'s funds should be increased and, there- fore, he appealed to the Warrington members to agree wholeheartedly to the higher subscription it had been proposed to levy. From what he knew of them he did not think the hopes of the Executive Committee would be falsified in this direction. (Applause.) Mr. W. Palin presided over a good attend- ance, and a vote of thanks was accorded the speakers on the proposition of Mr. W. Hutton, seconded by Mr. W. Burgess.</p>
        <p>After the Military Hospital Concert. "Well, Bill. I've heard a better sing-song than that by the Germans in the trenches—and we shot 'em for it."—" Punch."</p>
