      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <p>TANNER BROTHERSi</p>
        <p>CASH OR TERMS TO sfrngqt,pwo- -4114".i COMPLETE HOUSE kURNISHERS.</p>
        <p>27, WINWICK-STREET, WARRINGTON. (Next to Central Station.)</p>
        <p>Acknowledgments.</p>
        <p>Complaints that exemptions are being given in too great a number cannot be-levelled against the Warrington Tribunal. At their;first public sitting yesterday by far the majority of appli- cations were refused, and some of the men intim- ated that they would appeal; to ra higher Tri- bunal.</p>
        <p>The sitting .was not without its- bright inci- dents. The appellants had been requested to come, at various periods during the afternoon, so that they would have to waste little time in waiting for other eases to be heard.</p>
        <p>At one period, owing to there being no appli- cants present, the Tribunal had nothing to do, whereupon the Clerk (Kr. A. T. Holloway) turned to the Mayor and suggested that he should call the name of "John Wilkinson."</p>
        <p>Ready for a joke, the Mayor immediately acquiesced, and, being unaware of the plot, Councillor Wilkinson, who was sitting among the public in the distance, stood up obediently.</p>
        <p>"It's all right, Councillor," remarked the Mayor. "Someone is only pulling your leg !" And the Councillor was immediately exempted.</p>
        <p>Who do you think was the. first_ member of the public to enter the Council' Chamber when the Tribunal met? Alderman Bennett. The energetic Alderman is übiquitous and never misses anything.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Crosfield, accompanied by the Mayor, Yisited the Lord 'Derby 'Hospital on Wednesday, and Was delighted. with what is being done there for the comfort of our wounded " Tommies." He also had the. priyilege, of in- specting the hospital train .which arrived the same day at Arpley with 160 new patients for the hospital'.</p>
        <p>A short time ago Lady Gerard made an appeal in-" The Examiner" for funds for 'comforts for the -Inniskillings stationed in ..Ireland, In re- sponse to this appeal, Mr. Felix Mtigiiin has for- warded to the Mayor .:1:5 on tiehalf:Olthe- Alli- ance-Box Company. . • •</p>
        <p>Now that the Warrington Town Football Club has got going strongly,.l ank-expectinc, that a considerable number of supporters willbjourriey with them to Eccles to-day. - The train leaves the Central Station at 1.53:</p>
        <p>'Tis often argued by opponent of the re- stricted drink- regulations that you .c.annot make peaple sober by Act of PaTliameat.</p>
        <p>If that were true, one would naturally expect offences for drunkenness to &amp;hoc no signs of diminution. •</p>
        <p>How do they explain that for a whole week, from February 17th to 24th, not a single case of drunkenness was reported by ,the :;,Warrington police? 'Nutt' said!</p>
        <p>When walking along Parker-street .on Tuesday noticed an old woman espy something in the gutter which she thought would do for her fire- place.</p>
        <p>It .was a lean piece of wood, about five feet long, and, in order to carry it in her. basket, she tried to . break it by stamping on. it with her foot across the gutter, but it, was—too. springy, and she had to carry it sway full length.</p>
        <p>The incident reminded me of a ,*.l.th Stafford- shire story. "Ten years ago, odt- Bill got a piece er wood he wanted to break."' said a • brother to a friend. "First, 'e pitt,it'across 'is knee. but it wouldna break that Then 'e put one end on it on't I'o'Orsteo.:lsut it would- na break that road. So 'e put it a'Cross th well and trod or it. We 'ii sid 'im since!" •</p>
        <p>, I don't know what happene!ecto -the old lady</p>
        <p>Keep • to the right! • Epeeially on very dart: nights, or, now the lamps are ot shining brightly, results may be serious.</p>
        <p>I see the Chester magistrates have fined a girl 19s. 6d.-for having assaulted a girllainp-lighter, after having "passed reinaTks" 10)0a her oceu:. • _ pation. • •</p>
        <p>I hope that. if any .sueli ease arises in War- rington the defendant will be ..sent,to gaol with- out the option .Of paying up, even .:tOr " passing remarks." •</p>
        <p>The girl ov.the an who will net do neces- sary work and "passes remarks'''. at the ex- pense of thtse who will, deserves hard labour, whether or not the impudence is backed up by an assault.</p>
        <p>Why should the time of starting the cars from the Central change for the half of two days a week? The yule, w‘hich cannot always be re- membered, causes a regular loss of revenue. It is also the occasional cause of making would-he passengers into golf link linguists.</p>
        <p>VIGILANT.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 26, 1916. GOSSIP,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SIR A. H. CROSFIELD AND AFTER THE WAR.</head>
        <p>SIR A. H. CROSFIELD AND AFTER THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The visit to Warrington this week of Sir Arthur Crosfild to show his keen apprecia- tion of the work that is-being done by the women, not necessarily Liberals only, who assemble at the John Crosfield Memorial Hall to knit and sew comforts for the men who are defending us in the trenches, has enabled him to touch upon the important question of the</p>
        <p>after-the-war settlement. Last week we re- ferred to . the ill-advised and headlong rush that is being made by certain Protectionists to advocate their old heresy while passion has pog-kssion of men's souls and, hatred is likely to govern their actions. We counselled caution, and, by inference, Sir Arthur does the same; for, while taking note of the dread- ful drama that is being enacted before our eyes, and while acknowledging, as we all acknowledge, that the Germans have done such things as to make them the outcasts of civilisation, he holds that the first object of paramount and supreme importance, and the highest achievement of statesmanship, will be involved in the 'task of settling our relation- ships—after the war. The ,conditions -. by which we shall be guided are not fully known now, and it would be idle, or, at all events, unwise, to charge our righteously-inflamed minds with the task of determining what we shall do in conditions we cannot see or fully comprehend.</p>
        <p>• But we must be prepared for some startling changes when the war is over. That so liberal-minded a man as Sir Arthur Crosfield, along with many of the leaders of his ow. party, is prepareil to consider,: nay, conceives it to be haS duty to !indicate that he'will:(;,on- sider, in due season; the departure that ii to be made from time-tried and proved polity, is added proof that Germany and Austria as we know them to-day are doomed.. Only in- ternal revolution, by which the peoples of those deluded nations will separate themselves from '-and repudiate the action of their. war lords, including their ruler, the arch-criminal of the whole herd, can save-.them; and'even revointion, after the manner they 'have dis- graced.clemoeracy for flearly two years,- would scarcely give them inimediate..grace. How- ever, as Sir, Arthur says, , there will be, de-,1 velopments 'which will 'have' a vital bearing 1 upon the ultimate settlement, but our feelings! our judgment as human beings, .must,:and_will modify our practice even in matters that are! ! ruled by the laws of economics. We have 1 done all we could to make Germany friendly ! disposed towards us. Sir Arthur himself was at the head of a great international !novement whose object it was to humanise the condi- tions of labour by international agreement, and so to bring the toilers' milleniura a few stag ,nearer. Can the people. who tore 'up solemn Obligations and dubbed treaties scraps of paper be trusted to run in harness, indus- trially,. with decent, honest-minded! peoples? The recent action Of a British captain, who left a wrecked Zeppelin crew to their fate in the sea, because he could not accept their word of honour, may be -.taken as a model of the respect the world will have for the Ger- man's word in future. In present conditions</p>
        <p>in any movement of labour the Central Powers will be treated as outlaws; because they stand self-condemned and unabashed.</p>
        <p>But there is 'another reason, perhaps more powerful even than that of national respect, which forbids a proudly-righteous people having anything to do with thieves and mur- derers. It is, in fact, a mixture of sentiment and economic consideration. The trade that Germany has done with the 'Allies and .the Allies' possessions has made her rich enough to think she could challenge them successfully to mortal Combat. Must we not withdraw that Means for the acquirement of 'wealth that may lead to crime on a larger scale in the future ? A union of the, Allies in trade as against Germany and her. dupes Would be the most 'effectual barricade against the ad- vance in the future i that peculiar Product, enlightened barbarism, which is glossed .over by the. name of Kultur. These ,are ,'sugges- tions .of a few of the lines' of .future action upon which we .may bo employing our thoughts; they are some of the suggestions which it will be the duty of our stateSmanship to consider and put into working 'and .workable concrete shape.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>COLOSSAL EXPENDITURE.</head>
        <p>COLOSSAL EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>On Monday- the Prime Minister moved Vote of Credit for £420,000,000, of which £120,000,000 is for the service of the present financial year, and £300,000,000 for the finan- cial year 1916-17, which begins on April Ist. The .latter amount i 4 calculated to carry us through to the end of May. This latest Vote of Credit, the sixth to which he Prime Min- ister has asked the' assent of the House dur- ing the current financial year, brings the total since the outbreak of war •up to -t 1;:t82,00ci,000!' The 'basis On 'which the Vote- to ihe ed of May is ealculated,lii estpendivi ture-of £.50000.,000-n:.diy; hut that 'averagel has never yet 'heeirrearthed, and is'exPected'to allow a fair margin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WAITING ECR ZEPPS,</head>
        <p>WAITING ECR ZEPPS,</p>
        <p>Special Constables Make Merry While Wind Rages. .</p>
        <p>READY FOR EMERGENCIES.</p>
        <p>The force of special constables sworii in .at the Borough. Police Court on Saturday was maiiily recruited from the ranks of works man- agers, 'shopkeepers, • solicitors, school .teachers and journalists.</p>
        <p>They have come forward to serve in case of air raids, when their principal duty will be to warn the various works in the town. and insist upon the inhabitants taking shelter. No street parades or uniforms will be worn. A badge will be the only symbol of authority. After they had taken the oath the Deputy Mayor (Dr. Joseph) told them that this was the time for deeds not words, but he could not allow that opportunity to pass without thank- ing them for having volunteered. This was the time for everyone to do his duty. By offering to look after order and prevent panic they were doing their duty, and would be very useful, as a great number of policemen had gone abroad. This was the time for every ounce of force and brain to be used to save our country from ruin and to conduct affairs successfully against a most unserupulous but vigilant foe. He hoped that those present would not have a strenuous time, considering the purpose of their appoint- ment, but hoped they would be useful in pro- tecting the borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Billiards for Nothing.</head>
        <p>Billiards for Nothing.</p>
        <p>, As, yet the "Specials" have had little to do. Their extensive numbers have permitted an arrangement whereby they need. only report themselves, at the Pollee Station on an average of two nights per week. A few enthusiasts hav- ing volunteered to patrol the streets with detec- tives, they have carried Out their obligations satisfactorily, but most of the "Specials" are merely waiting for the airships. The hours of waiting are not at all tiresome. They are not being ,spent on the roof of the Police Station with eyes turned skywards. Instead, eyes look downward on to a billiard table. Until this week Only once since the out- break of war has billiards. been played here, the table 'in' the recreation room, at the Police Station having been bearded-across by the mili- tary and used as a bed for documents, &amp;c., but now, in the evenings, it ,is ,uncovered. The -" Specials", chalk the cues, hear. the regular policemen come in and remark, " It's,windy and a bit cold outside," retort with, ." Zepps. about, I suppose?" and then get 'on with their billiards. They are having, the most pleasant of times in the most. clubable Of ways. Some night work may follow, ' just as, occasionally, soldiers charge after enjoying bread 'and .jam. The names Of the "Specials "-are as follow :</p>
        <p>Henry Maitland Ashton. Cecil John Birtwistle Barlow, George Arthur Barlow, James Welsby ,Bibby,. Sidney Bolton, Robert Percy Booth, Arthur Be,ttoniley, George William Brook, George W. A. Brown. Fred. ChadiVick, Thomas Cooper. 'Harold Leach Dee, Percy -.Charles Dendy, Arthur Dickins. Harry Hope Evans.' John James Firth, Walter Thomas Flood. Robert Garnett, James Gray. , Charles Edward Hawthorn, James Henry Hodgson: •• J. Foster Jackson. William Richardson Laws, James Robey Lowe. William Mason, Thomas Edward Miln, Wil- liam Murray. • - George Newburn. Henry Stuart Page, •James John Alexander Parfitt, Thomas Seddon "Pemberton. . William Richardson, Harry Rogers. George Smith, James Smith, John Snailham, ThomaS Henry Spinks, Thomas Samuel Steel, Frederick Charles Garton Stone, Frederick Storey: William Thomas _ _</p>
        <p>Arthur Herbert Walker. Joseph Henry WnVier, Thomas Weston, William Walter West- well.</p>
        <p>John Vpson</p>
        <p>"SPECIAL'S" EARLY START.</p>
        <p>One Of the_ liewly-aPpanted pèciul constabled who wei.e sworn in on Saturday had the,satis- faction of booking his first case on Wednesday night, The charge was one of drunkenness, and the prisoner was convicted on Thursday miprning. pecial Constable .Mason spoke to • finding Edw*rd Davies, an old man, helplessly drunk en the footpath in Bride-street. As he was both a danger to himself and pedestrians, the officer was obliged to take him into custody. The Chairman of the Magistrates (Mr. J. Rymer Young), in fining him 10s., remarked that ,they would remove the temptation for further drunkenness by taking that amount from him .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LOCKERBIF,-BRERETON.</head>
        <p>LOCKERBIF,-BRERETON.</p>
        <p>She wedding took place on Thursday at Holy Trinity Church of Walter Sudlow Lockerbie, eldest son of Mr. and Mr's. Lockerbie, 124, Chester-road, Warrington, and Olive, the third daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Henry Brereton, of 161, Ellesmere-road, Lower Walton. • '</p>
        <p>The bride, who wore a fawn cbstume, curried a sheaf of lilies, and was given away by her father. Her two 'sisters aeted as bridesmaids: The best man was Mr. Alfred Stanley Hazie- hurst: Owing' to a double bereavement in the family of the bridegroom the wedding was of a quiet nature: The honeymoon is being spent in Scotland.</p>
        <p>match, was attended by Miss Margery Wiseman. Owing to the bride's only brother, Mr. T. E. Millinga.n Grundy, being absent and unable to give her away, Colonel A. M. Murray, c. 8., acted in his stead. The wedding was a very quiet one, on account of Captain Ridgway's brother having recently died of wounds received in action at the Dar- danelles, and Mr. T. E. Milligan Grundy being a prisoner in ITskub, erbia.Wjth Lady Paget's I:log:At:Al Unit. • • • . is ~iresting to' ment tht ion a ilium Grqn4;aidageeßtor.o£ the IgidP,,ut Ip9 yPrs ago,. Married ...Elizabeth' ,Bidgway, a . sister of Isaac 'Ridgway, the great-grandfather of Captain T. Ridgway.</p>
