      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>L.;-*cAL 2.. INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>L.;-*cAL 2.. INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Infectious Disease.</p>
        <p>The following cases of infectious disease were reported last week :—Scarlet fever, 12; diph- theria, 3; tuberculous phthisis, 2; measles, 6; erysipelas, 2; ophthalmia, 1.</p>
        <p>Re-elected Member of Education Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. Josiah Longland's term of office as a co- optative membez of the Education Committee having expired, he was unanimously re-elected at the monthly meeting on Monday evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Productive Pound Day.</head>
        <p>A Productive Pound Day.</p>
        <p>Thursday's Pound Day at the Thelivall Heys Military Convalescent Hospital produced the sum of .tl5 ss„ which will make the supply of cigarettes (procured at half-price) secure for some time to'come. The comforts and general funds will also benefit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Bronchitis Busy.</head>
        <p>Bronchitis Busy.</p>
        <p>- -.- In the week ended February 19 there were 23 deaths in Warrington against 37 in the corre- sponding week lait year. Of the 23; bronchitis was responsible for six, the other causes being: Phthisis, four.; enteritis, gastro-inter-catarrh, and pneumonia, two each; heart disease and accident, one each; all other causes, five.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Warrington Market_ Prices.</head>
        <p>Warrington Market_ Prices.</p>
        <p>At Wednesdays Warrington Market, the price of wheat 'ruled at Bs. 6d. to Bs. 9d. per 701 b., and 4s. 6d. to 4s. Bd. per 45112 was raised for oats. Straw brought 675, 6d.,. and oat_ bales. 7us. to 725, 6d... per ton. Flour realised 525. to 575. per 2801 b., 2s. VA. to 2s. 41d. 'being the price, of thirds. The ,price of bran- and other offals was is. lid. to 2s. 2d. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Wood's Colleg,e Successes.</head>
        <p>Wood's Colleg,e Successes.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to ).-cord 100 per cent of passes in connection with a recent Special Advanced Shorthand Theory Examination con- ducted at Wood's College, and superintended by outside officials. 'The successful candidates were as follows :—Misses K. •M. Bouniithrey, A. M. Boumphrey, M. Thompson, K. Eddowes, E. Charnock. M. Cunningham, L. Hamilton Smith G. Powell, E. Knight, E. Broady, D. ilipkiss, E. Bates. Prizes: 1, E. Broady; 2, E. Knight.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Increased School .Attendance.</head>
        <p>Increased School .Attendance.</p>
        <p>In. view of the slight decrease in the Govern- ment grants, Mr. John Upson, at a meeting of the Education Committee, asked what the aver- age school attendance was at the present tinie. Mr. Edwin Plinston, the chairman of the School Attendance Sub-committee,. replied that there was an imProvement-not so large as they would like, but ictifi it was gradual, and they hoped it Would continue. The DirectOr of Education (Mr. J. Moore Murray) said the average attend- ance for January was 83.8 as against 81.7 for the corresponding month last year.</p>
        <p>To Relieve Overcrowding at a School. . The offer of the managers of the Ladies' School of Industry to provide temporary accom- modation in the St. Clement's Schoolroom in order to relieve the present overcrowding at the Ladies' School of Industry, .has been accepted by the Education Committee,. who are to pay the managers the sum of 2,35: per annum and any reasonable sum in addition that the managers may 'be compelled to expend upon the cost of fuel, lighting, cleaning, decorating, repairs, .a.nd the care of the premises; this arrangement to be ,subjeet to reConsideration at the end of twelve months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Transfer of Teachers.</head>
        <p>Transfer of Teachers.</p>
        <p>A number of temporary transfers of teachers in schools haying- been made in consequence of enlistment, the Education Committee, at *their meeting on Monday, approved of the same. Mr: IL T. Tairclough said that the transfer of a teacher from the Paipchial School to BealMrllit School had been • made • without the managers' sanction being sought, but the I)i.•,r Education (Mr. Moore Murray) ':.lid that he thought if the letter sent to the Managers were looked up it would be found- that the con- sent of the managers Was, asked for. He -added that it was no easy matter to arrange transfers,. as the men .were called up at a moment's notice.- t 'the' 'preSent• Moine&amp; they had ,two schools with four 'teachers short, and things had to be done very quickly. All transfers, however, were made with the sanction of the Managers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Fatal Fall Don Stairs.</head>
        <p>Fatal Fall Don Stairs.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at the Police Station on Thursday, by Mr. S. Brighouse, concerning the death of Wm. Broughton (04), deputy lodging- house keeper, of 3, Cloth. Hall Yard, who died in the Infirmary. on Tuesday from the effects .of a fall downstairs.</p>
        <p>• Deceased was formerly a puddler, and about six weeks ago complained of pains in the stomach. Though unwell, he continued to follow his employment. On Sunday morning, his brother, Richard, was sitting ,in the kitchen of the lodging-house, when he heard someone fall downstairs.. On going to the bottom of the stairs he found Broughton lying there in a dazed condition, bleeding from a wound at the back of the head.. Dr. Liston was summoned, and the injured man was removed to the Infirmary, where he died. Verdict: "Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>When Schools Will Break Up..</head>
        <p>When Schools Will Break Up..</p>
        <p>School children particularly will be interested in the fixing of the holidays as arranged by the School Management Sub-committee of the War- rington Education Committee as follows:— Easter : Elementary schools, Secondary School and Day School of Art, April 19th (afternoon) to May Ist. Ascension Day: Elementary schools, June ..Ist. Whitsuntide: Elementary schools, Secondary School and Day School of Art. June 9th (afternoon) to 19th. Midsummer: Element. ary schools, July 21st (afternoon) to August 21st; Secondary School and Day School of Art, July 21st (afternoon) to September 4th. Mid- term: Elementary schools, October 27th (after- noon) to November 6th; Secondary School and Day School of Art, October 27th (afternoon) to November Ist. Christmas: Elementary schools, Secondary School and Day School of Art, Decem- ber 22nd (noon) to January Bth.</p>
        <p>Funeral of Mr. Arthur E. Hamblen.</p>
        <p>We regret •to record that Mr. Arthur E. Hamblett has passed away at his residence, 43, Oldham-street, in his 47th year. Mr. Hamblett had been in the employ of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell for a period of 23 years. During the past 18 years he served in the range fitting de- partment, and has given valued and much-ap- preciated labours .on behalf of the firm. He has enjoyed the esteem of his fellow workmen in ,a marked degree, and will be much missed by those with whom•he has laboured. 116 was. an ardent angler, and was a member of several local angling societies. He succumbed to an attack of pneumonia. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Tuesday, amid Many tokens of sympathy and regret. The They. J. J. Firth conducted the service, and among the mourners were: Mrs. Hamblett (widow), Lizzie (daugh- ter). Arthur, Stanley and Ernest (solos), Alice Itarablett (sister), Charlie Hamblett, John Hamblett, George Hamblett (brothers), Sarah Thomas (sister-in-law), James Houghton and William Houghton (brothers-in-law), A. Hough- ton, A. Ilamblett and K. Hamblett law), Mr. E. Ditchfield, Mr. W. Thomas I (brothers-in-law), Mr. George Smith. The coffin was carried by his fellow workmen: Mr. George Cade, Mr. E. Shaw, Mr. E. Bailer, Mr. J. Thompson, Mr. A. HUlmes, and Mr. *. Whitfield. Wreaths and floral tributes were sent by: Wife and family, "Charlie and Annie." "Alice and Ted," "George and Rate." "Grandma,'? "Will and Sarah." "Jim and Annie," "Flo and Tom,” Mr. and Mrs. Cothaan. "Ethel," Mr. and Mrs. Salisbury, "Ellis and family," "Fel- low workmen, Fletcher, Russell's,"7."Neigh- bolus a.md friends," Black Boar, Royal Oak, and Roan C.pw Angling Societies. Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Deafness and Blindness.</head>
        <p>Deafness and Blindness.</p>
        <p>Mr. lon (of Ison's Eye and Ear Dispensary, Ltd.), 71, Great George-street, Leeds, has a re- markable record of success. His methods are up-to-date and without .opera.tion„.d,he effectstna ry ell CIS cures in; a ppOT,tkt)yr,ilijow,*B-4-05e91 Write for t,e stiniconia ~An 4 agkpiti tom• crf question, vteltit licst free,. .„Xrt.;,ls ,attends .at the Royal: Motel, Wigan„ Fiiday, March Ord, hours 3.30- to 7; Bull Hotel, Preston, Friday, March 3rd, hOUrse 11 to 2.—[Advt.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Friends' Lectures.</head>
        <p>Friends' Lectures.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. E. Pollard, of York, is to address a conference at the Friends' Meeting House this afternoon, and on Sunday evening he is to speak on " John Bright,: the Quaker Statesman."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Police Court Mission.</head>
        <p>The Police Court Mission.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission will be held at the Town Hall on Friday next. Among those who are to take pat i the Meeting are the Van. Arch- deacon Howson, and Sir William Scott Barrett.</p>
        <p>Change of Bank Hours. On and after Wednesday, Ist March, until further notice, the Stockton Heath Batiks will be open from ten o'clock to one o'clock daily, instead of ten o'clock to three o'clock, except on Saturdays, when the hours will be as before, viz., ten o'clock to twelve o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Social at Goiborne-street.</head>
        <p>Social at Goiborne-street.</p>
        <p>At the GOlborne-streetl3aPtist Church School- room on Thursday evening, a tea .and concert, which had been arranged by Mr.. A. • Spittle, took place. A large number of people .sat down to an excellent repast, which had been.provid6d by the male members of the church.:. The ladies . - in • charge of the tea tables were Mesdames Ogden, Few, Buckley, Pepper, Owen, Spittle. Cope, and Miss Richardson. After the • tables were cleared, a splendid 'concert as given, the Pastor, the Rev. IL Ogden,' presiding. Vocal items, much appreciated,., were rendered .by Messrs. N. Pea ref. and •A Hamnan, and the Misses M. and 11. -14ipkiss and Whitmore. • -13 y .special request a -duet was given by Messrs. -Pearce and Hannan. The .proceeds 'were .on behalf of the, church funds. The, accompanist was .Mr.</p>
        <p>Tanner.</p>
        <p>Ratepayers Before Others.. Having been told by the Clerk (Mr. A Bottomley) that the greater part of the tea which had been bought for the use of the Work- house inmates is 2s. per pound. the Rev. A. H. Mitchell. at the Board of Guardians' meeting on Wednesday, said' he knew a London firm which would supply them at is. 11d. per pound. •</p>
        <p>. . W. J. Shaw declared that for the sake of a penny per pound, it was- far better that local tradesmen should get the benefit. It was much more preferable for the uloney to be -spent in town than elsewhere. • -</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell replied that be took no exception to that dictum, all thitigs being equal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Warrington Winners at Widnes Eisteddfod.</head>
        <p>Warrington Winners at Widnes Eisteddfod.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes. Eisteddfod, held en Thursday, Miss Jeannie Armstrong (Mrs. Smallshaw), Arpley-street, Warrington, was.aw.ardecl the first prize (silver cup value £4 4s. and V)s. in.the challenge solo. Miss Armstrong gained 95 marks, the judge stating that she had a beauti- ful voice, the same quality through the whole song and of very extensive 'range. There was profound depth in the lower notes and she was quite brilliant in the upper .notes. There ,was• a blending of the technique in singing as well. as soulfulness. Miss Armstrong won the same competition in 1909. • Other Warrington winners wer*:—Chairbock. cover: I? Miss Cheetham. Ouion cover: 2, Mrs. -Hooper; 3 (equal) Mrs.. Fenton.. Challenge solo' .(under 16)) Albert Green, consolation prize. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>TO CHECK INTEMPERANCE., Bishop Chavasse's Call In The</head>
        <p>TO CHECK INTEMPERANCE., Bishop Chavasse's Call In The</p>
        <p>Name Of God.</p>
        <p>The Lord Bishop of• Liverpool, presiding over a one-day conference of his diocese on Monday, said of three serious probleins they had to face one was the present outburst of intemperance, especially amongst women. About this ',,rave evil there could be no doubt. Our drink bill went up by eight millions of pound's in the first half of 1915. So serious was the. danger that .111 r Lloyd George could spea of- the, three' great enemies Of our nnPire as. the 'Ger- mans, the Austrians, and the drink, and the greatest of these was the drink. He declared the drink menace to be more formidable than, the- submarine menace. It was interfering with the efficiency of our transports, with, the output Of munitions, with the discipline of our Army and of our Navy. Why Women Fall. The high wages earned, the exhaustion, caused by prolonged hours of work, and by, Sunday labour (the failure of which had vindi- cated in a most remarkable manner the faith and practice of those who believed that the. Sunday was a Divine institution), were DO dOilbiT active causes of this grave outbreak of exe-,s- sive drinking amongst men. Loneliness, anxiety for sons and husbands with the colours, the, possession of larger sums of money than they had ever handled in their lives, the ennui or idleness, and the craving .for excitement amid' drab, sordid surroundings, helped to promote. excessive drinking 'amongst women. At the. present moment, while the evil had. been les- sened' it had not been scotched.. We still had to fac'e the fact that; while convictions for- drunkenness had: decreased, there was still a large amount of excessive drinking at home, and this must be checked at all costs. LegisTa, tion could do much to lessen temptation, but unaided it could not make a nation sober. Now, was the opportunity for the -national Church to help to bring about a national social reforma- tion. With her churches, her clergy, her Sun- day schools in every parish, she could put forth her strength and grapple by personal example and personal suasion with individuals.. The Bishop's Lament.</p>
        <p>He did not ask the clergy and laity of the Church to become abstainers, -though he pro- foundly regretted that so many of the clergy and laity had failed to follow the King and to become abstainers during the duration of the war; but he did ask them in the name of God and as members of th 6 national Church to do sqmething more to check this evil than merely talk and lament in this most critical hour of our history. He called upon them to help their country at this critical time, to save thousand&amp; ef valuable lives, to insure the .victory of a righteous cause, and to deliver an imperial race from the shame, the bondage, and the imminent peril of excessive drinking. Dearth of Clergy.</p>
        <p>The Bishop also dealt with the serions dearth of candidates for holy orders and the over- work entailed on the clergy, and announced that it would be necessary to authorise lay readers to assist incumbents by conducting \services and preaching in the churches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Spiritual Apathy.</head>
        <p>Spiritual Apathy.</p>
        <p>But by far the most serious problem, he said, was the spiritual apathy of thousands of the people. While he deplored a lamentable lack .of thrift and a huge and mischievous waste of treasure, he saw signs even more dis- quieting. They were told that 'divorce had lately increased by 25 per cent., that juvenile crime was rapidly growing, and that the 'spirit of undiscipline was,,eating out our strength. We were obsessed with a sense of self-satisfaction and self-Complacency. We coMpared ourselves with other people, and boasted our superiority. We ,were kind and generous and ready .to help. one another. Were we so superior? Could we vie with the Germans in their eager patriotism and their, stern discipline? Were we girding ourselves 'for this conflict with the grim ree6- lution of the French whom we. deemed so frivolous, or were we turning to God with their whole-heartedness? God could not shorten the days of this war until we turned to Him. He could not give us victory until we were pre- pared to use it for His glory and the good of our fellow-men. We needed as a nation to wake up out of this stupor, and the awakening must begin in the Church. It was to promote. this awakening that the Archbishops of Canter- bury and York had decided that a national mission should be held throilghout England in October and November. They had appointed a Council of seventy bishops, clergy, and laity, including women as well as men, with the Bishop of London as chairman, to draw up- and submit to them a detailed plan of procedure. He commended the mission to their sympathy, co-operation. i.a.nd prayer-. •</p>
        <p>_ . 'oCrA(,}S%%nt4eTrisonPt E'liEM-Sinl44 Atle cipakl'.'lOf -the Warrington Training College, was elected a 'delegate to the Central Council of Diocesan Conferences.</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SIR ARTHIit:CROSPIEtII- AND THE</head>
        <p>SIR ARTHIit:CROSPIEtII- AND THE</p>
        <p>GERMAN HOUSE OF CARDS.</p>
        <p>When Will lit Collapse?</p>
        <p>NO FLINCHING BY THE ALLIES TILL</p>
        <p>THEY TRIUMPH.</p>
        <p>During an interval at a Liberal women's con- cert, held in the John Crosfield Memorial Hall on Tuesday evening, Sir Arthur Crosfield, Bart., delivered an interesting speech, in the course of -which he declared that well-informed people in London are of opinion that the war will not last through another winter..</p>
        <p>Lad? Crosfield was to have presided, but, as Sir Arthur explained, she did not think it right at the present- time to leave the wounded sol- diers' hospital at Highgate, of which silo is the commandant.</p>
        <p>LADY CROSFIELD AS HOSPITAL COMMANDANT.</p>
        <p>llrs. Osborne, the hon. secretary of the Wemen's Liberal Associatibn, said that the ting did not seem the same without her Yship, but it was gratifying to know that ~ila the Liberal • women of Warrington were `,?Illg their best in the way of sewing for sol- . s, their President was also engaged in noble " wk in: London. (Applause.) They ought to send her a message appreciation. (Applause.) . Sir Arthur began his speech by explaining that he did his best to persuade his wife to come North,- as she had not been for some months, but she felt that she ought not to do so yet.' "The hospital we are running at Highgate is a very great success,'.' he continued. "It is a great. comfort to our wounded soldiers to be able to get out of what I call the peasoup.of London into the fresh air to be obtained at Highgate. We are over 400 feet above the Thames level, and our poor wounded fellows enjoy the fresh air very much indeed, as a change from Lon- don. And so there is not a bed empty in the hospital. The men even take it in turns to come out from the large hospital which feeds ours, and my wife, who has a _great eye for detail, is heart and soul in the work which needs doing. Really, she has not a minute to spare from morning until night, for, besides 'other necessary duties, she gives all her time to the hospital.. This being so, she did not -.feel justi- fied .in coming. away for three or four days at the present time." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>LADS AT THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>THE LADS AT THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>Praise For Warrington Women</p>
        <p>Workers.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur, continuing, congratulated those Present on the work they were doing on behalf Of the lads of Warrington who were serving at the Front. In Lady CroSheld's name, as well as his own, he thanked the workers for the splendid way in which that work had been kept /IP month after month ever since the war began. The way the labour had been tackled was .evi- dence of the earnest and sympathetic spirit in which-the women of Old England had so Splen- didly played their part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WILL WAR CONCLUDE THIS YEAR?</head>
        <p>WILL WAR CONCLUDE THIS YEAR?</p>
        <p>Ever since the Outset of the war everybody had been wondering how long the awful busi- ness waste go on. None of us knew, but it was only human nature that we should speculate on it. There was a mast remarkable divergence of opinion among the best -informed, but if we took the average we found it was not expected in high quarters • that the war would last through next winter. The -Rnssian Foreign Minister had recently said that there could be no question of the war being finiShed in. the course of the present year, 'hut well-informed opinion in London .did not fll3 eiltite so gloomy a view, because that would be a grim outlook for all of us in.• England, although it would be a much grimmer and gloomier 'outlook for the criminals responsible for the outbreak—the Cen- tral Empires. (Hear, hear.) Besides those in London he was happy to know- that there were ‘Try well-informed and influential persons among our Allies and members of other nations who took the more sanguine view and believed that the tremendous efforts. Great Britain and her Allies were , making would result in vic- tory before the end of the 'slimmer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>TO END THE THING FOR ALL TIME.</head>
        <p>TO END THE THING FOR ALL TIME.</p>
        <p>But, after all, that was only speculation, for there was not one man in Europe who knew When the end -would' come. Probably, when it Came, it would be with great suddenness. When it began to fall, the House of Cards would. fall very quickly. Apart from all speculation, there was a much more important 'factor for our cause, indeed, for the whole future of eivilisation—there 'was the determination of every one of the Allies neither to flinch nor to show the sign of flinching until the cause had triumphed. (Applause.) Of course, that meant sacrifice beyond anything we or anyone else had ever seen, but how much wiser to make an end of the thing,' not just for a day, but for all time, 'while we were about it? There were those who took a more pessimistic view than that, saving that whatever the Allies might achieve now, the combative instincts tlf poor human nature would result' in more battles in Europe in generations to come. But lie was thankful he could take a more -hopeful view. There was plenty of tragedy in history, and plenty of it to-day, but, after all; in .the pages of history was written progress which justified us on counting on progress in the future. (Hear, hear.) Black as were the crimes of Prussian mili- tarism, he thought the blackest record of all was their refusal to lift even a finger to save the 800,000 unfortunate Armenians who had per- ished at the hands of the Unspeakable Turk, but he no more believed that our descendants would ever see a,repetition of this hellish work, which was such a stigma upon civilisation to- day, than that America would ever have to face such a struggle as she. did 50 years. ago, when tlip. North and South took up arms,against each, ofEer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>CARE FOR OUR FIGHTERS</head>
        <p>CARE FOR OUR FIGHTERS</p>
        <p>"No, we have got to make an end of the job this time," declared the speaker. "When the right cause has triumphed, the greatest, highest and most sacred duty of European- statesman- ship will be to see to it that such a settlement IS made that will rule out the possibilities of 411 Y repetition of what we have to-day, not Merely for this generation, but for all those to 3nle." (Applause.) The human coukage which -uad been shown in the war exceeded what any I:Ilan might have thought possible, and, with our whole hearts, ive could regard Our fellows k4hting at the Front not as a hero here anti there. but OA a race of heroes. (Applause.) Ile WaS thankful there was no weakening in the efforts to nurse and provide for those who were- twounded in the terrible fray. That was why. me, congratulated the Liberal' women of War- on the work they were doing. -(Ap- Pla,use.) ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CONCERT.</head>
        <p>THE CONCERT.</p>
        <p>• The concert at which Sir Arthur Crisfield spoke followed a tea, enjoyed by, the Latchford Ward section of the Liberal Women's Sewing Class. Well over one hundred ladies were pres- ent..—Being ward captain, Mrs. Felix Maginn had char.* of the arrangements, and .was, 4ssisted by the following auxiliary workers, besides members of the committee: Mesdames Newell, Wright, Gough, liulse, and O'Brien. Thoee on the platform at the concert were: Mrs. Pemberton, Mrs. Osborne, Mrs. O'Brien, and Mr. and Mrs. Felix. Maginn. The various items submitted were greatly ap- preciated, the artistes being Mrs..E. A. Sanders, the Misses Kilduff, Miss Davenport, Mr. Kilduff, Qnd Mr. H. M. Ashton. A vote of thanks to all who had taken part ProPo9ed by Mr. Felix Magian. They all 11Y. he said, of the excellent work Sir Arthur .144 Crosfield are doing, and hoped Ihat conditions would soon change so that we coulo have Sir Arthur with wftelierle Yrgplird-Wttfi ItePlYing, ,Sir Arthur. zerPtssexilithir• Mat' action all felt at the improved 'health of seeretarY, Mrs. Osborne, and added his con- gratulations to Mrs. Maginn on the success of 'the gathering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
