      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>OUTLAW NATIONS.</head>
        <p>OUTLAW NATIONS.</p>
        <p>Will Allies Combine Against</p>
        <p>Central Powers ?</p>
        <p>THE .FUTURE PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>An important address, in which he touched Upon the problems tq be faced after the wax, was delivered by Sir Arthur Crosfield, Bart., Thursday evening in the 'same hall, on the occasion of the tea meeting and concert of the Howley and St. Austin's Wards. Sir Arthur began by saying he was. 4ure we were all doing our bit in our small way and. 'would keep on doing all we could, but what- ever we and the whole nation and Empire could do we should never be able to pay the debt we all owed to the men who had gone to the Front. kApplause.) - Sir Arthur also had a word to say about thrift, a subject upon which the Secretary (Mrs.. Osborne) had been speaking to the ladies. The principles of thrift were what we should all have to lay to heart .if the war lasted much longer, he said. 'lle was very, glad 'the question was ,being brought wisely to the fore in old Warrington, for before many. years were over we might all have to recognise the need of it in a 'very practical way. "Yesterday I was privileged to meet a good many of our wounded soldiers," he went' on, "and in most cases conversation, at their own desire, turned upon the old question as to how long this awful war is going to go on,, when we shall have brought the Huns to their Senses and .their knees. Sooner or later there will come a time when' the self-interests.-of the German nation. will overcome the pride of the German nation. (Hear,' hear.) I have no more idea than you have what will be the length of this awful tragedy, but we will all keep our pecker up. (Laughter and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>GERMAN AUTOCRATS.</head>
        <p>GERMAN AUTOCRATS.</p>
        <p>"If it depended entirely or mainly on the decisiop of those who are at the head of affairs in Germany, then, indeed, should take a very gloomy view of things, but I firmly believe the day will come when it. will not entirely rest with them. They are autocrats in the -worst sense of the word. We used to know autocrats in Old England, and we had te deal with them in the last resort by stopping, the flow, of money on which they depended for carrying out their policy. And it is Something to be hoped for that when the tide has begun to fiow etrongly in favour of the Allies there may come a day when the middle classes of Germany will say; 'We are now face to face with- ruin, even with national ruin. We have' spent and squandered more than enough blood and treasure and we are not content to .go on and face national annihilation.' That is the one prospect you and have to work for, and which our brave fellows and those of the Allies are working for." (Applause.) The most hopeful sign to-day was the abso- lute unity of opinion and feeling and the in- flexible determination on the part .of .every one of the Allies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>TO SAFEGUARD THE FUTURE.</head>
        <p>TO SAFEGUARD THE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>"When we have achieved our object—an op, jeet so well 'described by the Prime Minister On Wednesday "—proceeded Sir Arthur, "what is going, to follow? I raise'the ,point #ithout fte slightest idea of ,atternbting any premature forecast,of the exact character or the extent ot the measures that may have to be . taken in order to safeguard the Allied nations, including the whole of the British Enipire, against any possibility of a repetition of this horrible crime. It is• as indispensable for Europe to make a. repetition impossible as it would be unthinkable for the great Republic of the West to repeat the -awful tragedy they had to face between4B6l and 1864. What Steps, therefore,' are we going to take to guard against it? I will only submit to you this simple Proposition that when the war is brought to a Conclusion the chief object and, the higher achievement open to the statesmanship of 'the civilised world —I use the .word civilised advisedly, because we. are tackling 'barbarism now .just as much as the -Roman _Empire had to tackle it; and just as the Roman Empire went down before it we have got to see that this time civilisation ,is uppermost and that barbarism has to bite the dust—will be. to make a repetition of the tragedy impossible. (Applause.) "I am not going to enter upon the many means that may be taken to, bring that about. One might say at once that the old, unfair advantages that Germany has had•over many of her neighbours in trade, for •instance, in matters 'of freight and rail-transport, will be things of the past; .but we shall go far beyond that in the measures that are taken. I am certain that there will be a' strong feeling among all the Allies, including the dominions ot .the British -Empire, .that. so far as we can cement the union of to-day amongst ourselves it will be a good thing. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>GERMANY THE VOLUNTARY OUTCAST. "If you go back a few years, there was a time when we thought, and, I believe, with every good reason, that we might count upon the Kaiser having at least too much sense to yield to the Chauvinists and Junkers of Ger- many. At that time there seemed a fair chance of a gradual rapprochement, so that we could have taken Germany and Austria into an international settlement that would have made criminal warfare an impossibility. In those days Great Britain did all that could be done. While teaching Germany that Europe would not allow her to ride roughshod over her ncigh- bours, we, at the same time, did all that was possible here to help gradually to improve the relations between Germany and her neighbours and there was a chance that, if the-Kaiser had kept his head, or if democratic Govern- ment in Germany had come to be something like a reality, this utterly unnecessary and foolish ,cata,strophe, to say nothing of its fright- ful criminality, might have been averted. But that has all gone and there can be no com- prehensive settlement including Germany and Austria. •</p>
        <p>"I know very well when the tide turns there may be developments, and very surprising de- velopments, in the internal affairs -of Ger- many and Austria, and I know that these developments may, and probably will, affect to a certain extent the character of the ultimate settlement, but, at the same time, you know the old saying, 'Once bitten, twice shy,' and so we are not going to take any risks with the people who are guilty of this awful crime. In- stead of a settlement which would have em- braced all the modern nations of the West, we shall have to depend upon the co-operative action of the Allies and the British Empire in order to keep these two nations in °rip in the future." (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WORKERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Osborne •remarked that whilst they could not have Lady eras&amp;ld with them, they were very pleased to have Sir Arthur, who was always assured of a hearty welcome. He also was devoting a considerable amount of• his time, energy and money to the cause we had at hwirt—the overthrow of the Huns and the establishment of peace. (Applause.) The tea was prepared by Mrs. G. S. Pendle- bury (ward captain), and the following auxil- iaries besides the committee :—Miss Birch, ch Mes:mes Ridgeway, Vose, Galley, Aughton and A. Percival.</p>
        <p>Among those on the platform were Alderman' Tinuion, MesdarneS:Osbornei:PendleburY, lins, Dutton, Shaw,-;l9fattims-14(1'Plicival. , Alderman Tinhitd r3NittiSKl,4'N'Ote of thanks to 'the,. artistes, '-cohttibirted anex64loil musical progranitue,-.-Mrs.- tuhney, the.Miks4W Winstau/Oy., Jones, and Perdue, Mr. ..Tames Turton and Mr. Cain, with Mr. Ernest Wilkes and Mr. P. Pendlebury as accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEIRUARY 26, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEIRUARY 26, 1916.</p>
        <p>T4.RRliiiiiiilL:lloll OF - HONOUR. -</p>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>SERGEANT J. E. IVEBR.</p>
        <p>One Of Three Soldier-Brothers Struck • By Shell.</p>
        <p>Mr. James E. Webb, 25, Oldham-street, Latch.. ford, has received news that one of his three soldier sons has *been wounded while serving in •Yrance With the. local Territorials. He is Sergeant James Edward Webb, and Was wounded by a. shell while helping to repair a communi- cation trench.</p>
        <p>_ A letter, written on the 16th inst. by Second- Lieutenant T. G.. Tickner, or z:ie 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, has _been received, by Mrs. Webb. It runs as follows :—" lam sorry to Say that your son, Sergeant E. Webb, has been slightly Wounded out *, here. to-day. I was only two yards away at the time it hap- pened. We were repairing a communication trench when a few unexpected shells came over Your son, always .most helpful, was assisting</p>
        <p>*</p>
        <p>W6unded.</p>
        <p>me to remove ihe men aWay along the trench. when the last shell dropped „close to us,.. and a piece wounded your son. Of course, we very soon got him • bandaged and in the hospital, where be was Made ,as comfortable as _possible under the .circumstances. Captain Dickson and the other officers of ..the company join with me in sending to you and Sergeant Webb our sym- pathy- in this misfortune." • Sergeant .Webb, who is well known in War- rington, is 28 years of age. He has been in the Territorials for several y•ears; and was mobilised immediately upon the outbreak,of war. Previous to this time he was employed by Messrs.- Jos. Crostield and Sons, Ltd., with whom he had •been for a number of years. • He was very, fond. of football, and, indeed, ,was himself a good player, being'captain of the Latchford ,F.C., and et one time also a Member .of F.C. Ho also wert in for boxin.'lwo Ober. t.r(itters are serving in H.M. Forces, one in the A.S.C., andthe other in the R.E.</p>
        <p>...• •• • y • g • :.:•••••.., • ••• ••• • " • • • . , ' .W;;;::• -:: • •:)•••:,•', •</p>
        <p>SERGEANT J. E. WEBB.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>RADDON COURT HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>RADDON COURT HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Juveniles Help To Swel! Funds By Successful Concert.</p>
        <p>• It is usually adults who are expected to play prominent Parts in the cause of alleviating the sufferings of our afflicted heroes, yet the juvenile section of the community have not .been back- ward in the work of raising funds for our insti- tutions. At least one found such to he the case at a successful concert held in the Latchford Parish Rooms on Thursday- evening. The room wa.s packed and a varied programme was heartily appreciated by those who were present. Pro- grammes were One penny each, and they were worth it, for some cartoonist had done 'a. clever bit of work in a drawing entitled '" Watch your step," evidently from the revue 'of the same name. The artist has a future before him. . Of the concert itself, one might say that 'finan- cially and vocalry it was a great success. The stage, was occupied by about thirty juyeniles, all of whom •acquitted themselves in' praise- worthy, manner. Opening with a chorus entitled "There's a land," the whole' of the Reins re- ceived applause thoroughly merited. The homorous recitation by Master John Smith was very effective. Costume songs and dances by the children called for unstinted 'applause, espe7 cially the patriotic finale. Madame 'Lily Fair- hurst,. the talented vocalist, was at her best in "An Englishman's Home," and the solo effects of Ur. W. 11. Rigby, the conductor, were equally praiseworthy. The aceompanist for the evening was Master Ernest Hannan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
