      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>ARPLEY CANNONS.</head>
        <p>ARPLEY CANNONS.</p>
        <p>Should They Be Dismounted?</p>
        <p>Our attention has been drawn by a kentlemln in an eminent position in the town to the ad= visability of removing the Russian cannons .on the Crimean. Memorial near Arpley Station. The cannons have an honourable history; but we agree with thR suggestion that in view .of the friendly alliance with our former enemy, emblems, which marked a cOnflice of -amis. be- tween TIS, Might .now very ,snitibly pass into oblivion.</p>
        <p>The guns were erected at the cast of the late Sir Gilbert Greenall,,and it is ass'unied -that the. pregent 1 offvr no. obectiofi to ttieir- j/6)13,0i-a . .7, .1 st 1.. r. . t...)</p>
        <p>• Cotdhe contrhAryot ililbert might ihterest 111 m4el f thobtalning, for the town one or more German guns, wliich everybody in the town would dearly like to see placed there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>KILLED,</p>
        <p>TROOPER GILBERT DAYUS.</p>
        <p>One Of Tho Soldier-Brothers Meets Ills Fate In France.</p>
        <p>News has reached Mr. Dayus, who resides at 223, .„Liverpool-road, Sankey, that his"- son, Trooper Gilbert Dayus, of the 2nd Dragoon Guards, has been killed by a rifle grenade,</p>
        <p>The news was conveyed in a letter written by a lieutenant, who also stated that he helped to bury Trooper Dayus, and erected a wooden cross at the head of the grave. • Trooper Dayus, Who was 20 years of age, was, before enlisting, an employe of Messrs. Gar- nett's, cabinet-makers, late of Warrington. He occupied the rank of Colour-Sergeant in the Church Lads' Bridage, and was a member of the Sankey Church choir. He was fond of run- ning and football, and played the latter game with the Penketh F.C. Me was Rd flea ted at St. Mary's day school, Sankey. Another brother</p>
        <p>The photographs are of Lieutenant-Com- mander V. E., Bridgwateri. R.N., and Second- Lieutenant S. E. Bridg-water, Sherwood Forvters, ,nephews of Mr. Joseph Bridgwater, agent Ship Canal Dertartment, Howley Quay, Warrington, and, sos,of Mr. S. E. Bridgwater, We,ston-Siipep•Mare. . were stude:ilts of. the Stourbridge. Gram- ; lua on leaving,. which Lieitterkftnt7Com, Bridgwater jined tho Nay. AfOr lerni Of preparatory training at Devonpoit- 'fook-Aip duty on a man-o'-war.. He has recently been home, but is no* with his ship. a flotilla leader, of which he is commanding officer. His wife performed the ceremony of naming his ship.</p>
        <p>Hon at the Sacred Heart School. The fallen soldier was of a quiet and retiring disposition, yet withal was pNasant, courteous and of engaging manner to 'all *ho came in contact with him. .Up .to theiresent there is a lack .or news as to how he - met his: death, , But the Rev. Basil Churchward (chaplain) writes:— Dear MA. Kinder,—l am very sorry to have. to tell you .that your nephow, Rifleman S. Ban- kinson, ,was killed on February 13th, during the night. I am writing to you as your name was given as his next of kin. He was, I think, killed immediately, so would have no pain. He was buried' on Monday night in a little cemetery in the•shelter of the Yser. Canal bank. His grave will be marked with a cross and you will always be able to obtain information as to its exact locality by writing to Officer Commanding, headquarters, Graves Registration- Commission. B.E.F. You .may like to know that •some• snow: drops-ond other flowers have'been.plaCed on the .grave. May God comfort and help you. in this sorrow. - Hard as the loss will be to -hear, you will feel proud that your nephew was amongst those who have given their all .for the, safety and .honour Of the folks at home.. If 'there is anything- else I can tell you, I shall only be too glad to tell you."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Comrade's Letter.</head>
        <p>A Comrade's Letter.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Edward Handlow, a comrade in the same- regiment, writing to his parents, who re- side at 31, 'Powys-street, writes ; "I saw in 'The Examiner' of 'Billy' Keating winning the _D.C.A.I., and of how he won it. Good luck to him. I wish I could meet him out herf,'butl have tried to find out where they are, without success. • • I suppose you have heard about poor Sammy Hankinson,being killed. It was a blow to all of us when we heard of it, there, wasn't a better lad anywhere. ,He was liked by everybody, but I suppose it must have been his time to go. Poor Sammy!" Rifleman Hankinson's father. Thomas Hanliin- son, is at Present serving the Colours in Prance, after having been wounded on two occasions. Prior to going to reaside at Wellington, Salop, he was employed at Messrs. The "Whitecross Co</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>RIFLEMAN A. B. JOHNSON.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN A. B. JOHNSON.</p>
        <p>Meets His Death In Attempting To Rescue Wounded Comrade.</p>
        <p>Rifleman A. .Barnes _Johnson, of the .Rifle Brigade,, Son of Mr. and Mrs. Johnson,, 243, Lovely-lane, has been killed at the Front while assisting in an attempt to rescue a - wbunded comrade. .The news was conveyed in a.letter. written by Corporal James Taylor, of the Rifle Brigade, As follows:</p>
        <p>am deeply sorry' to inform- you that your son was killed in action on Sunday, the lath inst. -.lie was a brave, good lad, and died a hero's death:. He will be..greatly mimed by his pals,' for he was a bright, cheerful lad, and well liked. During last Saturday and Sunday, we Were subject to much heavy shelling and German attacks: Barnes helped greatly to keep them back, and we kept ouvline intact.- He was bombing • all through Saturday .morning, and he. came through all right. • On Sunday affernoon he volunteered to go with me and two others in order to carry a wounded comrade back to safety-. As we were going a. shell came, and a piece hit him. lie turned to me and said 'I am hit, Jim.' I started to bandage him, but. he passed -away at once.- Perhaps you will be pleased to</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN A. B,JOHNSON. • , Killed in Action.</p>
        <p>. Second-Lieutenant Bridgwater, who is now in the thick of our fighting somewhere, has had-an equally exciting career, and is quite, at. home with a gun, which he has used with good effect for King and country. On,tha outbreak of the' Boer War he served • with the Imperial Yeornanry, .saw much . active. :ff,rvica ,in ‘k1,1,t,74N14.tct1it,413 3y11iqh,..,* 19X idl4ool.;t ccl,ll/AN4F4CnJier ,j ~re.tUTR .to_trlexyla:,anAl .business.ia t but on-the outbreak of the present:s w,ax,..7110: again Offered his services in his country's 'de- fence, and soon found himself a Sub-Lieutenant' in the Sherwood Foresters. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>BROTHERS-IN-ARMS ON LAND AND SEA.'</head>
        <p>BROTHERS-IN-ARMS ON LAND AND SEA.'</p>
        <p>Nephews Of Mr. Joseph Bridgwater Who Are Serving Their King.</p>
        <p>4ft)*11,44i 4MPO:47OIVIL.</p>
        <p>LIEUTENANT-COMMANDER F. E. . BRIDWATER.</p>
        <p>LIEUTENANT S. E. BRIDGWATER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PR IVATE J. CDLQING</head>
        <p>PR IVATE J. CDLQING</p>
        <p>A Soldier Who Did His Duty AA ell.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Golding, of 60, Earl-street, ,has received news that his youngest brother, Private John Golding, of the 10th Lancashire Fusiliers, was killed in action in France on the 14th:inst.</p>
        <p>Private Golding, who was 28 years of age, en- listed in. August, 1914, at which time he lived with: his mother, who died last montlh lie was, before enlisting, employed by the British Pro- ducts Company. Langford, having previously been an employe of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. He was edu- cated at St. Benediet's School, and worshipped at St. Benedict's • Church. Bowling claithed most of his .spare time. lie was keenly inter- ested in the game, and 'played regularly With the Plough Inn Bowling Club,, Houghton Green, and had the satisfaction of winning the closing main there in 1913. This was one of the most popular wins since the inception of. the club, ..eight years ago, as he was quite a novice at the time. -</p>
        <p>-Writing on the 19th inst., to the late Private's brother, a Company-Sergeant-Major -stated "It is ..with sincere sorrow that I *rite to in- form - you Of the heroic .death of your brother, Private Golding of my company. .It is. a son- „row endured by 'both officers and men. Private Golding was a good and cheerful soldier, and did</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN GOLDING. Killed in Actin!.</p>
        <p>his duty well. It is a pride to us to know that Lancashire films out such men. He is buried just behind the firing line, and, being a Catho- lic myself, I-said a short prayer over the grave in which he lies with several comrades.. I feel for you in your sorrow, but it will .be some consolation to know that he died fighting for his country."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Good "Pal."</head>
        <p>A Good "Pal."</p>
        <p>A comrade,. Laprence Hannan, wrote: "Just a few lines relating to the death of your .poor brother John, who was killed while helping to repulse a big attack on our lines on the 14th inst. I know this sad news will be a great shock to you all, but I hope you will bear up in your sad hiss, for all the boys of his platoon knew .he was a- quiet lad, a good ` pal ' to all, and game to the end. There were five of us in the traverse at the time your brother inet.his death. Two weed killed and one wounded with the same shell. John was hit in the head with a piece of the Fihrapnel and was killed instantly. He wOuld have been going on furlough in a few days, and on the morning of the bombardment we were talking together. He was telling me that, he would like to see the new Catholic Church Of St. Benedict's. He died doing his duty 'faithfully 'and,, well, and yini _have Jost a good brother 'and I, a good par! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>CRIMEAN VETERAN.</head>
        <p>CRIMEAN VETERAN.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Who Has Been Bedfast</p>
        <p>Since King's Visit.</p>
        <p>A succession of stroies' having made him lid- fast for nearly three years, Sergeant William Cummings, a Crimean veteran, entered into his last long rest on Wednesday evening. lie died at his home, 9,t Wellfield-street, and at his funeral will be accorded the military honours worthy of an old and heroic soldier:</p>
        <p>Cummings enlisted in the 95th Foot Regiment just before the Crimean War, and was in one of the first transports to go to the scene of opera- tions. Difficult, arduous, and trying as was the campaign, both from the violence 'of the Russians, the scarcity of equipment and in- clement climatic conditions, Cummings came through unscathed and in good health. After a. time in England hfa went out to India for 14 years, and then returned to Warrington, where he continued service with the South Lancashire Militia until 1891, :when he retired on his pension. .</p>
        <p>Cuminings was a born soldier. Strong, and having the adaptability necessary for milftary, life under all conditions, he had a successful career. In all, he spent 36 consecutive years under the Colours. - At one time he .held a higher rank than that of a Sergeant, relinquish- ing the "position of Colour-Sergeant at his own .request. Office work was against his nature. Tie was all for the parade ground, and manceuvres in the field. A Sad Coincidence.</p>
        <p>It was a singular and regrettable coincidence that. Cummings had his first stroke on the 6th July, 1913, the d 4 on which King George V. and 'Queen Mary visited Warrington. For weeks he had been looking forward to being presented to tkeir Majesties, along With the</p>
        <p>~A., • TIM LATE SERGEANT V. CUMMINGS.</p>
        <p>other heroes of :bygone days; But just as he was about to leave the house illness suddenly came upon him, and he had to be carried to bed, where he has remained ever since, attended by his wife, who has the additional honour of being the daughter •of an Indian Mutiny veteran—Colour-Sergeant Bowers, of Gloucester. She herself was born in the East Indies , Tint ..&amp;he,..etcea d in Warrington, and was Ittlai‘ifeo t 6, him 8 years ago. 'DV the late Sergeant cu... '43 beriiilrttrin the 'verpool " Palgt tio;? •serVinOt, in Prance. Previous ~tp,.‘.the, war he worked at Messrs. Crosfieia's works as an electrician, but has also 14 years' service in the Navy to his credit.</p>
        <p>Atit</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>t G – CHILDREN</head>
        <p>t G - CHILDREN</p>
        <p>Give Dramatic Account Of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>War.</head>
        <p>The War.</p>
        <p>FINE PATRIOTIC DEMON-</p>
        <p>STRATION.</p>
        <p>The Parr [Tall was crowded on Thursday night by a highly appreciative audience who enjoyed to tile full a novel and delightful, enter- tainment. provided by the children attending, St. James' Mixed School and Fairfield Girls''School. The comprehensive title," A Dramatic Account of the War in Prose, Poem and Song," indi- cates the nature. of the programme, and the ambitious nature of the undertaking was more •than justified. On the spacious platform of-the hall a chorus of children .nearly 300 strong formed a charming background to a !picture in Which the diminutive principals told, the story of the great tragedy by which their 'young liver, have been darkened,, a story that, nevertheless, has its glorious side, upon which they dwelt with telling effect. , 'A war conceived by criminal minds and launched upon an unsuspectingivorld with 'criminal intent, resisted by pure _ motives and a determination to save -our homes, the world and Civilisation at large from the blight- ing hand of the Hun and the .modern Atilla, when_ properly ,depicted, as it was on this' occa- sion, has a lesson for those who , depict it so earnestly, and the' good the enterprise will do as an incitement to patriotic feeling and en- deavour.. in the rising generation cannot be over-estimated. To the older people who com- posed the audience the story the children told had an elevating effect; they came away more convinced than' ever Of the justice of our cause and the need for the sacrifices we are indi- vidually and collectively making for our Motherland.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stansfield, head master of St. James' Schools and Mrs. Stansfield, head mistress of Fairfield School, and their staffs, are to be coa- gratulated upon the high state of efficiency to which they had brought all the artistes. There was not a halt or a hitch throughout the whole long evening; the prompter had not to inter- vene on a single occasion; And all the work was, performed in first-class style. His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) expressed the opinion at the close that it was one of the best entertainments he had ever attended, and many 'present would share in his approval. After the payment of expenses there will be at least £5O to hand over to -the funds of the Y.M.C.A. and the Church Army Recreation Huts for soldiers, in aid of which the entertainment was given.</p>
        <p>The programme.. was divided into sections, each complete and well thought out. It open&amp;l with the song "At the Thought of Britain's Glory," which formed a suitable introduction to the section. "The Eve of the War," where the hopes and fears of the nation iluring the fateful moments when the answer to Britain's ultimatum to Germany. in the name of Belgium was awaited, were expressed in prose by Minnie Bailey and Alice Parr. The failure of our effort for peace concluded with the hymn "0 Lord of Hosts."</p>
        <p>"Our Navy Prepared" was then revealed in prose recitations by Brian Dunbavand and Edna Baldry. This passage was illumined by a tableau assembled in front Of the choir as "Rule, Britannia," was being sung. It was Britannia surrounded by representatives of the four countries forming the homeland, the' char- acters, in. national .costume;; being, taken as follows: Britannia, Nora Holbrook; England, Gladys Nicholls and Mary Brown; Scotland, Gladys Kenwright and Carrie Hickenbottom; Ireland, Edna Burn and Hilda Williams; Wales, Laura Buckley and Nora -Pucill. There was a realistic hornpipe, too, in which Frank Boyle and Cyril Boyle did a hornpipe that would have given a real Jack Tar, had one . been ik-esent, something to think about .*The neatlkAdressed sailors who. assisted in this' de- partment Were: Leonard Sykes, Fred Tole, Sid- ney Dykes, Hy. Ward, Cyril Wood, Thomas Harris, Ed. Clough, Geo. Lawrinson, Frank Boscow, Sidney Hamblett, Hy. Gleave, Wm. Sutch, Ivor Eckersley, Arthur Bibby, Edward Rafter and Robert Large. "Your King and %Country Need You," the section telling of the call and the way in which the call has been met, .was introduced, still in prose recitations, by Clara Clough and Marian Duckworth, a stirring 'element • being added by " way- of a song and mart*, "Drum' Taps," and militarydrill by members of the ,St. Elphin's Company of the Church Lads' Brigade. A double duet and chorus, "Admirals All," in which the principals were Minnie Lester, Violet, Molyneux, Florence Wei and Maggie Ashton, was a brilliant finish td this passage. "The Response of the Empire" was explained in verse by Evelyn Farrer, and was further illustrat4d by an augmentation of the Britan- • nia tableau by the addition of representatives in khaki of .our Dominions oversea, who natur- ally were highly popular with all parts of the audience. These ;children, who came from all the corners of the earth to shield their mother, and assembled tot&amp; singing of "One King, One • Flag, One Fleet," were portrayed by: Cana- dians, Arthur Bowden and Fred Woodhe.ad ; Cape Colonists, Ronald Webster and Stanley - Gorst; Hindoos, Stanley Smith and Wilfred Jackson; New Zealander, J: Ellison; Australian, Frank' Ellison. The Allies were: Belgians, John Pevitt and ••., W. Grainger; French, Herbert Brown and Wil- liam Prince; Russians, J. Buckley and John • Iligham; Italians, Thomas Brannon and J. Ver- non. , . ,</p>
        <p>"The Part Played by Belgium" was explained • in prose by Florence Sutch and Emma ..krstall;,.. and the song "Men of .Brave Liege" was fol- lewed:by the Belgian National Anthem. ~ • 'Evelyn Farier told the story of "Mons .and Its'Effect on. the Country" in prose, the'Clinrch. Lads' Brigade sounded "The Last Post," 'and'," Miss Nora '..Brock, a Fairfield. Sam': teach, recited ". A. Tale of British 'Heroism," The Viking 'Song conOuding•the-,Section. "The Part Played by France" was explained ' by 'Gerald Rainford and Jessie Berry in prose, and the Fiench National Anthem was sung. "Christmas in the Trenches, 1914," when to soldiers made their irregular truce, wal4 described in prose by Marjorie Weston; and carols and old English country dances were in- troduced, Gladys Shaw being, the 'soloist in the carol "Peaceful' Night." , "The Part Played by Russia" Was recited in prose by Emma Arstall and Bertha Melbourne, and-the Russian National -Anthem was Sung. • • Edith Glover and Dora Mitchell ,recited in prose "How Italy Entered the War," and Gari- baldi's Hymn followed. "The Prince of Wales," recited by Eric Clifs ton and Alice Burgess, was 'followed by the song and chorus, "God Bless the Prince of Wales," in which the Soloist was Miss Emily ,Taylor, an old scholar of 'Fairfield School.</p>
        <p>Phyllis Ontram outlined "The Part Played by Warrington," and the long programme was brought to a close by the song and tableau. "The Soldiers' Chorus"; the song, '"Land of Hope an Glory," in • which the soloist *as Madame SePhton, an old scholar of St. James' School; and the National Anthem. Miss Gandy and Miss /Mode, Grant. were th 6. accompanists.</p>
        <p>MAGAZINES AND NOTEPAPER FOR SOLDIERS..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Nlear Of Padgate's Appeal.</head>
        <p>Nlear Of Padgate's Appeal.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have just received a letter from the Rev. 11. F. S. Collier, vicar of Padgate, who is with the British Expeditionary Force in France as chaplain with the South Lancashires. He appeals to me to send him notepaper, envelopes, indelible pencils, magazines, &amp;c., for the use of the men—many of whom Art fpm Warrington. I'l Anil- be ikeased-'44eeeefrcels, or money ancll44 1;411141144a f?s.4lldly in- se glttlils let it, m,</p>
        <p>(Rev.) J. HIRST, , Diocesan Special Service Chaplair. Padgate Vicaraig,</p>
        <p>February 23rd, 1916.</p>
