      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>%•ij 4_4.trE t 1- titi ta Air</head>
        <p>%•ij 4_4.trE t 1- titi ta Air</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS AND DRINKING • s FACILITIES.</p>
        <p>t</p>
        <p>Sir,—Does Mr. Simpson really wish to put for- ward the plea that reduced facilities do not de- crease drunkenness? If so, he is too late, for e.ven the "Brewers' Gazette" of September 24th, 1914—seventeen months ago— told us the follow- ing :--" A. transformation of the night scenes of London has followed from the closing of the public-houses at 11. .. . The police found very little intoxication.to deal with." I believe Mr. Simpson hails from Liverpool. Is he not satisfied. with the report of the City Licensing Committee, published on January 14th, 1916? In a five months' comparison, August to December, 1914 and 1915, it shows a decrease of 44 per cent. I know it calls atten- tion to the danger of increase of home drinking, but Mr. Simpson, if he longs to do something for his country's good, should suggest to the Control" Board some niethod whereby this danger might 'to overcome, and so increase the good which is. -,being done by them. If nothing short of pro- hibition will do it, then lot us have prohibition, at any rate, during the .war period. As to why three towns with 702 licences have fewer arrests for drunkenness than three towns with 493 licences, probably police activity or indifference ig responsible; hence the Rome Office Order referred to on Friday last week at the Warring- ton roliee Court by the Chief Constable. BENJ. T. PARKES. R, Willis-street, WarHngtr-n.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>FOR POOR IND MED.</head>
        <p>FOR POOR IND MED.</p>
        <p>Enjoyable Concert At The</p>
        <p>Whitecross Hospital.</p>
        <p>. On Saturday evening a concert and variety entertainment, arranged by Staff-Sergeant F. Winkley, 8.A.M.C., one of the inmates of the Whitecross Military Hospital, was given in the Dining Hall of the Workhouse buildings to the inmates and a number of sick and wounded soldiers from the hospital. The variety and concert party were under ,the direction of Mr. J. W. Pearce, himself one of the entertainers, and the kindly efforts of the combined company to enliven the lot of the Workhouse inmates and amuse the military con- valescents, were highly successful, as was testi- fied by frequent rousing applause and many encores. Amongst the audience were Mr. and Mrs. W. Furness, Mr. A. Bottomley, Miss Broadbent, and Mr. and Mrs. Loxhara, the master and matron.</p>
        <p>Four lady vocalists of considerable ability sang a number of songs, which are just now favourites in the concert room. The contribu- tions of this character by Miss Annie Kenny, Madame Marie Wilkinson, Madame Nelly Hall. and Miss Dorothy Darling were mostly songs of graceful sentiment, familiarity with which seems powerless to breed feelings c•ther than tose of welcome. The singing of "Just a song at twilight" by Miss Kenny, and "Up from Zummerzet " by Miss Wilkinson are merely in- stanced as examples of the excellent manner in which many items of this kind were given. Laughter-makers.</p>
        <p>Several comedians of the exuberantly gay, as well as of the melancholy order, also contributed vocal items, which gave rise to much merriment. One did not know whether to laugh the more at the doleful lucubrations of Mr. Harry Dunworth or the broader comedies of Mr. Ernie Milton and Mr.' Phil Careless. Nor was the feminine variety of this popular class of enter- tainer wanting, for, in the persons of Miss Minnie Victoria, Miss Mabey Key and little Connie Slater, one had good specimens whose animated and highly amusing contributions were no less successful than those of their masculine colleagues. A well-presented comedy sketch puttied "Some Nurse," in which Miss Ambrose and Mr. J. W. Pearce figured, some meritorious musical mimicry of wind instru- ments by " Brasso," and a lively -ensemble " extra turn;" rounded off and gave repletion to a programme calculated to satisfy , the most exacting appetite. Miss Beckwith and Mr. W. A. Coupes each proved themselves capable accompanists, and clever executants also of short pianoforte pieces. At the close of the entertainment, Mr. W. Furness asked for, and obtained with hearty cheers, a vote of thanks to the artistes who had all come from Manchester at their own expense to give the entertainment which had been seen and listened to. Mr. Pearce briefly responded, with an expres- sion of the satisfaction the performers had de- rived at playing before, so appreciative an audience.</p>
        <p>How to Court. Josh Billings tenders to all whom it may con- cern the following excellent advice: "Don't court for munny. nor buty nor relashuns; those things are just abort as the kerosene ile refining bizziness—liable to grit out of ,repair dud bust at enny minnit. Court a gal for fun, for the luv yu bare her, for the virchu and bizziness there is in her; court her for a wife or for a mother; court her as you wpod court a farm for the sue and the perfection of the title; court her as though she wasn't a fule and yu another; court her in the kitchen, over the wash-tab, and at the planner; eenrt her in this way, young man, and if yu don't git a wife, the fault won't be in the courtin'."</p>
        <p>Jack and the Weak Excuse. The sailor had been showing the lady visitor over the ship. In thanking him she said: "I see that by the rules of your ship tips are forbidden." "Lor' bless yer 'eart, ma'am," replied Jack, "so were apples in the Garden of tden."</p>
        <p>CAPTURE GERMAN TRADE t Investors, Manufacturers and Merchants should consider the resources of Canada's largest Province</p>
        <p>TJ M 13E C</p>
        <p>THE MINING INDUSTRY provides the raw material of many British industries. Hardware. Enamelware and Woodw,are, Paper and Pulp offer excellent opportunities. AGRICULTURAL PRODUCTS ' include Wheat, Oats, Potatoes, and other Field Crops. Other products include Butter, Cheese, Apples, Tobacco, Live Stook, &amp;a. ' QUEBEC OFFERS UNIQUE OPPORTUNITIES FOR yrin PROFITABLE EMPLOYMENT OF BRITISH CAPITAL. Fur particulars apply : LtXol. the Hon. P. PELLETIER, Agent-General for Quoin°, 86, Singsway,London, W.C.</p>
      </div>
