      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>_ :to ; • IST I T •,. • • ~. no..a. X 41. :f 44e; r A</head>
        <p>_ :to ; • IST I T •,. • • ~. no..a. X 41. :f 44e; r A</p>
        <p>Shire Horse Society.—Mr. James Gould was elected on the Council of the Shire Horse Society at the annual meeting held in London this week. Lord Northbourne announced that the Prince of Wales had consented to become president-elect of the society for the ensuing year and president in the year 1917.</p>
        <p>Military Tribunal—The following is the list of those appointed by the Council under the Military Service Act: Messrs. R. H. Gotobed, W. G. Groves, J. H. Holt, T. J. Ridgway, S. W. Gould, F. W. Druce and W. Seymour. At the first meeting of the tribunal held on Friday of last week, Mr. T. J. Ridgway was appointed chairman. Several doses were dealt with dur- ing thoevening.</p>
        <p>Former Lymm Footballer Wounded.—Fol- lowers of "Soccer" will learn with regret that Sergt. T. Webb, of the Warrington Territorials, has been wounded in action. Further details are announced in another. colnnin of our paper. Sergt. Webb was for several seasons a player of considerable promise in the village club, and was, greatly esteemed for his gentlemanly play in this branch of sport.</p>
        <p>LiYMM</p>
        <p>trying circumstances, in fact they can't pos- sibly have the nourishment and good clothing that we British Tommies ' have been so lucky to get. Then again they can't fight with the same spirit as our people. They also do not get their pipes and cigarettes as regularly as our boys. All these comforts send the boys' fighting grit up 50 per cent. For myself I must admit that all who do their best in forwarding comforts, smokes, 44,:c., to the boys (willing to sacrifice their lives) are certainly doing their best to bring this terrible war to a close. Of course, you must know that we are up against a new enemy, and from what we can gather a very fiendish enemy at that.</p>
        <p>Served In Seven Countries.</p>
        <p>." I only hope I will have the good_ luck to get through this, when, I think, you will all agree with mo, that I shall be entitled to settle down in civilian life, at the same time feeling confi- dent that I have done my share for wife and country, being wounded twice and travelling to the extent of seven countries. I might add that our journey acros sto although interesting. At times we had wind up, but we got here safe. There are several Lymm lads in our Brigade. I come across them now and again. I have not heard from John nor Jim lately, but I trust they are both well. I know quite well that Jim's heart is quite in the right place, and that he would like to follow me around, but if he is not fit it is up to him to remain at home. He has certainly been through it, especially in that terrible charge -where he got wounded at Loos. Our lads will never forget that night. "In conclusion, I wish all Lymm lads now at the different war areas the 'best of luck and safe return. On reading your local paper I am fully convinced that Lymm has given its best; also I feel confident that very few will have to be fetched from their so-called dug-outs ' (the fireside), to do their share with their brave comrades doing theirs." Company-Quartermaster-Sergeant Hewitt is a son-in-law to Mrs. Booth, of Statham, Lymm, whose . three sons, Private James Booth, Ist Welsh Regiment; Corporal John Booth, 2nd Welsh Regiment; Private Charles Booth, Lanca- shire Fusiliers, are also serving King and country. We gave their history some time ago in OU7 columns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>NO LYMM CONSCRIPTS! Mr. Gotobed's Appeal To Maintain Splendid Record.</head>
        <p>NO LYMM CONSCRIPTS! Mr. Gotobed's Appeal To Maintain Splendid Record.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Military Service Act comes into operation on March 2nd, and I seek the hos- pitality of your columns to make a final appeal to any men in the Lymm Recruiting Area who are eligible and have not attested. The Military Service Act concerns oniy single men of military age who'on Thursday, morning next are unattested and whose occupation does not carry exemption. Those who have been medically rejected since August 14th need not offer again, but those rejected prior to that date should again present themselves. I understand there are still some in this dis- trict who are "waiting to be fetched." To these men I desire to make an earnest appeal not to mar the splendid response Lymm men have made to their country's call, nor to de- tract from the loyalty and patriotism so abundantly shown by their being compelled to do their duty. Hundreds of our Lymm men are serving, and , officers and men have won distinctions for duty nobly done, and all have gone freely. How will those who are " forced " meet the "free" men from their 'own district, and with what</p>
        <p>feelings? May there not be a single Conscript from Lymm! May the Military Service Act be a dead-letter in our. district! To any doubter I say play the man—attest at once.—Yours, &amp;c., R. HARRY GOTOBED, Chairman.Lymm Urban District Council.</p>
        <p>Council Office, Lymro, February 25th, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>AIR RAID PRECAUTIONS</head>
        <p>AIR RAID PRECAUTIONS</p>
        <p>District Council's Warning To The</p>
        <p>Inhabitants.</p>
        <p>The • Lymm Urban District Council have 'issued a notice warning the public that it has been deemed necessary to warn them that as a precautionary measure for the safety of the public, the supply of Gas may be turned off by the Council at very, short notice. In the event of danger the pressure of gas will he very con- siderably reduced, and in emergency cut off altogether. Householders are therefore warned to see that all gas taps are turned off, as the supply will not be renewed until six o'clock on the following morning. In the event of houses being left temporarily unoccupied, house- holders should turn the gas off at the meter before leaving. . The consumers are also asked to turn the gas off at the meter each night. In hotels, dwelling-houses, &amp;c., inside lights I must be so shaded or reduced, or the windows,' sky lights, and glass doors so screened by shutters, or dark blinds, or curtains, &amp;c., that no more than a dull, subdued light is visible from any direction outside. Civilians must not congregate together or remain in the streets. Military and police, instructions and any orders issued must be implicitly obeyed. Householders are requested to do their shop- ping earlier; in the day-time where possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>C.Q.M.S. J. M. nEwrrr</head>
        <p>C.Q.M.S. J. M. nEwrrr</p>
        <p>Tells In Letter Of Life In Salonika</p>
        <p>Mrs. J. M. Hewitt, of Cross Brow, Statham. Lymm, has this week received a letter from her husband, Company - Quartermaster - Sergeant Hewitt, who is at present serving in the Salonika campaign. He is an old soldier, having seen altogether 26 years' service. He holds two medals and seven bars for the late Soutfi African War, and is well known in Lymm and the surrounding districts : "At present we have not come across the enemy in our new war area, but you can take it iron' me.)hat should they ever tome 4.ur way they will have the time of their war experiences by being simply mown down. I am quite cer tam n it will be terrible for them and also for our troops. The enemy must attack ;as, which will make it ten times worse for them, especially when you know they will have to climb moun- tains to get at us, and nothing hut be, guns, machine guns, bombs and rifle shots flying at them from all angles. I think we are manaving them all right, and have quite the measure of them now.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Better Off Than The Enemy.</head>
        <p>Better Off Than The Enemy.</p>
        <p>"It certainly requires patience to stick the bitter weather we have to endure, but there is the consolation of knowing that the enemy have likewise to stick it, and no doubt under more</p>
        <p>. •ro • .4:b." ,4 •</p>
