      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Lymm Platoon. Platoon Orders.</p>
        <p>Sunday, February 27th.—Dedication of Win- dow at Oughtrington Church to the late Lieutenant Althorp Ridgway. Fall in at Drill Hall at 2.30 prompt. The Battalion Commandant and Bowdon Platoon will attend the service. It is earnestly requested that a full muster will be made. Uniform. -Thursday, March 2nd.—Hot-pot supper at Railway Hotel, Heatley, at 7.15. Tickets is. each, must be obtained before Tuesday from Mr. Garside, Mr. Moston, or the undersigned. Tickets will be sent to all who have already donated. Uniform.</p>
        <p>Sunday, March sth.—Battalion Manceuvres at Dunham Massey. Platoon will have to take up position near Dunham by 1.30 p.m. Further particulars later. G. R. WILLIAMSON, Plitoon Commander.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>THELW A Lb.</head>
        <p>THELW A Lb.</p>
        <p>The--1-tospital—The Thelwall Heys Hospital now has 23 patients, all making satisfactory pingress towards recovery. Six are expecting to be discharged to-day. They will be sent home for a short time before returning to the Front.</p>
        <p>Church Service Time Altered.—The Grappen- hall Parish Church commenced service on Sun- day night' at 5 o'clock instead of 6.30 as usual, the church being fairly well filled considering the early commencement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>FEARNHEAD.</head>
        <p>FEARNHEAD.</p>
        <p>Risley Independent M ethedists.—A week's mission, which concluded on Thursday evening, has been held in this place, conducted by Mr. IL Rimmer, of Bolton. Owing to the inclement weather the week-night services have not been so well attended, but good work has been done. Special musical items have formed a feature of the services, the soloists being Mr. J. Barton, Mr. T. Cook, Miss Bold, in addition to friends from other churches in the circuit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WOOLSTON.</head>
        <p>WOOLSTON.</p>
        <p>Parochial Tea Party.—The annual tea party in connection with the Woolston Mission was held in .the Woolston C.E. School the other evening, about 153 persons sitting down to tea. After tea a, concert wai held, presided over by the Rev. J. Hirst, of Padgabe. The opening pianoforte solo was played by Mr. F. M. Darby, and a glee entitled "Melody" (Rb.benstein) was very ably rendered by the choir. Songs were sung by BESS ' Walla= - and Messrs. Brimelow, Humphreys, R. Ryder and E. Gorton. A dialogue entitled The Black Rivals" was excellently given by Messrs. R. Ryder, E. Gor- ton, W. Platt and E. Brimelow. The children's prize distribution took place the following , evening, when the prizes were given to the successful scholars by the Rev. J. Hirst. A concert had also been arranged, a recitation being contributed by Miss Platt and a solo was also sweetly sung by Miss Gorton. The dialogue, "The Black Rivals," was also re- peated for the children's benefit. After the concert dancing was .indulged in; Mr. H. Staf- ford acting as M.C. The pianist was Mr. W. Wainwright, who very kindly gave his ser- vices. The proceeds, which were in aid of the Sunday school funds amounted to £4 10s. :Id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—On Tuesday evening the Rev. W. Reid, Presbyterian minister of Warrington, gave an address to the Wesley Guild. His sub- ject was "how God re'vealed Himself as our Saviour by the word 'Come." Another Ride to the question was the word "go," as addressed to Moses. Lads and men, members of our churches and Sunday schools, are now, hearing the word "go," and if they are mot going gladly they are willingly in response to the call of to-day. •--Mr. C. II Parker, who occupied the chair, said that the nations apparently have been asleep, and no doubt have received a shaking up. It now depends on them how they approach life 'under the new conditions.</p>
        <p>In a Flooded First-line Trench.—Mr. and Mrs. Molyneux, of 12, Warrington-road, have just received word from their eldest son, Corporal Herbert Molyneux, Rifle Brigade, that he is in the Canadian Hospital suffering from trench feet. His letter home gives a vivid description of his recent experiences in a flooded first-line trench. Returning from the rest camp on February 11th with his section, they proceeded straight into the trenches, where they stayed until the following Monday. During that time the Germans heavily bombarded their position making it imperative that, as far as possible, all sign of movement should be reduced to a mini- mum. Owing to the wretched state of the weather the trenches were half full of water, which nearly 'reached to the men's waists. One chum was killed trying to save a wounded com- rade, and to add to their further troubles and discomfort they were unable to light a fire to boil the small quantity of water they possessed. The last straw came when, in the scramble in and out of ,the trenches, the whole co their rations and equipment disappeared from sight. At present his feet are badly swellen, but !ike the usual type of, boys &gt;of-the .breed, is looking ahead.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
