      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 26, 1916. STOCKTON HEATH. RIFLE RANGES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 26, 1916. STOCKTON HEATH. RIFLE RANGES.</p>
        <p>Model Lighting Observancd.—A casual visitor to Stockton Heath cannot help but notice how the inhabitants have so loyally carried out the regulations with regard to lighting; in fact, it has been. deperibed by a prominent gentleman .as being a model village in this respect.</p>
        <p>The Good Work That Has Been Done At Appleton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>The Sewerage Scheme.—At a meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians a letter was read from Messrs. H. B. White and Sons, solicitors, of Warrington, complaining of a ditch being a nuisance on land tenanted by Mr. James Hankey, of Knutsford-road, Grappenhall.—lt was resolved that the attention of the engineer to the Grappenhall sewerage scheme be called to the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment. Orders for week commencing 27th February. Monday, 28th.—Musketry. Meet at the Range</p>
        <p>at- 7.30 p.m.' sharp. Tuesday, 20th.—Company and Squad Drill. Fall in at Headquarters at 7.45 p.m. sharp. Friday, 3rd March.—Musketry. Meet at the Range at 7.30 p.m. sharp. Saturday, 4th.—Parade and Company Drill (Uniform). Fall in at Headquarters at</p>
        <p>i p.m. sharp. Orderlies.—Section-Commander F. Short and Private J. W. Cottrell. By Order, F. A. FROST, 0.C., Platoon Commander. Headquarters, 24th Feb., 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>STRETTQN,.</head>
        <p>STRETTQN,.</p>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR SOLDIERS. Primrose League Raises £35 By Rummage Sale.</p>
        <p>The majority of the inhabitants of Stretton took goods to the rumiaage sale organised by the Primrose League in Stretton School on Saturday afternoon and, by the effort, 05 was raised to- wards furnishing material for making comforts on behalf of men engaged on military service. It was a pleasant function. The stalls were well-laden, tea was nicely arranged, and music was supplied by friends. Everyone in the village , seemed to be taking his or her part in making the event a success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Church Lads' Brigade.—A concert was given in the C.E. Schools on Thursday evening, the proceeds being in aid of the fund for carrying on the work of the Church Lads' Brigade in the district. A brilliant list of artistes had been engaged and the programme was a very attractive one. A quartet party—the Misses Blackburn and. Rigby, and Messrs. G. Walker and A. Davies—gave a very excellent rendering of "A Regular Royal Queen," from "The Gondoliers." Miss Elsie Hewitt, who is becom- ing a general favourite amongst Padgate audiences, sang with her usual ability "Dear Heart" and 'My. Hero," receiving well- deserved encores for ,both items. Miss Black- burn gave an excellent rendering of "Just a Little Bit of Heaven," and Miss Rigby was equally successful in Tosti's "Goodbye" and "Annie Laurie." Mr. George Walker 'sang "Young Tom o' Devon" and "Somewhere a Voice is Calling," and Mr. A. Davies delighted the, audience by his rendering. of "Phil of the Fluters' Ball," and " Zummerzetshirt." A very interesting item was Mr. Podmore's flute solos, which were very much appreciated. The meeting was presided over by the Rev. J. Hirst, who spoke of the good work being done by the Church Lads' Brigade, under the cap- able leadership of Captain Simpson, and he also heartily thanked the artistes for the services they had rendered in making the con- cert a success.</p>
        <p>Napoleon.—A very interesting and instructive lantern lecture on the life of Napoleon was given in the Wesley Hall on Friday of last week by the Rev. Arthur Grime, of Glasgow. The meeting was presided over by the Rev. J. Grange Bennett, who had come over specially for the occasion. He said he had only been a chairman three times in his life, and, twice was at Padgate.—The Rev. Arthur Grime said that he always remembered his last visit to Padgate, when he was a student in college, and he had many pleasant recollections of his stay in the -village. Speaking of Napoleon, he said that he always appreciated genius, and Napoleon's life was full of it. He gavep a graphic description of the French Revolution of 1789, the Reign of Terror, when thousands were put to death.. He also spoke of the early life of Napoleon, his youth being spent in Corsica, when he became a captain of artillery in the year 1792. One followed with interest the progress of the great leader, his promotion to General, and his various victorious cam- paigns, until he finally became the King of France. His meeting with Josephine and his domestic trials were also portrayed, resulting eventually in a divorce from the one he loved 60 that he might become united to a. princess of the Royal blood. A vivid description was given of the death of Nelson, the complete rout of the French, the Battle of Waterloo, and the .banishment of Napoleon to. St. Helena. —The lecture throughout was illustrated by over 100 slides, and the soloists were Mr. J. H. Draper, who sang "The Death of Nelson," and, Mr. W,. J. Bate, , who sang "Napoleon to Josephine." Mrs. Draper and Mrs. Bate were the accompanists, and Mr. Appleton and Dr. Bennett .were the lanternists. A vote of thanks to. the Lecturer was moved ,by Alderman Ben- nett, and' seconded by Mr. T. B. Duckworth. The. proceeds were in aid of the Trust Funds.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Home Missionary Work.—The annual Home Missionary meeting in connection with the Wesleyan Church, was held in the Schoolroom on 'Monday evening, presided over by Mr. T. B. Duckworth. The Rev. A. J. Morton gave a brief report of the work done by the Missionary Societies during the past• twelve months. He spoke of the good work being done among the Army and Navy at the various training centres and also in France, emphasising the revivals in the camps caused by the good influence of those who had left our churches and joined the Forces. In spite of those who had left there did not seem to be any general falling off, in attendance at the rreligious services. He also gave the financial statement of the amount collected for Mission- ary work, the Padgate contribution being .26 ss. —The Rev. A. Spate, of Liverpool, who was the deputation for the evening, gave a very inter- esting and inspiring address on ."The Humour and Pathos of Life and Work in East London." He paid a great tribute to the Rev. Peter Thompson and the Rev. John• Howard, and spoke of the earnestness and zeal with which they worked in the slums of the East End. There are no middle-class people in this part of the city, but they are the lowest of the low. In the inner heart of East London there are 325,000 people, 93,000 of whom are foreigners. The children are warped, starved, stunted and old before they are young. There is Poverty in this part of the world which is not known elsewhere, and 50 per cent. of the people are buried at the expense of the parish. He gave some very humorous and pathetic incidents as illustrations, all of which showed the good work which is being done to lift up broken and degraded humanity in the slums. He felt sure that the funds which were needed to help these poor and degraded people and lift them up to a higher level would command the sympathy of all present. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER UNITY OF 01M- FELLOWS' CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The Grand Master and Directors of the Man- chester Unity of Oddfellows have decided to hold" the annual movable conference of the Order at Bristol. It was provisionally arranged to meet at Douglas, Isle of Man, but in view of the position of national affairs it has been arranged fur tho meeting to take -Place in Bristol, which was the alternative selection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>NATIONAL' SERVICE.</head>
        <p>A NATIONAL' SERVICE.</p>
        <p>When the Appleton Rifle Range was installei something like eleven years ago, itfeas littli thought by the promoters that it would pia: such an important part in the history of the country as has been the case within the last two years. It is their proud boast that many of the gallant Warrington men who are defend- ing their hearths and homes in the fighting line received their first lessons in the art of handling a rifle at the "butts." at Appleton Range. Here it was that the majority of our local Territorials —"The Unbreakable Fourth "—scored their first bull's eye, at short ranges. The Appleton Rifle Club have every reason to plume them- selves on having made history, and the value of such an institution in the present strenuous times was the dominant note struck at the annual meeting of the club held on Tuesday at Atkinson's Cafe, over which Mr. Segar Owen (one of the vice-presidents) presided. Yet this organisation, which has done so much to equip Warrington's manhood for the firing line, is passing through lean times. In pre- vious years the club received a handsome dona- tion from the military authorities for the use of. the range for the men of the 4th, Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, but the advent of war the donation ceased, and the club have now only a surplus of a little less than a sovereign. Without the generous help and encouragement of Lieutenant-Colonel Charles Lyon (the presi- dent), who is away on active service, it would be well-nigh impossible for the club to continue. It is to be hoped that the financial strain is only temporary, and will not lead to any limita- tion of their activities. The &amp;ming season ought to result in a big influx of new members.</p>
        <p>A Le 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>TERRITORIALS TRAINING GROUND.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS TRAINING GROUND.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, in the absence of Lieutenant- Colofiel Charles Lyon, who is serving his country at the Front, said that they would agree with him that wherever Colonel Lyon and his men were, the Germans would know about it to their , cost. He was sure he would be echoing their wishes when he suggested that they should send their hearty' greetings and best wishes for a safe return to him and to the batteries under his command. Colonel Lyon was their presi- dent and patron, and in him they had one who was more than interested in any work which was carried out at the Appleton Rifle Range. He (Mr. Owen) was glad to say that he was one of the first helpers, along with Major Roome, an enthusiastic worker in the ,cause, to establish the range at .Appleton. The, early days proved a very trying period, because there seemed to be some idea that to train anyone ift rifle shoot- ing was making so many military men, or people interested in militarism, and that eventually England would become a military camp. (Laugh- ter.) It was' indeed hoped that they might in- fuse a certain amount of militarism into the English mind; and certainly no harm would have been done if the facilities offered had been accepted whole-heartedly. At ,the present time it would have been an infinite boon to the com- munity., Those who took up the work when Major Roome retired and carried it on through a most depressing period must now be ex- tremely satisfied with the result of their labours., They had been repaid by the fact that thousands of young fellows who were serving at the Front received their first training at the Appleton Quarry. He might say with certainty that every man in the gallant 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, which had now completed twelve months' service, received his first train- ing at Appleton. (Applause.) He did not know whether quite so many of the 2/4th Battalion received their training there, but at , any -rate the committee who have carried on the work during the last-ten years had accom- plished something in which England had found to be invaluable in the present crisis. When the war was over he hoped they would still go on helping men to handle rifles, not as though they were something to be afraid of, but as something that would make their enemies afraid of them. (Hear, hear.) It was rather interesting that whilst he was in London last week he met a Major of a Lincolnshire Regi- ment, who first introduced to Lord Roberts the idea "every man a rifleman." A pamphlet which the Major had written came under the notice of' Lord Roberts, who sent for him, and from that day took up the cry which for years he drilled into the people of the country. in- stead of listening to it, however, they turned it aside. To him, however, it was with consider- pble pride that he was able to inform the officer that they in Warrington, thanks to the interest of the Lyon family' and of Major Roome, had established a rifle range here. (Applause.) FINANCES: SAILING NEAR ?HE WIND.</p>
        <p>In submitting the annual financial statement, Mr. F. J. Grocott said that the credit balance of 19s. 10d. was very gratifying, inasmuch as the club did not receive in 1915 the amount of £2O from the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, and £5 from the National Service League for the use of the range, as ip former years: The receipts amounted to £B5 17a. 7d., and the expenditure £B4 17s. 9d. Mr. J. 11. Fletcher thought that the clttb was sailing a little too close to the wind in having such a small balance in hand. The question to be considered was how to augment the receipts, and he thought the only way was by obtaining new members. Was the quarry large enough for them to add new members to their. list?</p>
        <p>if they had not sufficient rifles people would not turn up for practice. Money was going to be _much tighter than at the present time, and _ _</p>
        <p>it behoved -the committee to e-xercise their dis- cretion where the expenditure was concerned, and cut down wherever possible. The Secretary said the committee were fully alive to the position, and would do everything to keep the club in good condition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>HON. SECRETARY'S REVIEW.</head>
        <p>HON. SECRETARY'S REVIEW.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. 11. Swinton, in presenting his first annual report, said the war had affected the club in many ways, chiefly in the number of the old members, who had been called away to defend their country. They all hoped that at the end of the war all their absent members would return safe and sound. He was pleased to say all of them were safe, although three had been slightly wounded. Three of their mem- bers had been mentioned in dispatches. Although on the outbreak of war there was a rush of new members, a large percentage of them did not, renew their membership in 1915 New members had, however, been coming in all through the year, and the total elected num- bered 33. At the end of 1915 they had a mem- bership of 132, of which number 33 were serving in the forces. Of the remainder, those who were not either over military age or working on munitions were a v.ery small percentage. A Record Membership.</p>
        <p>Since the close of 1915 28 new members had joined, making ,a total at the present time of 160, which was easily a record for the club. The Volunteer Training Corps Were largely respon- sible for the increase, the number of members Who 'belonged to snch 'Corps totalling 66, of whom 57 belonged to the Stockton Heath Detach- ment of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment. This detachment had had the use of the Appleton Range and equipment all through the summer on Tuesday evenings, and the members had at- tained a high proficiency with the rifle. It was 'hoped that their interest in shooting would not wane when the war was over, but that they would still keep up as a sport wha,t at present was to them a duty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Events of the Season.</head>
        <p>Events of the Season.</p>
        <p>aptain Mathams had kindly allowed shooting to take place at the Drill Hall, especially dur- ing the busy times of recruiting. The club commenced the year by losing in the first ;mind of the National Team Championsaii). T.:routc•ted by the Society of Miniature Nfle Clubs, their opponents being Padiham, one of the strongest teams in the 'North. Afterwards came the Queen Alexandra's Cup Competition, which was decided during the first stages by individual merit. Messrs. 13ennion, Christopher and Shep herd qualified for the second round, for whilch they received a certificate, but, unfortunately, they were unable to report any further pro- gress. During the summer the club took part in the competition organised by the Liverpool Minia- ture Rifle Association, and finished fifth in order of merit. The final competitions took place on Sature.ay,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
