      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>t.. 4., .). . . „4 .... , . _</head>
        <p>t.. 4., .). . . „4 .... , . _</p>
        <p>_Dat,ekach.e Kidney</p>
        <p>September 25th, when the principal feature was the shooting for the President's Cup. The pre- sent captain of the club, Mr. Black, had the honour of becoming the first holder of the cup. As a result of the increasing interest which was being taken in riffs shooting as shown by, the number of clubs in the district, the com- mittee decided, in order to encourage shooting generally, to hold a prize meeting, open to War- rington and district. Owing to the lateness of the season when the idea was first put forward, the committee were rather doubtful as to the success of the venture. The entries far ex- c,eeded the expectation of anyone, and a fine day set the seal of success. Cost of Service Ammunition.</p>
        <p>He was afraid that during the summer full use was not made of the opportunities which the members possessed of practising with the service rifle. The great drawback had been the prohibitive price of the service ammunition, which made argthing more than a few occa- sional'shots a very expensive matter. The com- petition for the Donegall Badge (held over from 1914) was, however, decided with the service rifle, and to everyone's satisfaction, the winner turned up in Company-Sergeant-Major Day, who happened to be over on leave at the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Thanks For Assistance.</head>
        <p>Thanks For Assistance.</p>
        <p>The committee were indebted to Messrs. A. Down, F. J. Grocott, A. Shepherd and Frank Warburton f* much .appreciated gifts. He, would like to thank Mr. Shepherd for the in- valuable help he had given him as sccrebrv. It was entirely owing to the efforts of Mr. Shep- herd that the greater part of the Stockton Heath Volunteers had joined the club. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The following officers were elected:--Presi- dent, Lieutenant-Colonel- C. Lyon; vice-presi dents, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Lieutenant-Colonel B. Fairolough, Messrs. Vere B. Davies,. J. J. Whitley, Segar Owen, W. War- burton, A. Down and H. Beresford hon. treasurer, Mr. F. J. Grocett; hon. secre-</p>
        <p>tary, Mr. R. M. Swinton; hon. armourer, Mr. J. Towers; hon. Auditors, Messrs. F. Gandy and Edwin Bradshaw; committee, Messrs. J. Towers, P. Harrison, J. W. Elliott, F. Tole, F. Flack, F ' C. Bennion, W. J. Fetherstone, A. Shepherd, 3 H. Fletcher, T. I. Fletcher, H. C. Westmorland and M. A. Piercy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>PRIZE-WINNERS.</head>
        <p>PRIZE-WINNERS.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the prize winners:— Pres:.Klit's ay Mr. F. Black .total .2(..fa 29h); club competitions, in order of merit,lie,•srs A. I Shepherd (also first in Drill Hall competition), R. M. Swinton. H. Warburton, F. J. Grocott, L. R. Rowson, I. Broady, J. Towers, F. Black, Peter Harrison, M. A. Piercy and D. Fleming; medals:. Mr. F. J. Grocott, "Daily Express" medal; certificates: Mr. H. Warburton, "Daily Telegraph" certificate; Messrs. F. C. Bennion, H. Christopher and A. Shepherd, Queen Alex- andra's cup certificates. A vote of thanks was passed to Lieutenant- Colonel Lyon for allowing the dub the use of the Appleton Quarry, on the proposition of Mr. Bennion and seconded by Mr. F. Warburton. Mr. Owen was warmly thanked for presiding, and Mr. R. M. Swinton for his untiring labour as secretary.</p>
        <p>The Pleasant Thief. Far out from the; village, in the middle of a lonely moor, a wayfarer was stopped by a foot- pad and relieved of his valuables.' And all the time the thief smiled and smiled. "What are you grinning at?" demanded the victim at last, angered by the smile. "Oh, nothing special." replied the thief lightly. "It's only that I'm one of those chaps who can't take things seriously."</p>
