      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>night it was missing. Four days later another key was missed. Prosecutor then kept watch over the premises, and on Sunday afternoon caught one of the boys trying to take another key from the lock. The police interviewed the boy in the presence of his mother, but she be- came so persistent in her endeavour to screen her son that the officer in charge was compelled to ask her to retire to another room. Some time ago a ton shilling note was stolen from a teacher's pocket at the school where the boys attended. The police were unable clearly to prove the culprit, but in the course of inquiries it was found that one of the accused had a " ski-cycle " which he admitted having stolen, as well as a number of packets of cigarettes. _ _ _</p>
        <p>•.“.1 • • V •r• - Mr. Murray said that one of the boys had been away from school since December 23rd, suffering from some complaint. The fact that the boy had had nothing to do all day but play was the reason he had got into trouble. The Chief Constable said the fact that fin mother of the • boy tried to screen him did not show a satisfactory state of affairs. in the home.</p>
        <p>The magistrates ordered one of the boys to receive one stroke with the birch and the other three strokes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>TREASURY NOTES.</head>
        <p>TREASURY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Girl Admits Stealing Two From Bar Till.</p>
        <p>A young girl named Dorothy Rowell, residing with her mother at 11, Keen-street, employed as a servant by Mrs. Winifred Jennings, wife of the litensee of the White. Bull, Horsemarket- street, was remanded at the Police Court on Tuesday in connection with a charge of stealing two ten shilling Treasury notes, the property of her employer. It was stated. by Mr. Nicholls that a remand of ,seYen days was necessary in order that the Police might continue inquiries as to the theft of three gold rings, which had also been taken from the hotel. The girl had admitted stealing the two Treasury notes. On Friday, February 10th, the prosecutrix placed one of the notes in a glass in a small- cupboard in the bar. Later it was missed, but no satisfactory explanation could be obtained. A second note was placed in the same position and this also disappeared. The girl was taxed, but denied any knowledge of the affair. She agreed to a search and con- cealed in her blouse was found one of the notes and a sum of 7s. 6d., which prisoner stated was the change out of the first note. • The Chief Constable added that the girl's father was Serving in the Army, and her mother had declined to appear at the Cowt, as she said she could do nothing with her. When the in- qUiries were completed he would suggest that r. Laws, Police Court missionary, should take the matter in hand to find a home for her. The Chairman (Mr. Henry Roberts) said it nitted very hard lines on a young girl to Znawee a charge without any sympathy from "er Parents. The remand was granted..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>SQUANDERED IN AMUSEMENTS. Boy's Disposal Of Money Stolen From</head>
        <p>SQUANDERED IN AMUSEMENTS. Boy's Disposal Of Money Stolen From</p>
        <p>Gas Meter.</p>
        <p>There seems to ir no diminution in the num- ber of juvenile offenders at the Police Court. Almost every week the magistrates who sit on the Bench on Wednesday (the Juvenile Court day) have the unpleasant task of ordering the -birch-rod to be administered to boys who have not attained their teens of years. On Wednesday of this week, before Mr. J. W. Rayward and other magistrates, a boy was charged with breaking into a gas slot meter and stealing 13s. 5a.- The Clief-Constable related the facts, which showed that the boy's mother reported to the Poll:CC that the gas meter at 'her house had been broken open. Detective Turner questioned the boy, and he at once admitted having broken the lock of the meter with a poker and stealing the money. The police had ascertained that the boy was in the habit of staying out late at night, and the parents seemed to have no con- over him. The money he had taken from the meter had been squandered on amusements, frequent -visits being paid to the picture shows No fault was attached to the boy's step-father, who was a hard working and steady man, and his mother also went out to work at a "cutting"</p>
        <p>Air. , Moore Murray (Director of Education) said that the 'boy 'had caused considerable trouble to the education authorities, and his parents had 'been before .the magistrates and the School Attendance Committee for the boy's non- attendance at school. • As it was 'his ' first offence, the magistrates decided not to send the boy to a reformatory, but ordered 'him to receive six strokes with the birch-rod.</p>
        <p>Sean 'Finds Some Mischief Stilt.</p>
        <p>Two other boys were charged with stealing three door-keys, the property 'of Wm. Henry Knight. 'O3. Xnutsford-road. On Sunday afternoon, the 13th inst., one of the keys was left safe in a stable door, and at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>INDIGESTION CAN BE WARDED OFF!</head>
        <p>INDIGESTION CAN BE WARDED OFF!</p>
        <p>Most of us are liable to have indigestion. Our ever-changeful climate severely taxes the digestive organs, our Modern habit of doing everything in a hurry leaves the stomach littie repose, and we can't always spare the time to eat slowly and rest awhile afterwards. Only a strong he:Othy stomach can stand the conditions of modern life, and if you wish to ward off pains after eating. a sense of fulness and weight, flatulence, and all the other miseries of indigestion, you must keep your stomach healthy and strong. 11 for any reason your stomach loses tone and efficiency, take Mother Seigel's Syrup after your meals for a while, and note the improvement in your health—the increase of your vigour. Be sure you get the .genuine Mother Seigel's Syrup. •No other remedy before the public possesses Its splendid powers of toning up and strength- ening ithe stomach, and of gently stimulating the _action of the liver and bowel..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>LAZY MAN.</head>
        <p>A LAZY MAN.</p>
        <p>Refusal To Work And Maintain His</p>
        <p>Wife.</p>
        <p>Frederick Looker, turner, 94, Catherine-street, was summoned by his wife, Mary Alice Looker, for arrears of maintenance amounting to £3 3s. In December Mrs. Looker obtained a separa- tion order with an allowance of 10s. weekly, but payments had been very irregular. The .Chief Constable said the prisoner was addicted to laziness. There was plenty of work, but the firm had the greatest difficulty in get- ting him there. Prisoner, who 'aid he was not prepared Vto make any offer to repay the arrears, was sent Ito gaol for 28 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>IN A RUT.</head>
        <p>IN A RUT.</p>
        <p>Motor Car's Lights Jerked Out In Battersby Lane.</p>
        <p>In mitigation of an offence for driving a motor car without a light, Thomas Pretty, of Orford House, Orford, alleged at the Police, Court on Thursday that our badly paved streets were responsible for the failure of his lights. He was also summoned for driving his motor car without a licence. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ On February 11th P.C. Coombes noticed the defendant driving a ,car along Marsh House- lane without a light at 11.15 p.m. On produc- ing his' licence the officer found it had expired on January 20th and had not been renewed. Defendant said that whilst he was proceed- ing along Battersby-lane he had to pass over a large rut in the road, which knocked out the light. The ruts in the roads had cost him any amount of money for globes. He omitted to renew his licence because he anticipated re- ceiving a note from the authorities on the day of, expiration. He had since taken out an- other. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable remarked that there was no obligation on the police to send out notices when licences expired. The magistrates imposed a fine of 15s. for driving without a light and the other charge would be dismissed on payment of the costs, ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>NOT FOR TRIPE.</head>
        <p>NOT FOR TRIPE.</p>
        <p>Chief Constable Cheeks A Defendant's</p>
        <p>Plea.</p>
        <p>For causing an obstruction in Butterinarket- street on Febrvia.ry 9th, by leaving for half an hour a heavy 'motor car and trailer, Thomas Ward, 212, Chorley New Road, Horwich, was fined 10s. by the Borough magistrates on Thurs- day. Defendant contended he was not away for so long a time and there: ,was plenty of room for other traffic, to pass. ' The Chief Constable pointed out .that when the police officer called the defendant's atten- tion to the obstruction he replied that he had only been for some tripe., As a matter of fact, the motor and waggon were standing outside the Lower Angel public-house, and he was sure people did not go in there to buy tripe. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>IS HE A GERMAN?</head>
        <p>IS HE A GERMAN?</p>
        <p>Another Remand Ordered For Suspee</p>
        <p>John Henry Steeler, who was before the Court -a week, ago in connection with a charge under the Aliens' Registration Act, was again remanded for seven days at the Police Court on Thursday. The Chief Constable explained that the police had not yet completed their inquiries. At present there were two charges against the prisoner, the chief one beinr that he was an alien and had failed to register. Prisoner came to Warrington and lodged at the Refreshment Rooms in Horsemarket-street The people for whom he was working were suspicious of his nationality, and from his appearance they were of the opinion that he was a German. When taxed by the police he stated he was an Ameri- ran subject, his father being a German and his mother a French woman. Prisoner had committed two other offences at Manchester, and these would be brought to the notice of the Court at the adjourned hearing, when the pollee hoped to complete the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>FIXTURE COMPLICATIONS.</head>
        <p>FIXTURE COMPLICATIONS.</p>
        <p>Local Law Case Before The Chancery Court.</p>
        <p>The case of John Buckley and Co., Ltd., v. The School Brow Colour Co. and William Scott was heard this week at Liverpool Chancery Court, before his Honour Vice-Chancellor Dud- ley 'Stewart Smith: The action arose out of the tenaney by the defendant company, of which the defendant, Scott was manager, of certain premises inkHigh- street, School Brow. The claim was for an in- junction to restrain the removal of certain land- lord's fixtures and the commission of waste, and for damages for injuries done to the premises by the removal of certain tenant's fixtures and for the removal of some landlord's fixtures alleged to have beef removed. The defendant company 'made no appearance, and an interim injunction was granted against them in November last, Scott giving an under- taking in the terms of the notice of motion. The trial of the action took place on Wednes- day and T,hursday last. Messrs. Henry Greenall and Co., solicitors, Warrington, repre- sented the plaintiffs, and instructed counsel (Mr. John Rutherford and Mr. McMaster). The defendant company were not represented, and the defendant Scott was represented by Messrs. Robert Davies and Co., solicitors, Warrington, who instructed counsel (Mr. Greaves Lord and Mr. F. J. Kerr). The Vice-Chancellor decided that Scott was not liable for conversion of the landlord's fix- tures, alleged to have been removed or con- verted, nor for damages caused by a falling chimney, or by the removal of tenant's fixtures, and as Scott had given his. undertaking not to do any of the acts in respect of which it was sought to restrain him, which undertaking he had kept, there could be no relief against him on the injunction. The plaintiffs had not made out a successful claim against Scott on any of the heads, and the result was that as between him and the plaintiffs, the action must be dis- missed with costs: Judgment went by default against the defend- ant Company.</p>
        <p>A Continuous Perfoi-mance. Mrs. Blunt: But why did you leave your last place.? New Cook: I couldn't stand the way the master and mistress quarrelled, ma'am. Mrs. Blunt: Dear me! Did they quarrel much? New Cook: Yes, mum; when it wasn't me an' him, it was me an' her.</p>
      </div>
