      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>TOWN'S SUCCESS AGAINST ECCLES BOROUGH.</p>
        <p>son; Broster, G. Whitfield and Osborne; Ditch- field, T. Whitfield, Aspinall, Foster and Owen. Eccles Borough: Foote; Lucas and R. S. Walker; Smart, A. Walker and Wilkinson; Leigh, Kennedy, Siddall, Goddard and Cain. Aspinall set the ball rolling and Owen receiv- ing from Foster, advanced towards the visitors' goal and, from .his centre, Aspinall was only inches wide with his final effort. There was every indication that the game was going to be keenly contested, and, from the goal-kick, the visitors returned the compliment in a very determined fashion. After Leah had made a good clearance, Kennedy tested Jardine, and then a free-kick for offside against Siddall gave the home defence relief. The goal-kick enabled the home forwards to pay a visit to the opposite end, where Aspinall was prominent with a couple of good shots, which Foote successfully dealt with. Subsequent attacks on each goal saw Cookson and then Lucas and Walker clear their lines by splendid tackling.</p>
        <p>The First Goal.</p>
        <p>• A brief stoppage was caused by an accident to Goddard, and following the free-kick, Osborne gained possession and, by good manoeuvring, sent in a shot from thirty yards distance, which Foote misjudged -and the ball entered the net and opened the score for the home team. Prom the centre-kick play was vet' even and keen, th? ball travelling very quickly from end to end. I:o.ti the full-backs were much in evidence. '1 h e visitors graulially forced the home tonm to heir own half, and a very hot attack.was made, the goal having a very narrow escape from being captured by a splendid shot from A. Walker. The • ball hit the. crossbar with Jardine well, beaten, and the ball, rebounding into play, was finally sent wide of the uprights.. From the• goal-kick Owen raced away along the touch-line, and Lucas played the ball dead.— From the. throw-in Owen centred grandly and. T. Whit- field scored the second goal. The leather was transferred to the home half, where Cain missed a good opening from a centre by Leigh. The home goal was again placed in danger by a free-kick against Broster, but the defence survived. Half-time : Warrington; 2 goals. Eccles Borough, nil. On resuming, an advance by the visitors was neatly checked by Osborne, and a free-kick drove the visitors back to their own half, but they quickly returned to the attack, and Siddall had very hard lines by hitting the crossbar with a hard drive, a corner-kick resulting. The visi- tors were now having the better of the ex- changes, but they failed at their final attempts at goal, and a good centre by Leigh went a- begging, Goddard being at fault by missing , another easy opening. At this juncture the visitors were striving their hardest to open their , account, but Cookson was in great form this half, and repeatedly beat the opposition. The home forwards during this time were not alto- gether idle, but they could not pass Lucas and Walker, who were kicking splendidly. The home half-backs were playing a very strong game now, and made good passes to their. forwards. Foote was called upon several times to clear, and Leigh transferred to the opposite end, his final effort dropping on the wrong side of the up- rights. After a spell of even exchanges in mid- field the home team took up the running and Ditchfield got the third goal with a splendid shot, which Foote had no chance to deal with. The visitors renewed their efforts very</p>
        <p>strongly, and a good shot by A. Walker just skimmed the bar. A little later Jardine had to kick away hurriedly, and then be made a grand save on the ground from a lirst time shot by Siddall, at close quarters. Final:— Warrington Town, 3 goals. Eccles Borough, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>To Meet Again To-day.</head>
        <p>To Meet Again To-day.</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell.</p>
        <p>The players and officials of the Warrington Town Club were in a very jubilant mood after thg match with Eccles Borough. They had every reason to be, for had not the club humbled the -pride of Eccles in a manner which surpassed even the most sanguine expectations? A clear three goals defeat requires some ex- planation from an Eccles' point of view.</p>
        <p>Without belittling the home team's success, it is only fair to say that Eccles hardly deserved such a sound beating. They had three chance?, but ill-luck, combined with ineffective shooting. led to their downfall. Within ten minutes after the interval Eccles had sufficient opportuniti to make the scores level. Twice did Ole tall strike the crossbar, with Ja dine,, who was deputing for Kite, well beaten. '</p>
        <p>If they were faulty in front of goal, the Eccles forward line displayed more method of combination than Warrington. They met a trio of solid defenders in Broster, G. Whitfield and Osborne, however, who revelled in the work of breaking up the attacks. The sterling play of the half-backs was quite a feature of the after- noon's excitement. When two goals in arrears, Eccles never gave up hope, and it was as. well for Warrington that the defence was safe and sound. The inclusion of Cookson at full-back in the.Warrington team is a rare stroke of busi- ness for the. club. He was the outstanding player on the field. In everything he undertook there was thes stamp of "class" about his methods. When assisting Tranmere he caught the eye of the crowd for his clever work, and he bids fair to become the idol of habitues of the Hood-lane enclosure.</p>
        <p>Those other players who were making their debut Jardine (goal), Broster (right-half), and Owen (left-outside), all did well. Jardine, who has played for Preston North End, Chorley and Denton, was a little nervous at the start, but soon got over this. In the second half he thrilled the spectators with several clever clear-</p>
