...;...k.,:, ... .',-. '.. git%W4;*P'"' roil ...m.,,,:. 1,4,6„.a,1aZ:.4., ,i-e.::',17.,... ', . i::.:VT,':A::::;,,i.k. " ••• :. .• - -.- ' :;'',.-ii,..... ; f....5::' ..-.. .... - . 4' '• :,:' '...A.,-,:.:" t1At.:10,...-"'''''" i'i.: ': -. :. ,4*,'".:;,.i.....':',..,:::..,'R.':::::....::-, •:. '...:,', .::,.. :,...;::-...,'.:: :g.-:•:...-i....1..':; ~ s' -....: :::',"?i,:-.-;: it..' A ....::: ~: .-..,:z.z,'-',,,,''',..'-••• '' • ;.1.:y,-,..t:iz.",,•:.•-•:.:::::::nai:t•Rinok',., ,14' .liii :ft l'::::tlitili•i:::::4*.4::',A3t4Zialiiitinfp:4ifq;;:' k:o,%•:'''''' ' ' ' ' i . .. . . . 'f4',f•iAk,,:.::',•-•'.43444*124.fif .::' ;11). OVA ;.::::' x.": . .''''.. . • 4 .„.........,,,..,..„,„„....„,,:..itt,..,4,,ii.:.t.t„..........1,3..:k.„....,,............„..,•••••• „•,,....t,,.....-....::.kw..,..,:tvizr,:.:::::.:...,:,,,,,....• ..;•,.f.:....,,..":,:::::,..,,..„.,,1,.:„....:,....,:"...,• ....:„...,,...7.....,..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>RUGBY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UN lON.</p>
        <p>A BIG SURPRISE.</p>
        <p>Swinton Beaten At Home By Broughton.</p>
        <p>A big surPrise in the Northern Union League matches on Saturday was the defeat of Swinton at home by such a low-lying club as Broughton Rangers. Swinton bad a stroner team out, yet they were outplayed, especially in the three- quarter line, and had to retire beaten by eight points to four. Oldham also went down at home to Rochdale Hornets by three points to two, while Huddersfield, Hull Kingston Rovers, Leeds and Wigan all secured easy home vic- tories. The results were as follows Salford, 7 points; Runcorn, 4 points. Dewsbury, 8; Batley, 2. Huddersfield, 14; Hull, 5. Hull Kingston R.., 6; Bramley, 0. Leeds, 22; Hunslet, 3. Wigan, 23; Halifax, 2. Broughton R., 8; Swinton. 4. Rochdale H., 3; Oldham, 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>NTERMEDIATE LEAGUE,</head>
        <p>NTERMEDIATE LEAGUE,</p>
        <p>ONLY TWO GAMES.</p>
        <p>Heathside Win Easily; St. Thomas' Have To Fight Hard.</p>
        <p>A fairly good muster of spectators assembled at Wilderspool on Saturday, when two matches were down for decision in the local Intermediate League. A good game resulted from the meet- ing .of St. Thomas' and Bolton, the former team —having the best of matters throughout, especially in the first half—ran out winners by three points to nil. The score would have been larger but for the defence of the Bolton lads. The other game—Heathside v. St. Mary's— proved to be little more than a farce, for both teams were three or four players short, and 'there was hardly an interesting moment throughout the game. It was a very one-sided affair all through:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>ST. THOMAS' V. BOLTON.</head>
        <p>ST. THOMAS' V. BOLTON.</p>
        <p>Bolton 'started, and within a ew minutes three points had been recorded against them This was due to a mistake by their full-back,</p>
        <p>who, although he had ample time to gather the ball, which had been kicked up the field by a St. Thomas' forward, failed to do so, even at the third attempt, with the result that Clarke, the St. Thomas' right wing, dashed up, and, gather- ing smartly; ran over practically unchallenged. The goal kick was a failure. • Nettled by such a , sudden and early reverse, Bolton, from the restart, dashed straight to their opponents' line, but were penalised for off-side. • They came again, however, and their left wing broke away, but was pushed into touch at the quarter line. St. Thomas' were awarded another free kick, and their full-back found touch. Fine work on the part of the Stockton Heath lads' half-backs, who were far superior to those of Bolton, resulted in St. Thomas' again breaking away, and, from a fine passing , movement, Clarke again dashed over, but was pushed into touch-in-goal before being able to ground. From. a scrimmage. in midfield, Bolton were put in possession, but the' opposing half- backs proved - to 'be quite as smart at breaking. up their opponents'. 'passing movements, as at starting them themselves. Consequently, the Bolton threequarter-backs never got far. Half- time arrived with the score three points to rain` favour of St. Thomas'. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN FOOTBALL TEAM.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN FOOTBALL TEAM.</p>
        <p>Back row (from left to right).—ii. Deakin. Midge, Tweedle, Parr; Williams, Barnes, Millington, " Shint " Daintith, A. Gleave, J. Deakin Middle row.—W. Longmore, J. Broster, G. Whitfield (captain), Jardine, Leah, Osborne, J. Booth (trainer),-P. Peacock (hon. secretary). Front row.—Cookson, T. Whitfield, Aspina</p>
        <p>Li, Foster, Owen.</p>
        <p>ances. Occasionally, he ran risks when he advanced too far out of goal to stop an invasion, but, on the whole, his play was sound. "Con- sidering that Broster has not been playing sinee last September, he played a tireless game, and was always in the thick of the fray. He is a hefty player and uses his weight to advantage. Old followers of the " Albion " are pleased to see him in the ranks of the Town Club.</p>
        <p>Owen, who figured at left-outside, did a lot of good work, his centres showing good judgment. In the match against Walkden the left-wing was a source of weakness, but with Forster as a partner the pair ought to strengthen the attack appreciably, and are worthy of an extended trial.</p>
        <p>The Town forwards ',showed a decided- provement on. previous efforts. There was de- cision and alertness in their. actions. Aspivall is in his right position at centre. He swings out the ball to the wing men at. the right time, and'i is not slow to snap Up an opportunity.. Tom Whitfield and Ditchfield *on, the right wing are beginning to fit info each Other's inethods.,Roth had the, honour of ..seoring.' a. :Ditchfield :is the sharpshooter of the team. His goal on Saturday was cue of the best yet seen on the I ground. An experienced goalkeeper like Foote was helpless with a fast oblique shot. A well- placed corner by Owen provided Whitfield with an opportunity of heading a goal; the ball being placed out of- the reach of the Eccles keeper. The most CO': us goal was the one scored iv Osborne. From twenty yards' range he essayed a shot. The ball bounced in front of Foote, who was expecting it to travel round the post, but,. to his surprise, the unevenness of the groun3 deflected its course and it leisurely entered the net.</p>
        <p>The visiting side included a Warrington player named Siddall.' He made strenuous efforts to score, hut on each -occasion was thwarted by :he home defenders. He and Cookson fought many a duel for Possession, and it generally ended in favour of the Warringtonian. e</p>
        <p>• To-day the Town visit Eccles to try con- clusions with the vanquished of last week. The team is to be selected from:—Kite, Leah, Cook- son; Broster, G. Whitfield, Bullen or Osborne; Ditchfield, T. Whitfield, Aspinall, Foster, Mill- ward ,or Owen. If. our fellows keep their heads we should win again; at any rate, it will be a capital game.</p>
        <p>THE GAME DESCRIBED.</p>
        <p>The Town hail the Borough team from Eccles as visitors to their enclosure at Hood-lane on Saturday, when fully 3,00 spectators witnessed the game. Several alterations were made in the local team, the most notable absentees being Kite and Millward, the former being one of the chief partners:in .a life engagement which was being solemnised,at thQ St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, and the latter had been sent laway by his employers on a munitions errand. Berry, an old Warrington and Everton goal- keeper, did not appear as announced, and Jar- dine made his first. appea,rance between the ,up- rights before a Warrington audience, Coekson reappearing at. full-back. Broster, Who has played with the Queen's Park Rangers, came in at right-half, and another newcomer was Owen, of Lymm and Latchford. The teams:—</p>
        <p>Warrington Town: Jardine; Leah and Cook-:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>An Early Free Kick.</head>
        <p>An Early Free Kick.</p>
        <p>Early in the amend half St. Thomas' were awarded a free kick, with the. result that touch was found at the corner flag. A scrimmage was formed, and a Bolton 'player, leaving the 'pack before the ball was out, was promptly penalised. A kick at goal was essayed, but.it failed. . From now onwards St. Thores' were pressing, and could do everything but score.. Only on one occasion did Bolton visit their opponents' quar- ters in this half, and they retired beaten by a try to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>H EATHSI DE v. ST. MARY'S.</head>
        <p>H EATHSI DE v. ST. MARY'S.</p>
        <p>As stated above, the Heathside v. St. Mary's match w.as a very. one-sided affair, , and tha Heathside thyee-quarter backs did almost as they chose. Consequently, when the final whistle sounded they had. put up thirty-seven points, while St. Mary's had only been able to reply , with five.</p>
        <p>RESULTS UP TO AND INCLUDING FEBRUARY 19th. Points r. W. L. D. F. A. P. ileathside Parochial Rylands's St. Thomas' Bolton Beamont St. MaryYs St. James'</p>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS. St. Thomas', 3 points; Bolton, nil. Iloathside, 37; St. Mary's 5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECS. v. PAROCHIAL.</p>
        <p>At Gorsey-lane to-day. Rylands's: T. Archer; Thomas, Littlewood, Britton and llill; Savoury and Swanton; Dum- bell (captain), Barker, Sutton, Mulvey, Butler and Latimer. Reserves.-31olyneux and Sloane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND !STRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND !STRICT</p>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District Junior League St. Elphin's received a shock in their return engagement with 'Orford Juniors. on the Arpley ground. The game was keenly and strenuously contested throughout, each side scoring once in each half. In the closing stages both teams made extra efforts to gain the lead, and both goals had lucky escapes. McDougall, Gibson, Hirst and Clare did well for the Juniors,. and Gleare, Miller,- Humphries and Shelmerdine were the pick for the Saints. The game resulted :inha. draw, each side scoring twice.</p>
        <p>The Scouts paid their first visit to the Recreation Ground at Penketh and, having only nine players, the-home captain allowed substi- tutes to be selected from the spectators. It was only in the first portion of the game, when the visitors were playing down the slope, that they really caused the home defence any trouble. After changing ends the Scouts were chiefly on the defensive, and at the close of play they were defeated by six clear goals.</p>
        <p>Because the Scouts had only nine of their own players they are claiming that this should be a friendly game, but that will be a matter for consideration at the next League meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>PENKETH JUNIORS v. STOCKTON HEATH SCOUTS.</head>
        <p>PENKETH JUNIORS v. STOCKTON HEATH SCOUTS.</p>
        <p>The above teams met for the first time last week on the Recreation Ground. The visitors on their arrival had only nine players, and sub- stitutes were allowed. The home club was fully represented. There was a good attendance of spectators when the visitors' captain, on win- ning the toss, decided to play down the slope. Glover set the ball rolling and after even ex- changes Finch led an attack on the visitors' goal, but the efforts proved futile. k few minutes later another attack by the home team was well checked by the visitors' defence. Then- ' Win.stanley was more successful with a long shot' at the end of ten minutes' play. From the centre-kick the visitors showed great improve- ment; they tried hard to get on an equal footing with their opponents, and the home defence was severely tested, but they survived the attack, Routledge, the home custodian, saving a couple of good shots from Smith. A corner-kick to the visitors was not improved upon. From the goal- kick the home forwards, by good combination, transferred to the visitors' end, Thomasson passing to Glover and the latter quickly trans- ferred to Pearson, for the latter to score the second goal. Half-time :—Penketh Juniors, 2 goals; Stockton Heath, nil. The home team now had the advantage of the slope, and the visitors' defence was soon in diffi- cnities, and their citadel was captured by Pear- son. The visitors appeared downhearted after these reverses, and were confined tá their own half. Finch, receiving from Watson, added their fourth goal: A good effort by the visitors , was smartly repulsed by Thomasson; and Moss,</p>
        <p>receiving the ball, raced along the line and passed to Glover, for the latter to add the goal. The visitors made a final rally, but their final efforts lacked sting. Pinch scored* again before the close of play. Result:--Penketh Juniors,. 6 goals; Stockton Heath Scouts, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>ORFORD JUNIORS v. ST. ELPHIN'S.</head>
        <p>ORFORD JUNIORS v. ST. ELPHIN'S.</p>
        <p>On the Arpley grounds. Orford: McDougall; Gibson and Doughty; Austin, Gleave and.Povey; Griffiths, Morgan, Hirst, Clare and Belshaw. St. Elphin's: Lockhart; Gleave and Buckley; Bothwell, Miller and Burns; Boyle, Shakeshaft Rigby, Shelmerdine and Humphries.</p>
        <p>Referee, Fitzgerald. Orford won the toss, and Rigby set the ball rolling. The visitors, making the first advance, were neatly repulsed by Buckley. Then the " Saints " transferred to the opposite end, and during a keen attack, McDougall saved 'finely: Midfield play followed for fl; Ow minutes, when the " Saints " again- made attack on the visitors' stronghold. Shelmerdille centring finely, Burns transferred to Boyle, for the latter to open the score after twenty minutee•iday. Nettled by this reverse, the visitors played LOare strenuously, but Lockhart cleared his line. Following some even exchanges in midfield, the visitors returned to the attack and gained a corner-kick'. This was well placed by Griffiths, and Hirst headed through, making the scores equal. Half-time r—Orford Juniors, 1 goal; St. Elphin's, 1 goal. The, " Saints" set a fast pace on resuming, _. and were soon attacking the visitors' goal. Gibson making a timely, elea.rance. Then the Juniors returned the compliment, but the " Saints' " defence proved equal to the occasion. From the goal-kick, Rigby gained possession and raced into the visitors' half, and passed to Shelmerdine. The latter, when ,in a good ' position; steered the ball safely past McDougall and gained the lead for the "Saints," The game was now contested in ding-don. fashien, and the home defence played well under hard pressure. This was followed by a keen attack on the visitors' goal, when McDougall made a grand save by falling full length. and Gibson kicked away. From the goal-kick- the, visitors again attacked strongly and Bothwell handled inside the penalty area. Hirst was entrusted with the kick and beat Lockhart with a low drive, making the scores equal again. Both teams now played very keenly, and the home defence had to exert themselves to clear. their Resnit Orford Juniors, 2 goals; St. Elphill'R, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
