      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <p>; •</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 26, 1916.</p>
        <p>CURRENT TOPICS'</p>
        <p>FROM , '4</p>
        <p>'STAGBLAND-: ."</p>
        <p>By "FOYER."</p>
        <p>Royal Court. • One of the old-stle dramas\has been playing :his week at Sir Joseph Beeeham's house, entaie,, `A Motherless Mite," and has been favourably received. . .</p>
        <p>. . Pay 'your sixpence, shed a .few tears, threaten dire results to the villain of the piece, and laugh at the clean and wholesome humour, and there you have your evening's amusement; at least that seems to be. the order this week at the Royal Court Theatre.</p>
        <p>Jolly,, rollicking pantoniime next week •by "Dick Whittington and his cut," which, by the way, is published as "not a revue."</p>
        <p>Pantomithes of to-day have little of the real story which once delighted us when we were youngsters,.and I long again for the time of th? funny elOwn with his sausages and the fat policennin. Childish. if .you will, but really good fun and remembrances of the good old days. -</p>
        <p>Miss Edna Latonne is portraying the title part and-as it is her last week prior to her de- parture for Australia. and South, Africa, she should giVe-of. her best</p>
        <p>. Mr. Walter Bishop, a comen!an of consider- able repute, is playing "Idle ,Jaek,!' and the principal girl is, Miss Bessie Burke... The latter artiste was principal girl at the spacious •Lon- don Opera House last year. Her singing. I hear, is something - different from the usual type associated with touring pantomimes.</p>
        <p>I raise my hat to the proprietors of the Rylancls-street house for their past generosity on behalf of 'charity, and ,note with pleasure that a matinee concert is to be held there next Thursday for the British Red Cross Society.</p>
        <p>It is organised for the purpose of providing hospital necessities for our sick and wounded. They are badly needed and .urgently, and I trust will call for your patronage. Artistes from the Palace and Hippodrome,. members of the Liver- pool Repertory Co., and well-known local enter- tainers are due to appear. Now journey to the Box Office.. . Hippodrome. • - - - -</p>
        <p>• If hearty laughter is better than doctor's medicine, then Nellie. Wallace, princess of comediennes, is a splendid pick-me-up, for at our variety house this week 'her "tonic" has been extremely palatable. Like her poster, she makes the house rock with mirth and merri- ment.</p>
        <p>Nellie tells me slie`was born in Glasgow, but that her favourite audience's are those of Lan- cashire and Yorkshire. She has toured America on . three occasions and met with a brilliant reception in the land of dollars, playing before crowded houses, especially ,in New ,York and Philadelphia.</p>
        <p>She -relates a_ little story of the, latter city: "I had lust finished My show at one of the big halls there, when the manager. asked me .if I mould appear at- a millionaire's supper party at the principal hotel 41 the. city. After the performance one of the bhoys' came round, and, with Yankee-like bluntness, exclaimed: 'Gee, madam, you ain't good lookin', but you're d funny. Guess I shan't want my 'doe.' for another month.' "</p>
        <p>In more than one city she has been termed the second George Formby, but the talented comedienne was playing in music-halls before the .Lancashire lad attained the zenith of his fame. Whilst in London .a short time ago she played 26 shows in one week, which speaks eloquently of her popularity in the Metropolis.</p>
        <p>Ganfy, "the little lump of fan," is also in- cluded in this week's attractions, This come- dian, however, has been suffering- from a very bad cold, which has, in consequence, hindered him from giving of his best.</p>
        <p>The little man has just finished playing at the London Hippodrome in "Hullo, Tango!" He scored a great joke off me on Tuesday, even- ing; as follows—" I've been top of the bill to the biggest music-hall turn ever seen on the stage," said he "Oh !" replied I. "Whop Marie Lloyd, Harry Lauder, or George Robey?" "No, my friend," said he; "Lockhart's Elephants."</p>
        <p>Ganty, I may say, had his home wrecked in a recent air raid on London.</p>
        <p>There are several artistes included in the bill of the Hippodrome next week which are of More than ordinary interest, for instance, Gilbert Heron. -awl Jessie Danvers ,in their topical sketch "The Slacker," and Dexter, the man who draws-with both hands at the same time.</p>
        <p>I propose 'interviewing .the above artistes, to- gether.with the famous Grace's within the next few days. The latter bail from sunr6: Spain and some inteitesting facts should be the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>Star Kinema.</head>
        <p>Star Kinema.</p>
        <p>As a lover of the silent drama, I visited the "Star " the other evening to enjoy "The Four Feathers," adapted from the novel ,by Mr. A. E. W. Mason, and I came away thor- oughly pleased with the film version. • :</p>
        <p>'I was taken through a bewildering maze of Eastern country, portrayed with a vividness and reality rarely to be found in films of 'to- day. The desert, the palm trees, and somnolent activities of the Arabs, reminded one forcibly of "Kismet."</p>
        <p>Marie Doro,, the Famous Players' cinema star, has during the latter part of this week again been endearing herself to all hearts in "The White Pearl,'! a film of great beauty. We saw this charming lady in "The Morals of Marcus," and in the present production she is equally good.</p>
        <p>On Monday, Ttiesday and Wednesday of next week the principal attraction is "After the, Ball," an adaptation of the old-time song of the same name. I am told it •is finely staged and liroduced by it star company.</p>
        <p>"Queen of the Movies," to wit, Miss Mary Pickford, is to be filmed during the three fol- lowing days in "The Girl of Yesterday," a picture which created a furore in the States.</p>
        <p>Mary is a bic, favourite with English cinema- goers, and rightly so, for the bewitching little lady has scored some great successes on the screen: She is probably th highest paid artiste at present appearing before the public, her salary being three times that of our Prime Minister.</p>
        <p>As showing her popularity all over the world she was, a short time ago, presented by the people of Australia with an immense loving CU p and* a big album of autographs.</p>
        <p>Mary is not contrary, hut gives of her hest in cinema work. "The.: Girl of Yesterday" is produced by the Famous Players and controlled by the J. D. Walker's World's Films, Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>Olympia, Liverpool.</head>
        <p>Olympia, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>You must not miss " Joyland " at the Liverpool Olympia, for this production is the greatest and best seen out of London and one which has already taken the Lancashire sea- port by storm.</p>
        <p>A veritable feast of colour, pleasing and en- chanting, with musical numbers haunting and delightful, Albert de Courville and 'Wal Pink's super-revue is assured of record receipts at this spacious house. "Foyer " went on Monday evening and was amazed at the bewildering blaze of splendour.</p>
        <p>One of the best numbers is the tulip scene, the multitudinous colours of typical Holland blending to such a degree that is pleasing to both eye and mind, and breathes the atmos- ;there of that land across the North 4ea. Miss Nan Stuart;, . undergtudi to Mi Shirley Kellogg scores tremendqufly in "Where the tulip Grows."</p>
        <p>Tr' ".Toyland "</p>
        <p>i:',':,i':,..1:KT,t::.;;:...•:',-.</p>
        <p>ilV!i......</p>
        <p>\</p>
      </div>
