      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>LAY PREACHING NOT INDISPENSABLE.</head>
        <p>LAY PREACHING NOT INDISPENSABLE.</p>
        <p>Twenty-four claims for:, ,exemption from military service or relegation to a later group were heard by the Warrington.Tribunal at the Town Hall yesterday afternoon. ' • Several interesting-: cases. came before the .Court,- which was open- to the public; •foi the first time.</p>
        <p>T.Tie --Tribunal consisted ,ofr'the Mayor - (Alder- man Peter Peacock), Dr. Joseph, Alderman Tinnion, Mr: F. 'A.-Frost, Sri. J. J. Whitley, Mr.. W. Bradbury, -and •Captain McLeod (the .military representative). -- .A, young man -claimed exemption on the ground of his close connection with Kent-street Mission. He said he conducted the larger' por- tion of the services and it-.was absolutely im- possible for him to - get anyone to take his. place. -He presented a 'petition signed by the different members of the chapel.</p>
        <p>His -employer, who is also his .uncle, Mr. F. W. Wright, a builder, of Latchford, 'appealed . for him- on the ground that he waS his fore- man joiner. Already his staff had been de- pleted by twelve men haling, joined the Army and now he only had three. left. If, the appli- cant had to go up he was afraid he would have to close his business down. Moreover, he added that he was engaged On making parts of huts for soldiers being erected in France. : Captain McLeod to the employer) : Will it be possible to train' a Man to take his place?. The Eniployer : I conk!. ;not do it. under five years. He understands everything.. , The Mayor: I think the war will be over by then. .(Laughter.) . The Mayor (to the applicant): You contend you would be serving your country better by staying on at the' Mission than by going into the Army? The Applicant: I believe that my work is absolutely necessary for the welfare of the nation at the present time, and I believe it is dbing A great share for the benefit of the nation. The Mayor:- What -do you do?—I take the preaching services and the general work of the Mission.</p>
        <p>How many others. assist in‘ that work?—My father. We have a large Sunday School, and. I have a large Bible Class, and a leader substi- tute could not be found., _</p>
        <p>The case was put hack for three months ,on the employ's application. The personal ap- plication—that relating to the Mission—Was refused.</p>
        <p>A well-k.newn local ASsociation • football player, who has assisted Everton and other teams, in addition to a -short :season- with the Warrington Rugby Club, was next called on. He said he was the sole supporter of his sister and was now engaged on . important and intri- cate work. In the evening he. carried on busi- ness as a dentist. He was at present suffering from fistula and had had an operation. He was quite. surprised when a doctor passed him at the attesting office. His wound had to be bgthed every day. Tn reply ,to a member of the Tribunal, the applicant said he had not played football for three years. . ITe added that he could not walk for three-quarters of an hour without going dizzy. . _ _</p>
        <p>Captain McLeod: He could have a further medical examination.</p>
        <p>The .applicant remarked 3 that Dr. Joseph would understand, the seriousness of his case and that gentleman remarked that with care he should be well in a month. •</p>
        <p>The Applicant : Dr. Anderson said it *ould take three months. )1.</p>
        <p>The application ,was refused, whereupon the applicant remarked: "I don't think it is a fair decision:"</p>
        <p>Captain McLeod: You will be called up in due course and can go befoye. a 31edioa1,Board of five doctors. .: • • HIS FINGERS' HIS' FORTUNE.</p>
        <p>The most interesting ,ea.A3 ilfiring the after- noon was that of a professional violinist, Who wished to preserve his finms.• Ye explained that his business was absolutely run by him- self and he had been: building up his connec- tion during the ,last ten years. He had many contracts made. and could; -not •arrange for deputies either at concerts or in teaching. He was a member of the Halle, Orchestra and played at the Manchester Promenade Concerts. He had a private: teaching ,connection in War- rington, Blackpool, Earlest9wn and Poulton. He was a music master. at Blackpool. In his prOfession, .he added, his hands and fingers had to be carefully preserved and would' be injured by heavy Work. -Unless his hands were kept in their present condition he was ruined in his profession. - _</p>
        <p>The Mayor: I suppose you have given all your life to getting up this eminection,, and if you went into the Army you could not possibly get it back; and the greatest danger would be that your hands would be spoiled? \The Applicant: Yes. Ido not mind giving up my business, but if you could put me in some department in the Army whereby my finrs would not he-erected I should: be grateful. The. Clerk, (Mr. A. T. Rallaway): You are prepared to go if you can be assured of being placed somewhere .where you. would not ruin your hands ?T—Yes. The Mayor announced that the case would he adjourned for a month and, in the meantime, inquiries would be made to see if the applicant's wishes could be met with. If something could not. be found the Tribunal would reconsider his case.</p>
        <p>XL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>BLIND IN ONE EYE !</head>
        <p>BLIND IN ONE EYE !</p>
        <p>A joiner in the employ of Mr. T. W. Wright; who stated he was blind in one eye, claimed exemption.. Wlien he was attested the doctor did not 'test his eyesight. The Tribunal did not consider the reasons,, but informed appli- cant that he would be summoned for medical examination, when probably he would not have to come there again.</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>AN ONLY SON.</head>
        <p>AN ONLY SON.</p>
        <p>A clerk employed at Messrs. Crosfield's ap- pealed on the ground that he was the only son and the sole supporter of his mother. He stated that he gave his mother .22 per week. He had three sisters married, and one single, living at home, earning about 10s. per week. If lie went into the Army he would be allowed 16s. weekly by the grm:</p>
        <p>The application as refused. The Applicant: Can I appeal to another Court?</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Yes; you can appeal within three days.</p>
        <p>A GROCER'S MANAGER.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Nay, of 70, Marsh House-lane, appealed on behalf of the manager of his grocer's shop. He. explained that he (Mr. Kay) was unable to attend to his business regularly himself, owing to ill-health, having been under a doctor for nearly eight years. The Mayor: How long Would it take for you to replace him?—l have advertised, but have had no reply. Besides, my manager is the only one who knows my pries. The Tribunal allowed Mr. Kay three months in which to replace the 'applicant.</p>
        <p>• •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>ANOTHER GROCER</head>
        <p>ANOTHER GROCER</p>
        <p>Arthur Clowes, grocer and provision dealer, Of '34, Iforsemarket-street, appealed on behalf of his chief assistant. He Mentioned that twO. .employes, had already gone and another was going into the Army. If he could not ,be ex= empted, a, postponement might be made until fresh labour could be found. .• The employer's appeal was disallowed, and also the personal one by the man concerned; his being heard in camera.. SERVING WARRINGTON. - In regard to an employe of Ackers, Whitley &amp; CO., • the firth's representative de- clared that the man was serving his country by keeping thei people of Warrington supplied with. coal. lie filled the bags and took *eights and was indispensable. The. Mayor : • Have you advertised for an- other many—No.</p>
        <p>What wages do you give him?-335. per week</p>
        <p>The Mayor (ironically): If you put the wages in the paper you will get applications.</p>
        <p>Claim disallowed.</p>
        <p>WATCHMAKER TICKED -OFF.</p>
        <p>A watchmaker' who worked with his brother also had his Claim disallowed, although it was pleaded that .he was necessary for the carrying On of -the business, as men in the trade were. very difficult to obtain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>BURDENED LIKE A MARRIED MAN.</head>
        <p>BURDENED LIKE A MARRIED MAN.</p>
        <p>„When a painter was called on lie said he was the sole supporter of his mother and brother, who: was 31 years old. He declared that he had had this • burden for several years and, wanted to be put' back in the groups. • He did not see. why men who were only married in September "should get out of it” and he Should not, although, of course, he did not want to avoid military service. He wanted' an extension partiCularly so that he could earn Some 'Money, as the busy .time in painting and decorating was now coming on. He would like to save 'some money for his mother. Application' -refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>CONSUMPTIVE BROTHER.</head>
        <p>A CONSUMPTIVE BROTHER.</p>
        <p>Another asked for exemption on the grounds that he partially supported his mother and his brother had been treated for consumption, having to pay periodical visits to the seaside for the sake of .his health,. Application refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>ALLOWED TO TAKE STOCK.</head>
        <p>ALLOWED TO TAKE STOCK.</p>
        <p>The son in a firm of provision dealers advanced the plea that he was a partner and the business was difficult to carry on. Already they were very short-handed. On his, claim for exemption being refused, he was allowed two months in order that he might help with the stocktaking.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALLER'S ENGAGEMENT IN ARMY.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Town Football Club player, who is -a -joiner at Messrs.- Crosfield's, Said he was the sole- supporter of his mother, who Was a widow..., Application refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>MOTHER IN ILL-HEALTH.</head>
        <p>MOTHER IN ILL-HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Another claimed exemption, remarking that he gave his widowed mother 245. per week. She had, a private income of 11s. per week also. He appealed for exemption mainly on the ground of his mother's ill-health.</p>
        <p>• On the Tribunal refusing to grant his appli- cation he said he would appeal further.</p>
        <p>HERB BEER BREWER.</p>
        <p>A botanical brewer and refreshment room proprietor said that if he went into the Army it would mean ;losing down. His case was adjourned until May 30th. .WATCHING SOLDIER-BROTHER'S INTERESTS. '</p>
        <p>The proprietor of a cabinet-making business and his foreman. appealed. The former said he had a brother in the Army and also managed a dairy shop for him while he was away. If he (the applicant) had to go into the Army it would mean closing down. The proprietor was provisionally exempted, the case a the foreman being adjourned for two months. 'AFFECTIONATE SON'S CONCERN.</p>
        <p>A basket-maker, who claimed to be the only wage-earner for an invalid father and a young sister, said he did not want a married man to go in his place. He was only fighting for the best terms for those whom he would leave behind. 'Unless the military authorities paid something. more than ss. it was a case of the Workhouse for them.</p>
        <p>On being assured by the Mayor that those whom he left behind would be helped from a financial point of view; the applicant said t was a great relief for him to know that.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: -Wait until you receive a notice from the. military authorities calling you up. They might forget all about it.. • Applicant: I bet they will !. They are the wrong lads to forget it. (Laughter.) Those whose claims have been refused will receive a notice from the military authorities almost immediately. and will then have to present themselves for service a fortnight from the date of the intimation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for the week commencing Feb. 28th : Monday, 7.30 p.m., Lecture by Sergeant Mainwaring in the Hall Cafe.</p>
        <p>Thursday, 7.45 p.m., l'arade as usual.</p>
        <p>The following appointments have been made: Platoon-Sergeant Stirrup to be Sergeant-major, Section-Commander T. I. Fletcher to be Platoon-Sergeant, Sub-Section-Commander E. Bradshaw to be Section-Commander, Private 11. 11. Evans to be Sub-Section-Commander.</p>
        <p>Parade today (Saturday), 3 p.m., Victoria square.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Death of a Forester.</head>
        <p>Death of a Forester.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, Bro. M. Beswick, 28, Tanners- lane,.of " Jaaziel " Lodge, Ancient Order of Foresters, died at tin advanced age. Foresters, for whose Order Bro. Beswick did much work in his earlier years, are asked to assemble at 28, Tanners-lane, at 1;15 on Sunday to accompany the funeral procession to the Cemetery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>War Curios for Guardians and Children.</head>
        <p>War Curios for Guardians and Children.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Brooks had a few score of war curios of several of the combatant ntitions conveyed to a committee room table at the Workhouse on Wednesday for his fellow Guardians to look at and give their approval to his taking triem to the I'adgate Cottage Homes for them to inspect and ask innumerable questions about. In get- ting such an admirable and comprehensive col- lection together Mr. Brooks had many friends to thank.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>LATE MR. GEORGE GLEAVE.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. GEORGE GLEAVE.</p>
        <p>to make pats and baskets. Interviewed after- wards as :to. his impressions of His Majesty, the old man said: "lie is a very nice gentleman a.nd very fine. • The Queen also is charming and a perfect lady; God .bless her." As showing the active life he led, it was only twelve months ago that he discontinued cycling, a hobby of which he was passionately fond. Sailing also claimed his attention, and he was a member of the Royal Boating Club, Fiddler's Ferry, up to the time of his death.' Years ago he Was associated with the Warring- ton Sailing Club, and had taken part in many memorable races. He was a life-long teetotaler and. non-smoker, but laughingly admitted some time ago that on one solitary, occasion he did venture to take a Whiff of his father's pipe, with consequences so dire that be never repeated the experiment: he was member of the Order of Foresters for upwards of 40 years. Mr. Gleave as a youth was educated at the Bank Quay School, which stood on part of the site now occupied by Messrs. Robinson's glass- house. lie took a great interest in Messrs. Crosfield's fire brigade, and about 38 years rgo organised their first fire brigade, he being placed in charge of it. Mr. Gleave was married on December 19th, 1861. at St. Paul's Church, Warrington, to Ann Williamson. who survives him. Altogether there are 37 descendants-.---five sons. 25 grand- children and seven great-grandchildren.</p>
        <p>... - The aged couple eefebrate-d their golden wed- ding in 1911:</p>
        <p>THE FUNEPAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Thursday afternoon at the Warrington Cemetery, in, the presence of a large gathering, the clergyman administering the last rites being the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton. The chief mourners were :—Messrs. Peter, Thomas, William, Joseph and John Gleave (sons), Messrs. William John Ellis, James, George, John and Alfred Gleave (grandsons), Mr. Peter Hill, (brother-in-law),"; Messrs. Arthur and Ernest Gleave ; 'Mr. .Thomas Basketter, Messrs. Samuel Hankinsan, James, Job and Joseph Pemberton,.Mr. 3. .Williamson and Mr. Antrobus• (nephews), Sir Arthur Cros- field, Bart, Mr. W. Clegg (representing Messrs. Crosfield's •Sick Club), Mr. James Lawton,. (the now Oldest employe of 'Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd.), Mr., Willett, Mr. W. Johnson and Mr. G. Johnson (old 'employes), Mr. Hall and Messrs, IL MeQuetn- and S. Williams, and the Rev. B. C. E. Carleton. Beautiful floral tributes, which lirally covered the grave, were received from the following :—His sorrowing Wife, "'Tom. and Bessie," "Harry and Martha," "Joe • and Maggie" ".John • and Emily," "Willie " and Sarah," "Peter and Annie," "Tom and Maggie," "Lily and George," " Sister-in-law and Nieces," sister and brother-in-law (Mr. and Mrs. P. Hill), "Thomas, Margaret and family,— " Sissie. Herbert and family," John Ellis :Ind Dolly Gleave, "Job, Joe and Mary," Arthur Gleave and family, The Mayor (Alderman P Peacock), Sir Arthur and Lady Crosfield, Mr. J. J. Crosfield, Mrs. John Crosfield, Chairman and Directors of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., Officials of Messrs. Crosfield's Sick Club, Mr. and "Mrs. A. Whittaker, Mr. and Mrs. Campbell, Mr. and Mrs. F. Jackson, Mr. and Mts. S. Williams. "From his neighbours," Mr. and Mrs. T. Welsby, and "The Boy's of the Royal Mating Club."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>SIR ARTHUR CROSFIELD'S TRIBUTE.</head>
        <p>SIR ARTHUR CROSFIELD'S TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>Knowing that Sir Arthur Crosfield had a very high opinion of the late Mi. Gleave we asked, him, at the conclusion of the funeral which he attended on Thursday, for a few words of ap- preciation, Sir Arthur at once, and with some warmth of feeling, complied; " George Gleave?</p>
        <p>" Yes! I had a last look at my old friend this afternoon. The old • natural, native dig- nity which never failed to strike at once any friend of .Gleave's was there—and even more than the old calm with which one was so long familiar."</p>
        <p>"lie had a wonderful reccrd at the works?" . , "Yes; indeed he was only abut eight years old when he went there three or four years aftor my father joined the firm. That was before the great Exhibition of 1851, before the Crimean War, America's great tragedy of the North and South War; and how many other great events! A Retentive Memory.</p>
        <p>"To refer to what more intimately concerns the old firm; Glea:ve entered its service two or three years before those far-off days when Mr. Gladstone took the duty off soap, thereby giving a great spurt to the industry. . "It is only a few months since my old friend came and had tea with us in Bold-street, and a long, long .yarn over bygone 'days. at Bank Quay. He talked of my Uncle George, of my. father, and of my Uncle Morland, and many an interesting personal reminiscence was fresh in the old man's 'memory. He was cheery and hearty, anvl looked so well that thought, and hoped, we should have him with us for many and many a long year to come. One is thank- ful he had so peaceful an..end to. so..lonfi,and so honoured a career.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-02-26_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>Dignity of Toil.</head>
        <p>The Dignity of Toil.</p>
        <p>' "No man .can aver wish to leavefit finer- record of honest and honourable work behind him. I</p>
        <p>l'avr•• Ownr4 loOked Georg+, C41,..17(A fig #l,,</p>
        <p>„S ,</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>manager at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron C 0... and resided at Lilac Villas, 13ewsey-road, Warrington,</p>
        <p>. . He retired twelve months last June and went (to reside at The Heath, Heswall, where he was deriving much pleasure from his gardening ' and other outdoor hobbies, but during the recent severe' weather he contracted a chill, . which resulted in his death, at the age of 65 years, after, a short illness. His widow is Mrs. Simpson, who, when in Warrington, did a con- siderable amount of public work, particularly in connection with ,Liberalism; :the Board of , Guardians' and the Band of Hope. ,_ •</p>
        <p>His remains were laid. to rest: in .the Heswall Parish Churchyard, the chief mourners being: Mrs. Simpson (widow), Fred and Alex. (sons), Alice and Eliza (daughters), Messrs. C. H. Larson and .J. Smith (sons-in-law), ,Mrs. :Annie Simpson- • (daughter-in-law), . Alex. Smith • and Charles Simpson (grandsons); Mrs.' and Miss Gwinnell (sister and niece), Mrs, Larson (sister- in-law), Lieutenant John Larson, (nephew); Messrs. George, Fred and Tom Hud- son, ,Mrs. Fred Hudson, Mrs. Tom Hudson, Misg. Hesba 'Shaw (niece),. Mr. Charles Whii-ehead' Mrs.., James Atkinson, Florence Atkinson and Mr. and Mrs. Wilkinson. Up to a few years ago. Mr. Simpson was an active member of the Warrington Field Cau4, he being an enthusiastic naturalist, botanist; and frequently went cycle tours through, various- parts of the United Kingdom in order to satisfy his general love of Nature. He also held the bronze medal for St. John ambulance work. • •</p>
        <p>A few years back he Was organist and also leader of a large adult Bible, class at Buckley*. street Methodist Free Church; and was highly' esteemed by all who knew him for his urbanity: and uprightness of character. . Less than, two years ago he suffered,' a sad. bereavement by the death of his eldest son, George. His second soifis a foreman at Meisrs Pearson and Knowles, and his third, and. youngest 'son, is in the Lancashire Fortress. Royal Engineers. ' '</p>
        <p>Printed and published by John Walker and Co. (WaTrington), Limited, at, the .Exottims germ* Printing Worts,. Ber-street, War- Xiirso ipublislislll4.4 •fliem at the EXANNER Offices, 2, Victoria-road, Widnes; 32, George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-street, Earlestown, all in the County of Lancaster; and at Church-street, Runcorn, in the County Cl.nator.</p>
        <p>MIT</p>
        <p>4444tia.</p>
        <p>' ..•• , , , • • v • :•••• ' , . • , •-• • ' •</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. SIMPSON.</p>
      </div>
