        <p>TO KEEP THEIR NURSES.. Runcorn Board Of Guardians Advance Wages. A nurse at the Dutton Workhouse having, asked for a testimonial in order that she might apply for another post, the Chairman and mein- bers of the House Committee interviewed her and learned that the wages paid to the whole of. their nurses were below those all over the, country. Therefore, at a meeting of the Board of Guardians on Tuesday, it was decided to in-- crease the remuneration of the nurses generally by £5 per year. Mr. J. Spruce remarked that the Board could not do better than try to retain-the nurses they had got. (Hear, hear.) Extra Shilling for Boarders-out. The management of the Pantasaph Orphan- age and the Children's Cottage. Homes having' pointed out that there was an increase in the cost of keeping children who had been sent from that area, it was decided to send is. extra per week. Poorer Wells. The contractor for coke requested that he should henceforth be paid 2s. extra per ton, and it was resolved to pay him Is. During a discussion on the Infirmary water supply, the Chairman (Mr. C. B. Linaker, jun.) remarked that the wells in the distirict were getting poorer and poorer. Wells, which, fifteen. or twenty years ago, gave a good supply were very poor now. • The Local Government Board forwarded copies of an Order for combining certain -Unions in the County of Cheshire for the purpose of the care and treatment of epileptio and feeble- minded persons together with a copy of a letter they had addressed to the honorary secretary of the Cheshire Joint Poor Law Provisional' Committee. COTTAGE PIGS. By-Law Which Should Help Production Of Food. Consideration of the increased food prices. nrompted Mr. Lister Kaye at a meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday, to• move that steps be taken to amend the bylaws so as to allow of pig-cotes within thirty feet of the cottage or any other dwelling to be used again for pig-keeping, provided the Inspearst gives his approval. Mr. J. W. Hayward seconded, and the motion was carried unanimously, the Chairman (Mr. T. Antwis) remarking that it was a particularly good motion. Farriers Wanted. Messrs. Jas. Lyn and CA., of Liverpool, wrote asking the Council to use their influence with farriers in the district and bring home to them the urgency of undertaking the making of horseshoes for the Army horses. The Chairman remarked that he did not see that the letter applied to this district, as most of the men had enlisted. Doctors, Too. The Local Government Board wrote empha- sising the need for every medical man of suit- able age (45 years and under) who could be spared from civit employment to place himself at the disposal of the authoirities, and be pre- pared to take.a eptomission eitheo it* the Arm" or Navy.</p>
