        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN F.C. • Daintith Incident: Mr. Bradshaw's Reply To Mr. Peacock. To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sir,—The letter in your issue of Saturday last from Mr. Peter Peacock, jun., is a deliberate attempt to mislead the public as to the true facts. • - , In the •first place Daintith was induced to play for the Town Club by sharp practice on behalf of the officials of that club, as, when he -was , approached by them he asked Mr. Peacock if he would secure the consent of the Warrington Committee, and that official informed -him that he had seen the secretary and that it was all right. That statement is untrue, as I had not seen or spoken to Mr. Peacock on the matter. Mr. Peacock admits that we granted a permit to Daintith to play for Runcorn, but adds." at what inconvenience to that player." If it was inconvenient for Daintith to. play for Runcorn, why did he ask my committee for the permit? Having played for Runcorn under that per- mit, the rules of the Football Association do not allow him to play for an Association team dur- ing the present season, and if Mr. Peacock would make himself acquainted with the rules ot: the Association before making rash statements', he would be acting in the best interests of his club. The Football Association Memorandum reads: "A professional player of one authority shall not Wallowed to play under the other as an amateur or as a professional during the same season." This, I think, is quite dear to any intelligent official, and a report to the Football Association for a breach of rules would, I suggest, be far from pleasant for the Town Club. The last paragraph of his letter states that "Daintith is very much hurt at the attitude taken up by the Rugby Club." Another in- stance of gross misrepresentation. It is quite true that the player is very much hurt, and rightly 89, for he feels that he has been placed in a false position by Mr. Peadock. The Town Club are welcome to take credit for any success obtained under present circum- stances. We have made our sacrifice in the interests of the nation. The opportune time to shout of success will be when operations are resumed under normal conditions.—Yours, T. P. I3RADSHAW, Hon. Sec. Warrington F.C. DAINTITH SAYS HE MISUNDERSTOOD. Mr. Peter Peacock, hon. secretary of the Town F.C., has sent us the 'following copy of letter received from Daintith :- 27, Ripley-street, Warrington, 24/2/16. Dear Peter,—l regret having caused any fric- tion between yourself an dthe Rugby Committee. I misunderstood you when you said you had seen the Combination Secretary and everything was all right. I' thought you referred to Mr. Bradshaw, but I have written to Mr. Bradshaw for a permit to play for the town, but have had no reply yet. Yours truly, J. D.AINTITH. What To Avoid In Constipation. This is as important to observe as what to eat. Baker's bread we/ have barred out. It is very often the cause of trouble; also milk and milk puddings in the case of grown-up people. Rice- ill-cooked should not be, eaten, but there is a cooked form of it, called " puffed " rice, which is good and handy for use.. Arrowroot, cornflour and other starchy foods should be avoided. These are all foods that leaver:o re.sidue as, they are presented to xis in the form of Twhito bread, Or the refinements of the cook and confection- ery, it is not easy to avoid. Where the bread is bad or doubtful, some wholesome brown biscuits free from sugar and salt, help much. The British tea-table presents us again with over- refined flour plus jam and other things far from wholesome, and all of which should be avoided. More than three meals a day is injurious. Use flesh once a day only, and then little. Avoid quack medicines as the pest. Where dyspepsia is present, avoiding liquids at meals will do more good than all the quacks. The right and natural way is to eat all. meals dry—no coffee, tea, or cocoa at the meal. •It is easy to take all the iquid necessary for health apart from meals.</p>
