        <p>.; GEORGE 0.i5,544 ;. Death d .Veteran Soap Worker.'. i? THREE-QUARTERS OF A CENTURY AND A GOOD LIFE. An old and good man has passed away by the 'death of Mr. George Dodd Gleave, of 34, Powys- street,. Warrington, and the oldest employe af Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. George Gleave was-a man whom one cannot over praise for his useful work - in religious, friendly society,- and sport circles, for his person- ality and character, together with his geniality and good nature endeared him to all who came in contact with him. For over three score years he was associated with our famous soap works, during which time he saw many changes in the firm's growth and extension. He was born on 'October 18th, 1841, and although not leading an uneventftil life, one can say that the greatest pleasure he ever, derived was upon the occasion of their Majesties' visit to•Warring,ton on July .76, 1913, when our King and Queen had a short conversation with this grand old man. One may be pardoned for again recording the brief chat that he had with their Majesties—a chat he ofttimes used to delight in recording, especially to old and new associates. .Mr. J. J. Crosfield introduced Mr. Gleave to the King, who at once cordially shook hands with him. Mt. Gleave was, of course, delighted with this recognition, and in a self-possessed, manner re- lated to His Majesty the leading features of his life story. The King inquired as to his age, and on Mr. Gleave replying "Seventy-two," he coni- mented : "I am glad to see You looking. so ,re- markably well." ".And I feel well, too," said .Mr. Gleave. He then went on to relate to His Majesty how his father before him worked for the firm from its foundation in 1815, and having , lost his sight in the course of his employment was sent to a 'Liverpool institution to learn how</p>
